 dual degree provision will be  applicable for online and distance learning  \nprogrammes too, and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(Dr. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a): The University Grants Commission (UGC) has informed that they do not have database of \nstudents pursing higher studies in foreign countries.  \n \n(b): The UGC has initiated several measures for internationalization of higher education system \nin India. In order to foster academic collaboration between Indian Higher Educational Institutions \n(HEIs) and fo reign HEIs, “University Grants Commission (Academic Collaboration between \nIndian and Foreign Higher Educational Institutions to offer Twinning, Joint Degree and Dual \nDegree Programmes) Regulations, 2022” have been notified on 2nd May, 2022.  \n \n(c): Under the provisions of the aforementioned Regulations, “Dual Degree Programme” shall be \na programme jointly designed and offered by the Indian and Foreign Higher Educational \nInstitutions in the same disciplines/subject areas and in the same level. These Regulatio ns are \navailable at https://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/4555806_UGC-Acad-Collab-Regulations.pdf . \n (d) & (e):  The UGC has informed that under the provisions of the aforementioned UGC \nRegulations, the Credits to be earned by the Indian students from the foreign institution and \ncredits earned by the foreign students from Indian institutions shall be obtained throug h \nconventional mode programmes.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|STARRED   |69", "t": "Management and Control of Flood", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *69 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nManagement and Control of Flood \n†*69  SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI  MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of project proposals pending or san ctioned by the Government for management and contro l of \nflood situation in parts of Bihar, Northern Odisha,  West Bengal, Madhya Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir and \nJharkhand due to Bagmati, Budhi Gandak, Lal Bakiya, Kosi, Tawi and Subarnarekha rivers; \n(b) whether the Government has conducted any study to control and manage flood situation in the said r ivers; \nand \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the recom mendations made in this regard and the reaction of the \nGovernment thereto? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI   \n \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*69 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 21.07.2022 REGAR DING “MANAGEMENT AND \nCONTROL OF FLOOD”.  \n(a)  Flood management, including erosion control, falls within the purview of the States. Flood management \nand anti-erosion schemes are formulated and impleme nted by concerned State Governments as per their \npriority. The Union Government supplements the effo rts of the States by providing technical guidance a nd \nalso promotional financial assistance for management of floods in critical areas. \n \nFlood protection and flood management measures are broadly classified as under- \n \n(i) Structural Measures - The structural measures f or flood control which bring relief to the flood pr one \nareas by reducing flood flows and thereby reducing the flood levels. \n \n(ii) Non-Structural Measures- Facilitating timely e vacuation of the people and shifting of their movab le \nproperty to safer grounds by having advance warning  of incoming flood through setting up a flood \nforecasting system. Discouraging creation of valuab le assets/settlement of the people in the areas sub ject to \nfrequent flooding i.e. enforcing flood plain zoning regulation. \n \nIntegrated flood approach aims at adopting judiciou s mix of structural and non-structural measures to \nprovide a reasonable degree of protection against flood damages at economic cost.. \n \nTo strengthen the structural measures of flood mana gement, Government of India had implemented during \nXI & XII Plan Flood Management Programme (FMP) for providing Central Assistance to States for works \nrelated to river management, flood control, anti-erosion, drainage development, anti-sea erosion, etc. which \nsubsequently continued as a component of \"Flood Man agement and Border Areas Programme\" (FMBAP) \nfor the period from 2017-18 to 2020-21 and has been  further extended up to September 2022. So far \nCentral Assistance amounting to Rs. 6,686.79 crore has been released to Union Territories/State \nGovernment under this programme since inception. Th e 415 projects completed projects under this \nprogramme have given protection to an area of aroun d 4.99 mha and protected a population of about 52.2 1 \nmillion.  The details of projects completed in the States of Bihar, Odisha, West Bengal, Jammu & Kashm ir \nand Jharkhand under FMBAP (no project has been implemented in the State of Madhya Pradesh) are- \n  \nSl. No State Projects in \nnumbers \nArea benefitted \n(Lakh Hectares) \nPopulation benefitted \n \n(In Lakh) \n1 \nBihar 42 28.67 223.46 \n2. \nJ&K 19 2.32 15.32 \n3. \nJharkhand 3 0.18 1.97 \n4. \nOdisha 66 1.94 11.54 \n5. \nWest Bengal 16 0.94 23.57 \n  \n\nFor non structural measures, Central Water Commissi on (CWC) is the nodal organisation entrusted with \nthe task of flood forecasting and early flood warnings in the country. Presently, CWC issues flood forecasts \nfor 331 forecasting stations (199 river level forec ast stations & 132 dam/ barrage inflow forecast sta tions). \nThese stations cover 20 major river basins in 23 St ates and 2 Union Territories. In order to provide m ore \nlead time to the local authorities to plan evacuati on of people & take other remedial measures, CWC ha s \nrecently developed basin wise flood forecasting mod el for 5 days' advance flood forecast advisory at \nidentified flood forecasting and inflow forecasting stations.  \n \n(b) & (c)  In order to formulate the strategy for flood manag ement works in the entire country and river \nmanagement activities and works in the border areas ,  a Committee was constituted by NITI Aayog under \nthe chairmanship of Vice Chairman, NITI Aayog with officials from various Departments/ Ministries of \nGovernment of India, experts from the field and Pri ncipal Secretaries from States of Jammu & Kashmir, \nUttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, Punjab, Assam, A runachal Pradesh, Tripura, Madhya Pradesh and \nKerala were included as the members of this Committ ee. The Final Report of the"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|STARRED   |7", "t": "Preservation of Intangible Cultural Heritage of Andhra Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.7 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \nSTUDENTS TRAINED IN ITIs \n \n7.  COL. RAJYAVRDHAN RATHORE \nWill the Minister of Skill Development and Entrepre neurship be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of students being trained in ITIs ( Industrial Training Institute) and \nGovernment and Private polytechnics for the last th ree years, state-wise including \nRajasthan district-wise; \n(b) the status of setting up Indian Institutes of S kills across the country and the \nproposed timeline for them becoming operational; \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal of sett ing up a national level ITI to \nbridge the gap between supply and demand of skilled  workforce and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal for inc luding Institutions of National \nImportance under Ministry of Home Affairs as for co ntributing towards skilling, reskilling, \nupskilling of the workforce? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir. The details of students trained in I ndustrial Training Institutes and \nGovernment and Private Polytechnics for last three years, state-wise including Rajasthan \ndistrict-wiseareat Annexure I&Annexure IIrespectively. \n(b)  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Skill Development and En trepreneurship has taken initiative to \nset up Indian Institute of Skills (IIS) in three lo cations viz. Ahmedabad, Kanpur and \nMumbai. The vision behind setting up IIS is to buil d world class skill training centres by \nlearning from and imbibing best practices from inte rnationally renowned existing skill \ninstitutions.The status of three Indian Institute of Skills is as follows: \nSI No Name of IIS Status \n1.  IIS Ahmedabad For setting up of IIS at Ahmedabad in  PPP model, TATA \nEducation and Development Trust (TEDT) is selected as \nprivate operating partner through RFP process. \n2.  IIS Kanpur Construction work for ISS building is in progress a nd the \ndiscussions for operations including agency are under way. \n3.  IIS Mumbai For setting up of IIS at Mumbai, Tata Ed ucation and \nDevelopment Trust (TEDT) was selected as private \noperating partner through RFP process.  Short-term courses \nhave started for IIS Mumbai at Pune (in partnership  with \nFesto). \n \n\n \n(c) Setting up of Industrial Training Institutes (I TIs) is a concurrent subject, wherein the \nCentral Government is entrusted with the responsibi lity \nof formulation of policy, laying down training stan dards, norms, whereas opening of \nITIs, implementation of norms and day to day admini stration rests with the respective \nState Governments. Whenever such proposals are rece ived for setting up of ITIs, these \nare examined and decided as per the affiliation sta ndards and norms by Directorate \nGeneral of Training (DGT). \n(d) As on date no such proposal has been received. \n\n+ \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sa bha Unstarred Question No. 07 to \nbe answered on 18.07.2022. The number of students t rained in Industrial Training \nInstitutes (ITIs)for last three years, state-wise are as given below: \nSl. no.  State  Enrolment 2019  Enrolment 2020  Enrolment 2021  \n1. Andaman and Nicobar Islands 441 619 531 \n2. Andhra Pradesh 56264 49142 45600 \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 564 577 497 \n4. Assam 2965 3210 3500 \n5. Bihar 112124 104809 109884 \n6. Chandigarh 968 831 910 \n7. Chhattisgarh 21831 19228 22319 \n8. \nDadra and Nagar Haveli \nDaman and Diu 319 318 443 \n9. Delhi 9757 8672 8773 \n10. Goa 1727 1742 2080 \n11. Gujarat 69531 85391 81220 \n12. Haryana 52772 54209 48701 \n13. Himachal Pradesh 22203 17864 20332 \n14. Jammu and Kashmir 4396 7297 8024 \n15. Jharkhand 33459 25606 29196 \n16. Karnataka 73714 54243 65697 \n17. Kerala 35165 31578 35493 \n18. Ladakh 151 118 171 \n19. Lakshadweep 246 435 374 \n20. Madhya Pradesh 73232 71061 63009 \n21. Maharashtra 110734 102679 112979 \n22. Manipur 80 82 108 \n23. Meghalaya 512 514 508 \n24. Mizoram 274 242 254 \n25. Nagaland 228 222 186 \n26. Odisha 49043 49694 57373 \n27. Puducherry 789 687 689 \n28. Punjab 39623 47588 39946 \n29. Rajasthan 136139 101487 94776 \n30. Sikkim 207 260 181 \n31. Tamil Nadu 35024 27314 28401 \n32. Telangana 32775 29466 27177 \n33. Tripura 1497 2018 1603 \n34. Uttar Pradesh 335642 279799 272778 \n35. Uttarakhand 11052 8342 8906 \n36. West Bengal 32506 30692 29207 \nTotal 1357954 1218036 1221826 \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sa bha Unstarred Question No. 07 to \nbe answered on 18.07.2022. The number of students t rained in Industrial Training \nInstitutes (ITIs)for last three years for the State  of Rajasthan district-wise are as \ngiven below: \n \n \n \n \nSl. \nno. District \nNos. of \nGovt. ITIs \nNos. of \nPvt. ITIs \nTotal No. \nof ITIs \nEnrolment \n2021 \nEnrolment \n2020 \nEnrolment \n2019 \n1 Ajmer 50 7 57 3412 3127 3779 \n2 Alwar 9 112 121 7089 9129 10888 \n3 Banswara 2 6 8 691 835 822 \n4 Baran  5 29