the Central Universities of the country; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof;  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR ) \n \n(a) to  (e) Setting up of a Central University is a continuous  process. There is already a \nCentral University in the State of Rajasthan namely the Central University of Rajasthan. \nHowever, at present there is no proposal under consideration in the Ministry for setting up of \na new Central University in Rajasthan.  \n \nIn pursuance of the National Education Policy, 2020, it has been decided to conduct \ncommon entrance test for admission  in central universities from the academic year 2022 -23 \nto reduce the burden on students, universities and the entire education system.  The Common \nUniversity Entrance Test ( CUET) for undergraduate programme is to  assess the students of \ndifferent boards at the same level, giving them equal opportunity . It is convenient for \nstudents as they can  apply to more than one universities as per their choice with  one \napplication form. They can appear in the test in 13 languages over hundreds of examination \ncentres. The syllabus for undergraduate level is based on the understanding of subject at \nclass XII level, irrespective of affiliations to any board.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |216", "t": "Recovery of Tourist Destinations from Natural Disasters", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 216 \nANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nRECOVERY OF TOURIST DESTINATIONS FROM NATURAL DISASTERS \n \n \n216. SHRIMATI SARMISTHA SETHI: \n        \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is considering to devise an action  plan to \nmake tourist destinations disaster resilient so as to minimize \ndamage to tourist infrastructure in view of the floods and o ther \nnatural disasters; and \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                  (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (b):  The  Ministry of Tourism in its’ revamped Swadesh \nDarshan scheme as Swadesh Darshan 2.0 (SD2.0) has given emphasis to    \ndevelop  sustainable  and  responsible  destinations,  following  a  tourist \n& destination  centric  approach.  The Scheme envisages to encourage \nadoption of principles of sustainable tourism including environmental \nsustainability, socio-cultural sustainability and economic sustainability.  \n \n Under Standar d Operating procedure (SOP) of PRASHAD \n(Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual Heritage Augmentation Drive) \nScheme of the Ministry of Tourism, during evaluation of project \nproposals, States/ Union Territories  concerned are advised to take care \nof structural requirements as per the Seismic Zones in which the project \nfalls .  \n \n Further, National Disaster Management Authority (NDMA) has \ninformed that it has issued the Guidelines on Cold Wave, wherein a \nmatrix clearly defines the roles and responsibilities on t ourism to be \nundertaken by concerned Central Govt. agency, State Govt. and district \nadministration. \n*********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |217", "t": "Archaeological Excavations in Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 217 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nARCHAEOLOGICAL EXCAVATIONS IN TAMIL NADU \n217. SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \nSHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S.:  \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) has done any new archaeological \nexplorations and excavations in Ancient Historical and Dravidian sites in South India \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether archaeological remnants belongi ng to Sangam period in ancient Tamil Nadu \nhave been recovered from Tamil Nadu in various ancient sites like Poompuhar and \nKorkai;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and   \n(d) whether ASI proposes willing to associate with the State Department of Archaeology, \nTamil Nadu in the marine exploration works and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir. Archaeological Survey of India is conducting archaeological excavations at \nVadakkupattu and Adichanallur.  \n(b) \n& \n(c) \nYes, Sir. Archaeological remnants belonging to Sangam period have been found at \nnumber of sites. \n \nThe details are at Annexure- I.  \n(d) At present, there is no such proposal under consideration. \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure –I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) & (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 217 FOR 18.07.2022. \n \nDETAILS OF SITES FROM WHERE ARCHAEOLOGICAL REMANANTS BELONGING \nTO SANGAM PERIOD HAVE BEEN RECOVERED FROM ANCIENT TAMIL NADU. \n \n \nS.No. Name of site Distirct Nature of remnants \n1.  Kaveripattinam Myladuthurai A massive brick structures of \nBuddhist stupa, the remains of \nBuddha Pata, sculpture. \n \n2.  Manigiramam  Myladuthurai A brick Warf near the sea \nshore  and associate  of \nceramics. \n \n3.  Thondi Ramanathapuram The trial excavation was \nconducted at a mound near \nThondi Amman Temple. \nPotteries and bricks belonging \nto late period like coarse red \nware were collected. \n \n4.  Alagankulam Ramanathapuram The most significant findings \nof the excavation are hundreds \nof potsherds of the \nMediterranean region. They \ninclude Rouletted ware and \nAmphorae jar pieces. Pieces \nof Red ware with Tamil \nBrahmi letters have been \nfound. They are assignable to \nthe first centu ry BCE. Other \nantiquities include beads, \nperforated tiles, and bricks in \nvarious levels. Three Roman \ncoins were unearthed. They \ncontain the figure of the head \nof the Roman emperor on one \nside and the figure of goddess \nof victory, holding a globe on \nthe oth er side. The legend on \nthem shows that the Roman \nEmperor Valentine II who \nruled around 375 CE issued \nthe coins. \n \n5.  Kayalpattinam Thoothukudi  A few ceramics of sangam \nperiod such as Black -and-red \nware, sherds on black slipped, \nplain red ware. \n\n \n6.  Korkai Thoothukudi Black-and-red ware, sherds on \nblack slipped, plain red, and \nred slipped wares, terracotta \ntiles also found. The bricks \nalso available in this site \n(45cmx28cm x7.5cms). \nYielded number of Graffiti \nand inscribed potsherds \nbearing Tamil Brahmi scripts.  \nThe Antiquities from this site \nconsists various object copper \nand iron, torso in terracotta, \nbone earlobes and arrow -\nheads. \nThe pearl -oysters found in \nvarious level of the trenches. \n \n7.  Adichanallur Thoothukudi Black-and-red ware and red \nware with associate pots such \nas bowl, lid, ring stand, plate, \nshallow bowl, etc., Copper \nobject such as lamps and \nrings, bangles, Copper Coins,  \niron implements such as \nArrow head, Antimony rod. \n \n8.  Keeladi Sivaganga Coins, Brick Structure,  Semi -\nprecious beads, Galss Beads, \nTamil Brahmi pot sherds, \nGraffiticmarcks,  ware with \nassociate pots such as bowl, \nlid, ring stand, plate, shallow \nbowl, etc., \nThe  lamps and rings, bangles, \nCopper  Coins,  iron \nimplements such as Arrow \nhead, Antimony rod. \n \n9.  Porunthal Dindigul Glass, Semi -precious beads, \nCoins, Brick Structure, Beads, \nTamil Brahmi pot sherds, \nGraffiti marks,  ware with \nassociate pots such as bowl, \nlid, ring stand, plate, shallow \nbowl, etc., \nThe  lamps and rings, bangles, \nCopper  Coins,  iron \nimplements such as Arrow \nhead, Sword. \n \n  \n\n10.  Kodumanal Erode Glass, Semi -precious beads,  \nBrick Structure, Beads, Tamil \nBrahmi pot sherds, Graffiti \nmarks,  ware with associate \npots such as bowl, lid, ring \nstand, plate, shallow bowl, \netc., \nThe Bone Tools, lamps and \nrings, bangles, Copper  Coins,  \niron implements such as \nArrow head, Sword. \n \n11.  Uraiyur Trichy Beads, Brick Structure, Tamil \n