�\n�������\n���������\n������\n����������\n������� �\n�������\n����������\n���������\n������������\n�������\n����������\n������� �\n���� � ��� � �� � ��� � �� � ��� �\n���� � ��� � �� � ��� � �� � �� �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ��������� ���������� ���� ��������� �������������� ��� ����� ������ ���� �����\n������������ �������� ������������������������� ������ ����������������������������� �\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |302", "t": "Platform for Export of Fish", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 302 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.08.2022 \n \nWELFARE SCHEMES FOR BEEDI WORKERS \n \n*302. SHRI M. SELVARAJ: \n  \n Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)the details of the current welfare schemes available for the beedi \nworkers; \n(b)whether the Government has received any proposal to carry out an \nimpact study of these schemes available for the beedi workers and \nwhether these are reaching them on ground; \n(c)if so, the number of beedi workers benefited by the schemes for the \nlast three years, State-wise; \n(d)whether the Government has any new proposal for creating \nalternative employment opportunities for the beedi workers; and \n(e)whether the Government has any plan to announce special schemes \nfor the women beedi workers in order to ensure protection of their \nhealth from the tobacco threat and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to  (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**                                                  ****** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO  PART (a) TO (e)  OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *302 FOR 08.08.2022 RAISED BY \nSHRI M. SELVARAJ  REGARDING WELFARE SCHEMES FOR BEEDI \nWORKERS. \n(a) to (e): Ministry of Labour and Employment runs a Labour Welfare \nScheme for the Beedi workers and their family members. The scheme \nhas three components namely Health, Scholarship & Housing. Details \nare as under:-  \n \n(i) Health:- Health care facility is provided with a network of 285 \nDispensaries and 10 Hospitals located across the Country. \nThere is also a provision for reimbursement of expenditure for \nspecialized treatment under the Government recognized \nhospitals in case of critical diseas es i.e. Cancer, Tuberculosis, \nHeart Diseases, Kidney Transplantation, Hernia, Appendectomy, \nUlcer, Gynecological diseases and Prostrate diseases to the \nbeneficiaries. The scheme covers both men and women beedi \nworkers. \n \n(ii) Scholarship:- There is provision for Financial Assistance for \neducation to the wards of Beedi workers from class -I to \ncollege/University ranging  from Rs.1000/ - to Rs. 25,000/- per \nstudent per annum, depending upon class/course. \n \n(iii) Housing:- Subsidy of Rs.1,50,000/ - (per beneficiary) in three \n(03) installments at 25:60:15 ratio i.e. Rs.37,500, Rs.90,000 and \nRs.22,500 respectively for construction of own pucca houses, \nunder Revised Integrated Housing Scheme (RIHS). However, \nRIHS has now been co nverged with Pradhan Mantri Awash \nYojana. The sunset of RIHS is 31.03.2023. \n \nThe Labour Welfare Scheme is implemented across the \ncountry through Labour Welfare Organizations situated in 17 Regions. \nThe number of Beedi workers benefited by the schemes, regi on wise \nfor the last three years is attached at Annexure . No separate data is \nmaintained for wards of beedi workers under scholarship scheme. \n \nMinistry of Labour &  Employment in collaboration with \nMinistry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship have been \nproviding Skill Development training to the Beedi Workers and their \ndependents to engage them in alternative jobs.  \n  \n\n Annexure  \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY T O PART ( a) TO (e) OF  LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *302 FOR 08.08.2022 RAISED BY SHRI M. SELVARAJ REGARDING WELFARE \nSCHEMES FOR BEEDI WORKERS. \n \nSl.No. Region Year Number of Beedi Workers Benefited under \nthe Labour Welfare Scheme \nHealth Housing \n1 ALLAHABAD 2019-20 141568 43 \n2020-21 139989 407 \n2021-22 154090 328 \n2 AHMADABAD 2019-20 51832 447 \n2020-21 35821 1017 \n2021-22 58898 322 \n3 AJMER 2019-20 100338 256 \n2020-21 73004 27 \n2021-22 99950 967 \n4 BANGALORE 2019-20 294715 69 \n2020-21 180508 3 \n2021-22 209546 10 \n5 BHUBANESWAR 2019-20 407120 382 \n2020-21 217332 2343 \n2021-22 263494 898 \n6 HYDERABAD 2019-20 184414 91 \n2020-21 159809 255 \n2021-22 109450 120 \n7 JABALPUR 2019-20 148951 384 \n2020-21 116068 3017 \n2021-22 157514 497 \n8 NAGPUR 2019-20 153614 892 \n2020-21 66501 348 \n2021-22 94128 250 \n9 RANCHI 2019-20 56221 321 \n2020-21 46776 1474 \n2021-22 67107 833 \n10 PATNA 2019-20 27021 82 \n2020-21 37080 1126 \n2021-22 53960 303 \n11 RAIPUR 2019-20 42564 70 \n2020-21 25324 85 \n2021-22 30393 19 \n12 THIRUVANANTHAPURAM 2019-20 35282 237 \n2020-21 14877 160 \n2021-22 19458 103 \n13 KOLKATA 2019-20 248932 0 \n2020-21 170943 75 \n2021-22 195460 0 \n14 GUWAHATI 2019-20 5006 323 \n2020-21 4691 181 \n2021-22 8289 146 \n15 CHENNAI 2019-20 367732 150 \n2020-21 242572 315 \n2021-22 248819 28 \n16 CHANDIGARH No registered Beedi Workers in Chandigarh region. \n17 DEHARADUN No registered Beedi Workers in Dehradun region. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |303", "t": "Khelo India Youth Games", "head": " \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *303  \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.08.2022  \n \nPM-YUVA  \n \n*303.     SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY: \n \n            Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government is implementing Pradhan Mantri Yuva Udyamita Vikas \nAbhiyan (PM-YUVA) all over the country to provide t raining and develop entrepreneurship \nskill among the youth;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the programmes being conducted under this scheme particularly in Andhra Pradesh? \n \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)  \n \n (a) to (c)        A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *303 TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.08.2 022 ASKED BY SHRI \nY.S. AVINASH REDDY REGARDING ‘PM-YUVA’  \n \n(a) & (b) The Ministry of Skill Development and Ent repreneurship in association with \nWadhwani Operating Foundation (WOF) launched the Pr adhan Mantri Yuva Udyamita \nVikas Abhiyan (PM-YUVA) Yojana on 9\nth November, 2016 with the objective to create an \nenabling ecosystem for entrepreneurship development  through entrepreneurship \neducation and training across the country.This was implemented in Institutes of Higher \nLearning (Colleges, Universities, Polytechnics). A total of 239 Institutes of Higher Learning \nwere empaneled across 26 States/ UTs. However, the scheme was restructured in June \n2018 to include skill training institutes under MSDE, viz. Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs), \nJan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS), Pradhan Mantri Kaushal  Kendra (PMKK), Polytechnics. \nThe number of beneficiaries reached through PM-YUVA  1.0 (state-wise) is given in \nAnnexure-I. \n \nBased on the learnings from PM YUVA Phase-I, NIESBU D rolled out Phase-II of \nthe PM-YUVA on its own from 2019. Ministry of Skill  Development and Entrepreneurship \n(MSDE) implemented project on Entrepreneurship Deve lopment (PM YUVA Phase-II) \nthrough NIESBUD, at an estimated cost of Rs.12 Cr u pto March, 2022, in around 300 \ninstitutes under the skill ecosystem. The aim of th e Pilot Project was to promote \nentrepreneurship as an alternate career choice and provide mentoring support to potential \nand early-stage entrepreneurs by imparting entrepre neurship education and mentoring \nsupport to trainees/ beneficiaries from skill ecosy stem. The project was implemented \nthrough organizations having experience in entrepre neurship development, \nentrepreneurship training as well as mentoring and handholding in 10 States and 2 Union \nTerritories (viz. Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Bihar , West Bengal, Assam, Meghalaya, \nMaharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Kerala, Delhi a nd Puducherry). The scheme \nenvisaged creation of 600 new and 1000 scaleup ente rprises, against which 1045 new \nenterprises were set-up and 968 existing enterprise s were scaled-up. Further, the details \nof activities undertaken under the Pilot Project is given in Annexure-II . \n  \nIn consultation with National Council for Vocationa l Education and Training \n(NCVET), Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepre neurship (MSDE) has now \nembedded Employability skills programme (which incl udes Entrepreneurship education) in \nall its long term and short term programmes, with a pproximately 10% of course duration. \nApproximately 25 lakh students of ITIs and all othe r students of short term courses benefit \nfrom this programme. Under National Education Polic y (NEP) 2020, Employability \nSkills/Entrepreneurial skill courses are being intr oduced by National Council of \nEducational Research and Training (NCERT) in School s and by All India Council for \nTechnical Education (AICTE) and University Grants C ommission (UGC) in higher \neducation courses. \n \n\n(c)  Activities conducted under PM YUVA (Phase-I) i n the State of Andhra Pradesh is \ngiven under: - \n \nS. No. \n Name of the Activity  Number of Beneficiaries \nreached out  \n1 No. of Institutes Empanelled 4 \n2 No. of Faculties Trained 4 \n3 No. of E- leaders Trained 6 \n4 No. of Students enrolled in Entrepreneurship \nDevelopment Programme (EDP) \n295 \n5 No. of Students  Attempted Final Assessment 124 \n6 No. of Students passed Final Assessment 116 \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) L OK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *303 REGARDING ‘PM-YUVA’ TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.08.2022  \n \nThe number of beneficiaries reached through PM-YUVA 1.0 (state-wise)  \n  \nSl. \nNo  State Name \nNumber of \nStudents \nEnrolled \nStudent \nAttempted Final \nAssessment \nStudent Passed \nFinal Assessment \n \n1 Andhra Pradesh 295 124 116 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 36 14 12 \n3 Assam 440 150 91 \n4 Bihar 0 0 0 \n5 Delhi 1920 829 655 \n6 Gujarat 9 1 0 \n7 Haryana 563 473 382 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 285 42 34 \n9 Jammu and Kashmir 58 0 0 \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |304", "t": "Production of Oilseeds and Edible Oil", "head": "The \ndetails of National Conference\ns\n \norganized by Ministry in last three years are \ngiven below:\n \nS.  No.\n \nNational Conference for\n \nDate\n \n1.\n \nKharif\n-\n2022\n \n19.04.2022\n \n2.\n \nZaid\n-\n2022\n \n27.01.2022\n \n3.\n \nRabi\n-\n2021\n-\n22\n \n21.09.2021\n \n4.\n \nKharif\n-\n2021\n \n30.04.2021\n \n5.\n \nZaid\n-\n2021\n \n22.01.2021\n \n6.\n \nRabi 2020\n-\n21\n \n21.09.2020\n \n7.\n \nKharif\n-\n2020\n \n16.04.2020\n \n8.\n \nZaid\n-\n2020\n \n17.01.2020\n \n9.\n \nRabi 2019\n-\n20\n \n20.09.2019\n \n \nIn these conferences\n,\n \nvarious issues related to agriculture at National and State \nlevels \nare\n \ndiscussed. The outcomes of discussion in these conferences on the seed \nsector are as follows:\n \n \n\n \nReview of seed requirement and availability is done in every conference. An \noutcome of this is that every year we have surplus seed in comparison to \nrequirement. The requirement and availability of seed for the last three years is \nplaced at annexure\n-\nI.\n \n\n \nThe seed of newly released high yielding varieties of different crop should be \nmade available to farmers on time.  \n \n\n \nLandmark varieties of different crop\ns should be identified and their substitute \nmay be developed to enhance productivity of different crops/varieties.  \n \n\n \nPromotion of stress tolerant, climate resilient varieties to meet the requirement \nof climate change. Inclusion of bio\n-\nfortified varieties i\nn public distribution system \nshould be encouraged to cope up nutrition deficiency in the areas of \nmalnutrition.  \n \n\n \nFocus on preparation of dynamic seed rolling plan by States Government that \ninclude newly releases short & medium duration high yielding varie\nties of \ndifferent crops.  \n \n\n \nCapacity building by means of trainings for officials of seed law enforcement \nagencies, seed certification agencies, seed testing labs to be carried out. \n \n\n \nTo popularize newly released varieties, farm demonstration & awareness \npro\ngrams for farmers to be organized by the State Government.  \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nState\n-\nwise Requirement and Availability of Certified/Quality Seed from 2019\n-\n20 to \nCurrent Kharif\n-\n2022\n \n(\nQty. in Lakh Qtls.)\n \nSTATE \n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \nKharif \n-\n2022\n \nR\n \nA\n \nR\n \nA\n \nR\n \nA\n \nR\n \nA\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n24.26\n \n31.88\n \n25.21\n \n30.85\n \n23.28\n \n29.85\n \n13.54\n \n18.29\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n0.21\n \n0.21\n \n0.21\n \n0.21\n \n0.13\n \n0.19\n \n0.15\n \n0.15\n \nAssam\n \n3.42\n \n3.42\n \n4.43\n \n4.43\n \n3.78\n \n4.24\n \n3.19\n \n6.92\n \nBihar\n \n13.67\n \n17.46\n \n15.12\n \n16.10\n \n15.53\n \n18.40\n \n5.80\n \n3.57\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n10.93\n \n12.07\n \n11.94\n \n12.28\n \n12.44\n \n12.52\n \n10.00\n \n10.19\n \nGoa\n \n0.05\n \n0.05\n \n0.06\n \n0.06\n \n0.06\n \n0.06\n \n0.05\n \n0.05\n \nGujarat\n \n53.11\n \n55.90\n \n53.35\n \n57.24\n \n64.14\n \n66.30\n \n13.64\n \n13.84\n \nHaryana\n \n17.31\n \n27.15\n \n18.86\n \n34.80\n \n18.93\n \n24.87\n \n1.23\n \n2.84\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n0.80\n \n0.80\n \n0.85\n \n0.28\n \n1.16\n \n0.95\n \n0.26\n \n0.25\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n1.72\n \n1.73\n \n1.27