��� ������������ ���������������� ���������������� ������ �������������������� ���������� ���������������������� �������� ������\n������������������\n���������� ���������������������������� ���������������������� �������������������� �������� �������������������� ���������������������� ���������������� ����������\n���������������������� ���������������������� �������������������� �������� ���������� �������������� ������ �������� ������ ���������������������� ������ ��������������\n�������������������� ���������������������� ������ ���������������� �������������� ���������������������� �������������������� ������������������ ����������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n\n������������������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |470", "t": "Export of Steel", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.470 \nFOR ANSWER ON 20/07/2022 \n \n            EXPORT OF STEEL   \n \n470.  SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAY BHATT:  \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the export of steel is facing difficulty due to costly raw  \nmaterial and if so, the details thereof; \n (b) whether the Government has taken any steps so far in this regard; and \n (c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE     (SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE) \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \n(a)to(c): Details of Export during last three years are given below:- \n \n \n         \n Government has taken various steps to increase availability of iron ore and \nmake them available at reasonable price s, which, inter-alia, including Mining and \nMineral Policy reforms to enhance production/availability of iron ore. In Budget \nannouncements 2022 -23, Basic Custom Duty (BCD) on steel scrap has been \nexempted upto 31. 03.2023. Government has also made modifications in tar iffs on \nraw materials of steel and other  steel products vide notification dated 21. 05.2022 \nwherein import duty on Anthracite/Pulverized Coal Injection (PCI) coal, Coke and \nSemicoke and Ferro -Nickel has been reduced to zero. Export duty on Iron \nores/concentrates and iron ore pellets has been raised to 50% and 45% respectively, \nand 15% export duty has been imposed on pig iron. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \nTotal Finished Steel Export  \nYear Quantity (MT) \n% Change over last \nyear \n2019-20 8.36 31.4 \n2020-21 10.78 29.1 \n2021-22 13.49 25.1 \nSource: Joint Plant Committee (JPC); MT=Million Ton "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |471", "t": "Fibre Cables under BharatNet", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 471 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \nFIBRE CABLES UNDER BHARATNET \n \n471.  SHRI RAJMOHAN UNNITHAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of the prescribed standard of fibre cables under BharatNet;  \n \n(b)  the details of inspection protocols to ensure adherence to this standard;  \n \n(c)  whether steps have been taken to  address the draft report of Comptroller and Auditor \nGeneral of India which criticises the quality and efficiency of BharatNet infrastructure in gram \npanchayats;  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n \n(e)  the number of private firms which have submitted proposals to participate in the PPP mode \nunder the project, year-wise from inception till date; and \n \n(f)  the number of private firms currently engaged under BharatNet and State -wise data on \nconnectivity provided by these firms since the inception of the project? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) Under BharatNet Phase -I, 24 core Optical Fibre  Cable (OFC) has been used whereas in \nPhase-II, 48 & above core OFC has been used for underground OFC laying.  Further, 24 & above \ncore All Dielectric Self Supporting (ADSS) cable has been used in case of aerial OFC under \nBharatNet Phase-II. \n(b) As part of the project, Acceptance Testing is conducted to check adherence to the standard.  \n(c) & (d)  Yes, Sir. Based on the observations in the draft report of the Comptroller and \nAuditor General of India, suitable steps, as required have been taken to address th e quality and \nefficiency issues on BharatNet, inter-alia including the following: \n The Service Level Agreement based Operations &Maintenance (O&M) for BharatNet \nPhase-I, through professional agencies, has been entrusted to Bharat Sanchar Nigam \nLimited. \n  Under BharatNet Phase -II, OFC has been laid directly from Block to Gram Panchayat \n(GP). \n\n2 \n \n Under BharatNet Phase-II, the implementing agencies have been entrusted to carry out the \nO&M of the network after completing/ commissioning the work.   \n BharatNet network is being given to Telecom Service Providers /Internet Service Providers \nunder Revenue Sharing agreements to provide broadband services in rural areas. \n(e) Global Bids were invited on 20.07.2021 for implementation of BharatNet under PPP model \nin 16 States and opened on 27.01.2022. No response was received. \n(f) The Phase-I of the BharatNet has been implemented through three Central Public Sector \nUndertakings namely Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL), Power Grid Corporation of India \nLimited (PGCIL) and RailTel  Corporation India Limited (RCIL). The Phase -II of the BharatNet \nis under implementation through BSNL and State Implementing Agencies except Punjab and Bihar \nwhere the Phase-II was implemented under Private Sector Model. In Punjab, HFCL and Vindhya \nTelelinks Limited (VTL) have made 4,713 Gram Panchayats (GPs) Service Ready whereas in \nBihar, VTL and Polycab have made 2,669 GPs Service Ready. Further, Bharat Broadband \nNetwork Limited (BBNL), the executing agency for BharatNet project has signed about 100 \nAgreements with Service providers for utilizing BharatNet Phase-I network in the country. BSNL \nhas on-boarded over 700 franchisees so far for utilisation of BharatNet. \n \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |472", "t": "Broadband Connection in Rural Areas", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 472 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \nBROADBAND CONNECTION IN RURAL AREAS \n \n472.  SHRI ABHISHEK BANERJEE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the percentage of population with access to broadband connection in rural India and the State-wise \nbreakup thereof;  \n \n(b)  the details of the steps being taken to increase this broadband penetration in rural India; and  \n \n(c)  the details of the steps being taken to increase digital financial literacy in rural areas? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n (a)   As per Telecom Regulatory Authority of India Yearly Performance Indicators Indian Telecom \nSector (Sixth Edition) 2021, out of 792.09 million broadband subscribers, number of rural broadband \nsubscribers is 314.83 million. Rural internet tele -density increased from 34.60 to 37.25 from Dec -20 to \nDec-21. The state-wise rural broadband subscribers percentage is attached as Annexure \n \n(b)  Mobile/Broadband/Internet services are provided in a phased manner by the Government and \nTelecom Services Provider (TSPs) in the uncovered villages of the country. The Government, with funding \nfrom Universal Service Obligation Fund (USOF), has schemes namely  Comprehensive Telecom \nDevelopment Plan for North Eastern Region, Left Wing Extremism (LWE)  Affected Area schemes, \nAspirational Districts Schemes, Comprehensive Telecom Development Plan for Islands, etc.  to \nextend mobile connectivity through setting up of towers in the uncovered villages of the country. \n \nBharatNet project is implemented in a phased manner to provide broadband connectivity to all \nGram Panchayats (GPs) in the country. The infrastructure created under BharatNet project can be utilized \nby Service providers, for provisioning of broadband/internet services. The scope of BharatNet has been \nrecently extended up to all inhabited villages beyond GPs in the country. \n \nNational Broadband Mission (NBM) was launched on 17th December 2019 with a vision to fast -\ntrack growth of digital communications infrastructure, bridge the digital divide, facilitate digital \nempowerment and inclusion, and provide affordable and universal access of broadband for all. \n \n To enhance proliferation of broadband services through public Wifi, Prime Minister Wireless Access \nNetwork Interface (PM -WANI) framework has been introduced.  The PM -WANI framew ork enables \nprovision of broadband through a distributed architecture and unbundling of functions. Under this \nframework entities are neither required to take any license nor pay any fee to the Government. The details \nof the same are available at website pmwani.gov.in \n \n(c)  Various promotion and awareness campaigns, through traditional means of publicity as well as \nemergent means such as social media platforms, have been initiated by Government to encourage citizens \nto use digital payments. \n  \n\n2 \n \nAnnexure \nState/UT wise number of Broadband subscribers in Rural India per 100 population as of 31st \nMarch 2022 \nS.No. State(s) \nTotal Rural \nsubscribers  \n(in millions) \nTele-density (%) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 14.91 43.92 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0.44 38.30 \n3 Assam 9.19 30.70 \n4 Bihar 26.73 24.34 \n5 Chattisgarh 6.86 31.39 \n6 Goa 0.59 150.50 \n7 Gujarat 15.80 43.19 \n8 Haryana 7.79 44.64 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 3.80 56.91 \n10 Jharkhand 8.36 28.98 \n11 Karnataka 16.40 43.48 \n12 Kerala 13.73 144.66 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 15.60 25.63 \n14 Maharashtra incl. Mumbai 29.04 44.74 \n15 Manipur 0.83 38.37 \n16 Meghalaya 1.06 40.07 \n17 Mizoram 0.45 81.53 \n18 Nagaland 0.85 68.94 \n19 Odisha 13.14 36.59 \n20 Punjab 8.40 47.00 \n21 Rajasthan 20.27 34.36 \n22 Sikkim 0.23 62.43 \n23 Tamil Nadu incl. Chennai 14.41 40.23 \n24 Telangana 10.77 53.54 \n25 Tripura 0.86 33.78 \n26 Uttar Pradesh (UPE+UPW) 45.39 25.54 \n27 Uttarakhand 3.76 50.57 \n28 West Bengal incl. Kolkata 19.78 31.53 \n \n \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |473", "t": "Coal Consumption", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.473 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n  \nCoal Consumption \n473. SHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n  \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n(a) whether India’s coal consumption is expected to increase at an average \nannual rate of 3.9 per cent to 1.18 billion tones in 2024; and; \n \n(b) If so, the steps taken / proposed to be taken by the Government to increase \nthe production of coal in the coming years?   \nAnswer \n  \nMINISTER OF  PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n (SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a)&(b):  During 2012-13 to 2021 -22, consumption of coal increased at CAGR \nof 3.7%. In 2021-22, the total consumption of coal was 1028MT. Consumption \nof coal is estimated to increase to 1069 MT in 2022 -23 which is further \nexpected to increase to 1112 MT in 2023-24.   \nIn order to ramp up domestic production of coal, several steps have been taken \nup by the Government of which the major steps include the following: \n Auction of coal blocks to private parties including Captive Power Projects. \n Commercial Auction of coal blocks on revenue sharing basis.   \n Allowing sale of coal or lignite of captive mines up to 50 percent after \nmeeting the requirement of the end use plant linked with the mine. \n 100% Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) for commercial mining. \n  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |474", "t": "Telecom Towers in Punjab", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 474 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \nTELECOM TOWERS IN PUNJAB \n \n474.  SHRIMATI KIRRON KHER:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the total number of villages in the rural areas of Chandigarh and Punjab where telecom \ntowers have been installed, State/UT and telecom company-wise during each of the last two years \nand the current year; and  \n \n(b)  the number of such towers targeted to be installed in the rural areas of Chandigarh and \nPunjab in the coming financial year? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a)  Sir, the total number of villages in the rural areas of Chandigarh and Punjab where Base \nTransceiver Stations (BTSs) have been installed, State/UT and telecom company-wise during each \nof the last two years and the current year is attached as Annexure. \n \n(b)  The Telecom Service Providers (TSPs) decide their future plan based on their capacity, \ntechno-commercial assessment and other parameters.  \n \n  \n\n2 \n \nANNEXURE \n \nTOTAL NUMBER OF VILLAGES IN THE RURAL AREAS OF CHANDIGARH AND \nPUNJAB WHERE BASE TRANSCEIVER STATIONS (BTSs) HAVE BEEN INSTALLED, \nSTATE/UT AND TELECOM COMPANY -WISE DURING EACH OF THE LAST TWO \nYEARS AND THE CURRENT YEAR: \n \nS No State/UT \nName of \nTSP \nNo. of Census inhabited villages where BTSs* were \ninstalled  \nDuring  \n2020-21 \nDuring \n2021-22 \nDuring current year  \n2022-23  \n1 Chandigarh \nBAL 3 4 NIL \nBSNL NIL NIL NIL \nRJIL NIL NIL NIL \nVIL 1 1 NIL \n2 Punjab \nBAL 345 347 35 \nBSNL NIL NIL NIL \nRJIL 1 NIL NIL \nVIL 120 636 108 \n \n* The data in the tabulation gives the number of village where Base Transceiver Stations (BTSs) \nwere installed, as Tower is a shared infrastructure, and may not be owned by Telecom Service \nProviders (TSPs). However, BTS is owned by TSPs and it is the BTS that accounts for transmission \nof mobile signal and coverage in the area. \n \nLegends: \nBAL :  Bharti Airtel Limited \nBSNL :  Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited \nRJIL :  Reliance Jio Infocomm Limited \nVIL  :  Vodafone Idea Limited \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |475", "t": "Death of Animals due to Train Accidents", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 475 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nDEATH OF ANIMALS DUE TO TRAIN ACCIDENTS \n \n475.  SHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \n SHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI: \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that increasing number of animals, \nespecially elephants have got killed in train accidents over the last \nfive years and the current year; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the number of elephants \nkilled during the said period; year-wise;   \n \n(c) whether the Government is taking/has taken any action in this \nregard and if so, the details thereof; and   \n \n(d) the details of the steps taken by the Government to prevent such \nincidents in future? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 475 BY SHRI ANURAG SHARMA AND \nSHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 20.07.2022 REGARDING DEATH OF ANIMALS DUE TO \nTRAIN ACCIDENTS \n \n(a) & (b) The details of elephants killed in train accidents during the \nlast five years and current year are as under: \n \nYear No. of Elephants killed \n2017 15 \n2018 26 \n2019 10 \n2020 16 \n2021 19 \n2022 (upto July, 2022) 07 \n \n(c) & (d) A number of preventive measures undertaken/being taken \nby Zonal Railways in coordination with Ministry of Environment and \nForest for avoiding such incidences include the following:  \n \n(i) Imposition of speed restrictions in identified locations, \n \n(ii) Provision of sign age board to warn loco pilots about identified \nelephant corridors, \n \n(iii) Sensitization of Train Crew and Station Masters on a regular \nbasis,  \n \n(iv) Need based clearance of vegetation on the sides of the track \nwithin railway land, \n  \n(v) Construction of underpasses and ramps for the movement of \nelephants at identified locations, \n \n(vi) Provision of fencing at isolated locations, \n \n(vii) Forest Department staff deputed in Railway control offices to \nliaison with Railway and elephant trackers engaged by Forest  \nDepartment for timely action by alerting station master and loco \npilots, \n \n(viii) Installation of Honey bee sound systems at locations, which \nare prone to crossing of elephants, \n \n(ix) Counselling of villagers for avoiding cattle coming near track. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |477", "t": "Graddal Fuel Pump", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.477 \nFOR ANSWER ON 20/07/2022 \n \n             GRADDAL FUEL PUMP \n477.  SHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI: \n          SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:  \n \n Will the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state :  \n(a) whether it is a fact that the vehicle could not be started even after installing the      \ngraddal  accessories, etc of S.M.S.-II fuel pump in Bhilai Steel Plant of Chhattisgarh; \n(b) the details of the total number of additional items procured for graddal vehicle;  \n(c) the reasons for graddal vehicle lying idle which is bought for crores of rupees;  \n(d) whether an investigation has been conducted by the Government for the stock of \nsafety shoes and safety gloves as well as the rigging of diesel and oil in the workshop; \n(e) if so, the action taken against the guilty persons;  \n(f) whether the Government is aware that the Price Rate Worker (PRW) is being paid less \nthan the minimum wage and car elessness is also being taken in buying the spare parts of \nthe vehicles; and \n(g) if so, the action taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE             (SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE) \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \n(a) No, Sir. The Gradall  machine started and operated smoothly after changing of fuel \npumps every time during the period of its operation i.e. 2007-2018. \n(b) Total 201 numbers of additional items have been purchased for this machine. Detail \nof additional items procured is at Annexure 1. \n(c) The Gradall machine has been in operation from year of purchase i.e. 2007 till \n2018, till its useful life.  \n(d) Stock verification has been carried out and no discrepancies have been found.  \n(e) Does not arise in view of (d) above. \n(f)&(g):There are no Piece Rated Workers (PRW) in Bhilai Steel Plant. However, the \ncontractual labours are paid by the respective employer (i.e. Contractors) as per the \nMinimum Wages notified by Government of Chhattisgarh. In addition, “Additional \nWelfare Amenity” @ R s. 88.46 per day of attendance, subject to maximum of Rs. \n2300.00 per month is reimbursed to the Contractor towards payment made to the \ncontract labour. As on date, Bhilai Steel Plant has not received any notice from the \nState Government for non - payment of Minimum Wages. \n \nCarelessness/negligence has not been noted in the purchase of vehicle spare parts \nas they are purchased as per procedure / guidelines. \n*****  \n\n \n \nAnnexure I \nTotal no. \nof  Items  Details of Items \n16 Hose Assembly, Suction Switch, Element Air Filters, Ignition Switch, etc. \n3 Fuel Filers, Oil filters \n1 Drill blades  \n2 Retainer, Pilot Adoptor \n1 Retainer Tap Hole \n1 Moil Peg PNT F/DRL M/C \n80 Different types of Hose Assy, Adaptor, Pilot, Blade,Tap Hole Drill Rammer \nHammer CF S29 of cast, etc. \n1 Engine Harness \n28 Sendor, Relays, Sensors, Filters, Center Pin Assy, Membrane Acc, Packing \nKit ,Hoist CY, Seal kitTilt Gland, Turbo Charger etc. \n14 Swing Motor Assy, Swing Transmission, Swing Brake Assy, Tilt Brake Assy, \nTrack Adjuster Assy, etc. \n1 Moil Peg Point \n51 CE Muffler, R.H. Joystick Assy, L.H. Joystick Assembly, Foot Pedal Valve, \nPilot Manifold Assembly, Curved Link Assembly, Pin, Straight Link Assy etc. \n1 Tool Cylinder Assembly \n1 Fuel Injection Pump \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |478", "t": "Development of Jute Sector", "head": " \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 478 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF JUTE SECTOR \n \n \n \n478. DR. MOHAMMAD JAWED: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any plan for the development of the jute sector and to \nincrease the quality and productivity of raw jute including for diversification and modernization of the \njute sector in the Seemanchal region of Bihar other th an Purnea District and if so, the details thereof; \nand  \n \n(b) whether the Government has taken any decision for working and sustaining the demand for jute \nproducts in Kishanganj district and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) & (b): The Government of India has approved an Umbrella Program namely National Jute \nDevelopment Program (NJDP) with an outlay of Rs. 485.58 crore  for overall development and \npromotion of jute sector during the period of 2021 -22 to 2025 -26. NJDP also comprises schemes for \nincreasing the quality and productivity of raw jute including diversification and modernization of the \njute sector. NJDP is a Pan  India program which also covers districts (Araria, Madhepura, Saharsa, \nSupaul, Purnea, Kishanganj and Katihar) of Seemanchal region of Bihar State. \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |479", "t": "Railway Hospitals", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.479 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nRAILWAY HOSPITALS \n \n   479. SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA: \n \n            Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Northern Railways Central Hospital, New Delhi is \nunderinvested with lack of adequate facilities in Super Specialist \ncategory like CTVS, Cardiology, Neurology, Gastroenterology unlike \nthe Railway Hospitals in Mumbai, Chennai and Kolkata and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n \n(b) whether any Committee on Rationalisation of Railways has \nrecommended that Railway Hospitals be upgraded with more \ninvestments so as to provide adequate facilities and manpower; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a): No, Sir. Northern Railway Central Hospital is not underinvested \nwith lack of adequate facilities in Super Specialist treatment. Clinical \n\nconsultation is being done by case -to-case Consultants in super \nspecialties in the hospital. Various investigation/ tests like \nEchocardiogram, Holter Analysis, Treadmill Test, Electro Encephelo \nGram (EEG), Nerve Conduction Velocity (NCV), Electro \n….2/- \n-2- \n \nMayo Graphy (EMG), Upper Gastro Intestinal Endoscopy, Colonoscopy, \nPlastic Surgery etc. are done in -house in the hospital. For tertiary care \ncases patients are being referred to Empanelled Private Hospitals from \nNorthern Railway Central Hospital.  \n \n(b) & (c) : Yes, Sir. Shri Sanjeev Sanyal Committee has recommended \nfor upgradation of  Railway Hospitals. Further, up-gradation of  Railway \nHospitals is an ongoing process and works and equipments are \nsanctioned to improve the existing infrastructure and add new \nfacilities based on the needs of the Railway beneficiaries. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |48", "t": "Assistance Policies for Tribal and Rural Artists", "head": "����������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n(a) whether any social and financial assistance policies for tribal and rural artists have been \ndevised by the Government; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any steps have been taken by the Government to start and develop new art forms \nor cultures under Art Culture Development Scheme during the last two years; \n(d)  if so, the State-wise details thereof; \n(e) the details of the proposals submitted by various Cultural Organizations and the financial \nassistance provided to the said organizations and the achievements made under the said \nscheme during the last five years; and \n(f) the details of the financial assistance provided or the funds disbursed under the said \nscheme so far? \n \n \n��������������\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������\n(a) Yes sir, the Ministry of Culture administers various schemes for providing financial \nassistance to the artists engaged in performing arts throughout the country including the \ntribal and rural artists.��\n(b) The names and details of the schemes mentioned at (a) above are placed at ���������������������� \n(c) No scheme by the name of Art Culture Development Scheme is administered by the \nMinistry of Culture. However, Ministry of Culture administers an Umbrella Scheme by the \nname of Kala Sanskriti Vikas Yojana (KSVY) under which all major arts forms & cultures of \nclassical, folk & tribal including vanishing arts are covered. \n(d) Does not arise in view of (c) above. \n(e) The details of the proposals of cultural organizations and financial assistance provided to \nthem during the last five years under the various schemes/scheme components of Kala \nSanskriti Vikas Yojana (KSVY) are placed at �������������������������� \n(f) The details of funds disbursed under Kala Sanskriti Vikas Yojana (KSVY) during five years \nare placed at ������������������������. \n\n��\n������������������������\n��\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. †48 for 18.07.2022 \n��\n������ �������������������� ���������������������� �������� �������������������� ������ �������������������������� ��������������������\n������������������������������������\n��\n The objective of this scheme is to provide financial support for all genres of performing arts \nactivities like dramatic groups, theatre groups, music ensembles, children theatre etc. and \nimparting training of artists by their respective Guru on regular basis in line with Guru–Shishya \nParampara. As per the scheme, support is provided to 1 Guru and maximum 18 Shishyas in the \nfield of theatre and 1 Guru and maximum 10 Shishyas in the field of music & dance. \n \nAmount of Assistance – Guru Rs.15000/- p.m., Shishya – Rs.2000-10000/-p.m (depending upon \nthe age of artist). \n�� ��\n��\n������ �������������� ������ �������������������� ���������������������� �������� �������������������� ������ �������� �������� ����������������������\nThe scheme consists of eight components. The names and details of the scheme \ncomponents are as given below: \n \n�������� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n The objective of this scheme component is to provide support for cultural activities at Large \nscale National / International level. The Quantum of assistance under this scheme is up to Rs. 5 \nCr. \n \n���������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n The objective of this scheme component is to provide financial support to NGOs/ Societies/ \nTrusts/ Universities etc. for Seminars, Conference, Research, Workshops, Festivals, Exhibitions, \nSymposia, Production of Dance, Drama-Theatre, Music etc.  The maximum grants provided under \nCFPG is Rs.5 Lakh (Rs. 20 Lakh under exceptional circumstances) \n \n������������ ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������\n��\n The objective of this scheme component is to promote and preserve the cultural heritage of \nthe Himalayas through research, training and dissemination through audio visual programmes. \nThe financial support is provided to the organizations in the States falling under the Himalayan \nRegion i.e. Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Sikkim and Aruna"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |480", "t": "Indo-Nepal Bharat Gaurav Tourist Trains", "head": "  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 480  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nINDO-NEPAL BHARAT GAURAV TOURIST TRAINS \n \n 480.      SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR  SU:  \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Indian Railways has flagged off Bharat Gaurav \nTourist Trains   connecting India and Nepal; \n \n(b)       if so, the details along with the response and future prospects \nthereof;  \n \n(c)   whether the Railways has also proposed to run Krishna \nCircuit, Buddhist Circuit and other circuit trains; \n \n(d)      if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e)      if not, the reasons therefor ? \n    \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) and (b) : Yes, Sir. Indian Railway Catering and Tourism \nCorporation Limited (IRCTC) operated  the Bharat Gaurav tourist \ncircuit train with a Ramayana theme - based itinerary departing from \nDelhi on  21.6.2022 covering Nepal with the following itinerary: \n \n \n- - 2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \nDelhi Safdarjung -Ayodhya-Buxar-Janakpur (Nepal) -Varanasi-Nasik-\nHospet-Rameshwaram- Kanchipuram-Bhadrachalam Road -Delhi \nSafdarjung. \n \n499 passengers availed this trip. \n \nThe Bharat Gaurav Train policy gives full flexibility to the service \nprovider to decide on themes and routes.  IRCTC has also launched \nthe Ramayana theme circuit w.e.f 24.8.2022 departin g from Delhi \nand covering Nepal. \n(c) to (e) : No, Sir. Indian Railways have issued the ‘Bharat Gaurav \nTrains’ policy with the objective to showcase India’s rich cultural \nheritage and magnificent historical places to the people of India \nand the world through Bharat Gaurav Trains. These are theme \nbased tourist circuit trains.  Under this policy, Railways does not \npropose routes or circuits; ins tead, service providers have full \nflexibility to decide the themes/itineraries for operation of Bharat \nGaurav tourist circuit trains which may include any theme -based \ntourist circuit to be decided by the service providers. \n * * * * *    \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |481", "t": "Mega Textile Park under PM Mitra", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 481 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \n \nMEGA TEXTILE PARK UNDER PM MITRA \n \n \n481. SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) whether Srikakulam district has been proposed by Government of Andhra Pradesh (AP) to develop \nmega textile park under PM MITRA and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the list of locations proposed by AP to develop mega textile park under PM MITRA;  \n \n(c) the list of locations , fund allocation for the Government’s recently announced plans to create 75 \ntextile hubs like Tamil Nadu’s Tiruppur;  \n \n(d) the State-wise, year-wise turnover, exports of textile industry for the last ten years; and  \n \n(e) the district-wise, year-wise turnover, exports of textile industry in AP since 2014? \n \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) & (b): Government of India has decided to set up PM MITRA Park (Greenfield/Brownfield) \nacross the country. Andhra Pradesh has sent one proposal only for setting up of PM MITRA Park at \nKopparthy, YSR Kadapa District. The final selection of site shall be done through challenge matrix.    \n(c): Hon’ble Minister of Textiles has announced to create 75 Tirupur like textile hubs across the \ncountry for holistic development of textile sector to commemorate 75  year of India’s independence. \nThe list of such textile hubs has not yet been finalised. \n(d):  The State-wise, year-wise exports of textile industry for the last ten years is at Annexure-I.  \n(e): The district -wise, year -wise exports of textile industry in Andhra Pradesh is at Annexure-II\n\n \nAnnexure I \nThe State-wise, year-wise exports of textile industry for the last ten years (Value in Million USD) \nExport of certain Principal Commodities