akhand \nAmritsar \nKolkata \nIndustrial \nCorridor \n(AKIC)  \nIntegrated \nManufacturing \nCluster (IMC) at \nKhurpia Farm \n Consultant has been \nappointed for carrying \nout Detailed Master \nPlanning and \nPreliminary \nEngineering activities;  \n Share Holder \nAgreement (SHA)/ \nState Support \nAgreement (SSA) has \nbeen approved by \nNICDIT ; \n Project proposal has \nbeen approved by the \nBoard of Trustees of \n- \n\nNICDIT  on 8 th June, \n2022. \n3. Haryana \nDelhi \nMumbai \nIndustrial \nCorridor \n(DMIC) \nIntegrated Multi \nModal Logistics \nHub (IMLH), at \nNangal \nChaudhary, \nHaryana \n Government of India \napproved the project in \nMay, 2018; \n SPV has been \nincorporated; \n 686 acre of land has \nbeen transferred by the \nState Government to \nthe project SPV; \n State agencies have \nbeen engaged \nfor  External \nconnectivity works \n(water, power & road)    \nEquity – \n208.05 \nDebt – \n100.00 \nAmritsar \nKolkata \nIndustrial \nCorridor \n(AKIC) \nIntegrated \nManufacturing \nCluster (IMC) at \nHisar, Haryana \n Consultant has been \nappointed for carrying \nout Detailed Master \nPlanning and \nPreliminary Engineering \nactivities; \n- \n4. Rajasthan \nDelhi \nMumbai \nIndustrial \nCorridor \n(DMIC) \nKhushkheda \nBhiwadi \nNeemrana \nInvestment \nRegion (KBNIR) \n  \nJodhpur Pali \nMarwar \nIndustrial Area \n(JPMIA)  \n SPV has been \nincorporated; \n SSA/SHA has been \nexecuted;  \n Consultant has been \nappointed for carrying \nout Detailed Master \nPlanning and \nPreliminary \nEngineering activities; \n For KBNIR, 14 acre of \nland is in possession; \n For JPMIA, 1573 acre \nis in possession  \nInitial \nEquity – \n4.90  \n5. Madhya \nPradesh \nDelhi \nMumbai \nIndustrial \nCorridor \n(DMIC) \nIntegrated \nIndustrial \nTownship ‘Vikram \nUdyogpuri’, \nUjjain, 1100 acre \n State Govt. has \ntransferred 1026 acre \nland to the Project SPV \n Major Trunk \nIn"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |645", "t": "Ban on Wheat Export", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 645 (H) \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20th JULY, 2022 \n \nBAN ON WHEAT EXPORT \n \n645(H).  SHRI RAJIV RANJAN SINGH ALIAS LALAN SINGH: \nSHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज ्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री) be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether the Government has completely banned the export of wheat;  \n(b) if so, the quantity of wheat and wheat flour exported from the country so far after \nthe ban;  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to lift the ban on the export of wheat and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(d) the wheat stock currently available with the Government;  \n(e) whether the Government has any data regarding the quantity of private wheat stock \navailable in the country;  \n(f) whether the Government proposes to implement any incentive scheme for the \nfarmers to promote the production of wheat; and  \n(g) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a):    The Government, on 13 th May, 2022, has amended the export policy of wheat from ‘Free’ \nto ‘Prohibited’. Export of wheat shall be allowed under the following conditions: \n  \n(i)      As a transitional arrangement, export will be allowed in case of shipments where Irrevocable \nLetter of Credit (ICLC) has been issued on or before the date of this Notification, subject to \nsubmission of documentary evidence as prescribed; or \n \n(ii)     Export will also be allowed on the basis of permission granted by the Government to other \ncountries to meet their food security needs and based on the request of their governments. \n\n2 \n \n  \n(b) :          The total quantity of wheat exported after imposition of ‘prohibition’ on export of wheat \ni.e. from 14th May, 2022 till 14th July, 2022 is 14.76 Lakh Metric Tonne (LMT). With effect from \n6th July, 2022, the export policy of wheat flour (atta) though  ‘Free’, but export shall be subject to \nrecommendation of Inter-Ministerial Committee (IMC) on export of wheat. The export of wheat \nflour (atta) from 7th July, 2022 till 14th July, 2022 is 29,460 MT.  \n \n(c) : The Central Government is closely monitoring the situation with regard to Wheat at the \ndomestic and international level and no decision on lifting the ban has been taken.  \n \n(d) : The total Wheat currently available with the Central Pool Stock of the Country is 285.10 \nLMT as on 01.07.2022.  \n \n(e) : The Government has no data regarding  the quantity of private wheat stock available in \nthe country. \n \n(f)&(g) :  Under the National Food Security Mission( NFSM)programme,  NFSM -Wheat \nprogramme to increase the production of wheat through area expansion and productivity \nenhancement; restoring soil fertility and productivity; and en hancing farm level economy is \nundertaken. The NFSM -Wheat is being implemented in 124 districts of 10 states & 2 UTs viz. \nJammu & Kashmir and Ladakh. \n  \n Under NFSM, assistance for wheat is provided to the farmers for interventions viz. cluster \ndemonstrations on improved package of practices, demonstrations on cropping system, Seed \ndistribution of High Yielding Varieties (HYVs), improved farm machineries/resource \nconservation machineries/tools, efficient water application tools, plant protection measures, \nnutrient management/soil ameliorants, processing & post-harvest equipments and cropping system \nbased trainings to the farmers etc.  \n***** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |646", "t": "Promoting Handloom Sector", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 646 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \nPROMOTING HANDLOOM SECTOR \n \n \n646. SHRI AJAY NISHAD:  \n        SHRI T.R. BAALU: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) whether the Handloom Sector in the country is perishing due to lack of support from the Union and \nState Governments;  \n \n(b) if not, details of financial aid and institutional support extended by the Union Government and \nvarious State Governments to protect Handloom Sector and the growth achieved therein during the last \nthree years;  \n \n(c) whether any assistance is available from the International institutions including United Nations \nOrganization for protecting and promoting the Handlooms Industry and weavers; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof including the assistance/ recognition by India in the last three years? \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) to (d): The Government of India and various State Governments are providing financial and \nInstitutional support for development of handlooms and welfare of weavers. The Government of India, \nMinistry of Textiles is implementing following schemes: \ni) National Handloom Development Programme \nii) Raw Material Supply Scheme \nUnder the above schemes, financial assistance is provided to eligible handloom agencies/weavers for \nraw materials, purchase of looms/accessories/lighting units, construction of worksheds, Mudra loans at \nconcessional rates, design development, common& techn ical infrastructure, marketing of handloom \nproducts, developmental projects, registration of handloom products under GI Act, promotion of \nHandloom Mark and India Handloom Brand, inspection of powerloom units etc. \nAt the same time, the State Governments hav e been implementing their handloom schemes for \npromotion of handlooms.  \nMinistry of Textiles and State Governments are extending support to Institutions across the country for \nimplementation of schematic/non -schematic interventions for development, promoti on of handlooms \nand welfare of weavers. These include agencies/bodies working in field for handlooms like State \nDirectorates, State Corporations, State Apex Co -operative Societies, Primary Co -operative Societies, \nProducers Companies, Non -Government Organisations, Weavers’ Service Centres, Indian Institutes of \nHandloom Technology, Textiles Committee/National Handloom Development Corporation, Handloom \nExport Promotion Council, National Institute of Fashion Technology, State Enforcement offices etc.  \nThe handloom Sector is supported by the budgetary allocations made by the Government of India and \nState Governments. However, t he Government of India, Ministry of Textiles has approached United \nNations Development Programme for capacity building of Handloom Producers Companies.  \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |647", "t": "Filling up the Vacancies", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS \n(DEPARTMENT OF PERSONNEL & TRAINING) \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 647 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022) \n \nFILLING UP THE VACANCIES  \n \n†647. SHRI DINESH CHANDRA YADAV:  \n          SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV:  \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of vacancies notified by UPSC and the number of posts mentioned in the \nnotifications during the last ten years, year-wise;  \n(b)  the number of posts filled/not filled on the basis of all the above mentioned notified \nvacancies and the present status of these notifications;  \n(c)  the total number of candidates selected by UPSC during the last ten years, year-wise;  \n(d)  the quantum of an nual budget of UPSC and the year -wise amount released by the Union \nGovernment to UPSC during the last ten years; and  \n(e)  the number of employees working in UPSC at present? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES \nAND PENSIONS AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c):  Every year, the Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) conducts its Examinations \nas per the Programme of Examinations (Calendar)year notified by the Commission  well in \nadvance for a calendar year. \n \nThe number of vacancies advertised and number of candidates recommended during the \nlast ten years are as under: \n \nYear No. of Vacancies* No of Candidates \nRecommended** \n2012-13 5681 5705 \n2013-14 11326 8852 \n2014-15 8347 8272 \n2015-16 8096 6866 \n2016-17 6726 5735 \n2017-18 5729 6294 \n2018-19 5207 4399 \n2019-20 5913 5230 \n2020-21 4997 4214 \n2021-22 5153 4119 \n \n    *Recommendation to some of the reported vacancies might have been made in the subsequent \nyear. \n**may include recommendation to vacancies reported the preceding year. \n \n \nContd.P.-2/- \n\n-2- \n \n \n \n \n(d):   The details of allotted Budget (Charged) under Major Head 2051 -UPSC for the last ten \nyears are as under: \n \nFinancial Year Final Grant (in Crore) Actual Expenditure(in Crore) \n \n2012-13 146.53 146.52 \n2013-14 166.64 166.62 \n2014-15 188.81 188.72 \n2015-16 213.00 212.94 \n2016-17 241.85 241.78 \n2017-18 241.30 240.99 \n2018-19 280.75 280.64 \n2019-20 298.16 297.96 \n2020-21 285.00 284.93 \n2021-22 316.18 316.16 \n \n(e):    As on 01.07.2022, the total number of employees working in UPSC is 1238. \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |648", "t": "Foreign Trade", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 648 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20th JULY, 2022 \n \nFOREIGN TRADE \n \n648.  SHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज ्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री) be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether the Government intends to have a policy framework with regard to foreign \ntrade with any country in the future; and  \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the name of countries with which India has signed \nMoUs for trade in future in the recent Indian Delegates visit? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a) & (b): India has so far signed 13 Free Tra de Agreements (FTAs) and 6 \nPreferential Trade Agreements (PTAs) with its partner countries for \npreferential access in those markets. These also include the recently \nsigned FTAs with Mauritius, the UAE and Australia. As part of its \nendeavour to enlarge the scope of market access, India has entered into \nFTA negotiations with promising trading partners namely, the United \nKingdom, Canada and the European Union. Apart from these \nFTAs/PTAs, India has also entered into MoUs for Trade and investment \narrangements with its trading partner countries. Some of these are MoU \nbetween DP World FZE (DP World) of the UAE and National \nInvestment Promotion and Facilitation Agency (Invest India), MOU \nbetween Invest India and Abu Dhabi investment office etc. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |649", "t": "K-Rail Project", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA                                                                                                            \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA                                                                                                                                 \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 649                                                                                                           \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n                                               K-RAIL PROJECT \n   649.   SHRI HIBI EDEN : \n             Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government of Kerala has submitted new documents / \nclarifications for the K -Rail (Silver Line) project for approval and \npermission, on or after 16.03.2022 and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has re -examined the project on or after \n16.03.2022 and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the consideration of the project is likely to depend upon \ntechnological viability and economic viability of the project and if so, the \ndetails of technical reasons that may be considered before the \nGovernment grants permission/ issue NOC; and  \n \n(d) whether the Government is aware of the protests over K Rail – \n(Silver Line) Project and if so, the stand of the Government in this \nregard? \n \n    ANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \n ELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n   (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW)  \n \n(a)  No, Sir. \n \n(b) No, Sir. Sufficient details for technical feasibility are not available in \nthe Detailed Project Report (DPR). Therefore, Kerala Rail Development \nCorporation Limited (KRDCL), a Joint Venture Company of Government of \nKerala (51%) and Ministry of Railways (49%) has been advised to provide \ndetailed technical documents such as alignment plan, particulars of \n\nrailway land and private land, crossings over existing railway network, \nduly depicting affected railway asset through  Zonal Railway after site \nverification for detailed examination of the project and to arrive at \nconclusion about feasibility of project. Details have not yet been \nsubmitted by KRDCL \n \n \n(c) Yes, Sir. Soil condition, natural drainage, environmental issues, \nInteroperatibility, debt serviceability with mainly passenger traffic, etc. \nwill be looked into after receiving of details from KRDCL. \n \n(d) Many representations are being received in this Ministry. Major \nissues raised are : -  (i) thousands of hectares of cultivable land, 20,000 \nthousand houses, shops and business establishments will be ruined (ii) \nAngamaly railway station operates railway sidings for Food Corporation \nof India. Silver line will be an obstacle for expansion (iii) P roposed \nalignment will  demolish many religious structures (iv) Proposed track \nbeing built on standard gauge so it cannot be integrated with the \nexisting railway track network (v) Proposed route is prepared in such a \nway that the same is adversely affecting  the life and property of the \nresidents of Poundukadavu who have been residing there for the past 70 \nyears. (vi) Kerala Govt. having a big amount of debt. Even for giving \nsalary and pension Govt is borrowing a big amount. Therefore, this \nproject will be a heavy loss for Govt. of Kerala. (vii) The proposed plans \nbe adjusted in such a way that the land is not acquired for the purpose of \nthe silver line project. \nThese issues will be looked into after receiving of details from KRDCL. \n \n                                                    ***** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |65", "t": "Project Elephant", "head": "������������������� �\n���������������������������������������� �\n��\n���������� �\n����������������������� �\n�������������� ������������� �\n��\n����������������������������������������������� �\n��\n�������������������������� �\n��\n������������������������������������������������ �\n��\n�������������������������������������������������\n��\n�������������������� �\n��������� ������ ������� ��� ��������� ��������� ������ ������ ������������ ���\n����������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������\n��������� ������� ��� ����� ���������� ��� ���� ������ ����� ����� ���� ��� ���\n������������������ ���������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������� �\n��\n������ �\n��\n������������������������������������������� �\n��\n������ ��������������������� �\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������� �\n��\n� �\n\n�\n���������������������������������������� � ������� ���������������\n�������������������������������������� �����������������������\n����� ���������� ��� �� 5(*$5',1*\u0003 ´&216758&7,21\u0003 2)\u0003 1$7,21$/\u0003\n+,*+:$<6\u0003,1\u00037$0,/\u00031$'8µ �\n�����������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� ����� ��� ��� ��� ���������� ����� ��� ���� ������ ������ ���� ������\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������� ��������������\n�������� ����\n��\n���������������������������������������������������������� ���������������\n����������������� ��������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� �� � �������� ���������� ����� ��������� ��� ������ ���\n�������������������������������� �\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������� �����������������������������������\n����� ����� ���� ����������� ����� ������������ ���� ������ ��� ��������������\n������������� �������� �������� ����������� ��� ��������� ����� �����������\n���������������������� ��������� ���� ������������ ��������������� ���\n����� �\n��\n��\n��\n��\n�\n�\n�\n� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |650", "t": "Development of Railway Infrastructure in Gujarat", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 650 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF RAILWAY INFRASTRUCTURE IN GUJARAT \n \n650.   SHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH RATHOD: \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) the details regarding rail infrastructure  development, gauge \nconversion (meter to broad gauge) and fund utilisation made therein in \nGujarat, districtwise; \n \n(b) the details of the progress made on the pending  gauge conversion \nof the railway track in Sabarkantha Lok Sabha constituency; and \n \n(c) the details of funds utilized for the Modasa to  Shamlaji upto \nUdaipur, Ahmedabad to Himmatnagar upto  Udaipur and Himmatnagar \nKhedbrahma gauge conversion projects? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 650 BY SHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH \nRATHOD TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2022 \nREGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF RAILWAY INFRASTRUCTURE IN \nGUJARAT \n \n(a) to (c ): Projects are not sanctioned S tate-wise/ district -wise/ \nconstituency-wise, but sanctioned Zone wise as Indian Railways’ \nnetwork may span across various State boundaries.  \n \nHowever, to improve rail infrastructure development in Gujarat, \npresently 19 Gauge Conversion projects, covering a length of 1,636 km \nat a cost of ` 10,077 crore, falling fully/partly in the state of Gujarat, \nwhich include Sabarkantha constituency  of Gujarat also, are under \ndifferent stages o f planning/approval/execution, out of which, 641 km \nlength has been commissioned and an expenditure of ` 3,589 crore \nhas been incurred up to March, 2022.  \n \nGujarat is covered by North Western Railway and Western Railway \nZones of Indian Railways. The details of allotment of funds and \nexpenditure project-wise and Zonal Railway wise are made available \nin public domain on Indian Railways website i.e. \nwww.indianrailways.gov.in> Ministry of Railways> Railway Board> \nAbout Indian Railways>Railway Board Directorates> Finance (Budget), \nPink Book (year)>Railway wise Works Machinery & Rolling Stock \nProgramme. \n \nAhmedabad-Himmatnagar-Udaipur gauge conversion project including \nModasa-Shamlaji Road new li ne (318 km) has been take n up, o ut of \nwhich, 256.39 km length has been commissioned. An expenditure of \n`2082.58 crore has been incurred upto March, 2022 and an outlay of \n`268 crore has been provided for this project for the year 2022-23. \nContd…2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \nSince 2014, there is substantial increase in Budget allocation for \ninfrastructure projects and commensurate commissioning. Average \nannual Budget allocation on infrastructure projects and safety works, \nfalling fully/partly in Gujarat during 2014 -19 has been  increased to \n`3,327 crore per year from  ` 589 crore per year during 2009 -14. Thus, \nthe average annual Budget allocation during 2014 -19 is 465% more \nthan average annual allocation of 2009 -14.  Budget allocation for \nthese projects has been increased to  ` 4,803 crore in financial year \n2019-20 (716% more than average annual budget allocation of 2009 -\n14), ` 4,071 crore in financial year 2020 -21 (591% more than average \nannual Budget outlay of 2009 -14) and ` 4,924 crore  in financial year \n2021-22 (736% more than average annual budget outlay during 2009 -\n14). Budget outlay of ` 4,745 crore has been provided for these \nprojects for financial year 2022 -23 which is 706% more than average \nannual budget outlay during 2009-14. \n \nDuring 2014 -22, 1294 km sections (142 km New line, 607 km Gauge \nconversion and 545 km Doubling ), falling fully/partly in the S tate of  \nGujarat, have been commissioned at an average rate of 161.75 km per \nyear, which is 22.54% more than average annual commis sioning \nachieved during 2009-14 (132 Km/year). \n \n*****  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |651", "t": "Fisheries Subsidy Negotiations at WTO", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 651 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20th JULY, 2022 \n \nFISHERIES SUBSIDY NEGOTIATIONS AT WTO \n \n651.  SHRI VINAYAK RAUT:  \nSHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज ्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री) be pleased \nto state: \n(a) the details of final text that has come up for fisheries subsidy negotiations at the \nWTO;  \n(b) whether the proposed text provides level -playing field for Indian fisheries sector, \nour traditional fishermen and their livelihood;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) the details of support measures that developed countries provide to their fishing \nsector and reasons behind their demand to stop developing countries from giving \nsubsidies? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n \n(a) : The Agreement on Fisheries Subsidies (Agreement) agreed at the recently concluded \nWorld Trade Organization (WTO) Ministerial meeting will prohibit subsidies being \nprovided for Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated (IUU) fishing and overfished stocks. As \nSpecial and Differential Treatment (S&DT), Developing Countries and Least Developed \nCountries (LDCs) have been allowed a transition period of two years after the entry in to \nforce of the Agreement.  The Agreement also prohibits providing subsidies for fishing in \nhigh seas, which are outside the jurisdiction of coastal countries and Regional Fisheries \nManagement Organizations/ Arrangements (RFMO/As).   \n\nAs per the Agreement, no prohibition has been imposed on a WTO Member regarding \ngranting or maintaining subsidy  to its vessel or operator as long as it is not carrying out \nIUU. Similarly, no prohibition on providing subsidies has been imposed for fishing \nregarding overfished stocks as long as such subsidies are implemented to rebuild the stock \nto a biologically sustainable level.  \n(b) &(c):   Under the Agreement, enough flexibility is available to India with a complete right \nto determine within its Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) regarding any IUU fishing activity \nand on overfished stocks. Since fish move across marit ime zones, IUU fishing deprives \nIndia of fisheries resources, thereby greatly impacting the livelihood of our coastal \ntraditional fishing communities that depend upon fisheries. With the implementation of \nAgreement, India will be in a position to take acti on against subsidies provided to foreign \nvessels or operators of other countries by challenging such subsidies at the WTO \nCommittee on Fisheries Subsidies and the dispute settlement system of the WTO. \nSimilarly, if any country provides subsidies for catching fish in an overfished stock, India \nwill be able to challenge such subsidies at the WTO.  \n(d):  As per notifications by WTO Members, the subsidy provided by developed countries to \ntheir fishing sector consists of: programmes  for support to acquisition of fishing vessels, \nvessel de -commissioning programmes, research and development, improving fishing \npractices, purchase of innovative gear technologies, and support for establishment of \nenvironmentally and economically sustainable fisheries. \n The Agreement is to discipline the subsidies on IUU and Overfished stocks rather than stop \nthe subsidies.   \n***** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |652", "t": "SCCL", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 652 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n                                               \nSCCL \n  \n652.   SHRI UTTAM KUMAR REDDY \nNALAMADA:    \n             \n            Will the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)         whether Singareni Collieries Company Limited (SCCL) has put up any coal mines in \nNaini, Odisha up for auction; \n  \n(b)          If so, the capacity of these mines; \n  \n(c)        whether the qualification requirements for th e bidders is the same as issued by the Coal \nMinistry in similar such cases and if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(d)        the details along with the number and names of bidders who have submitted bids for this \nventure; \n  \n(e)        whether SCCL has taken all  measures to allow maximum possible competitors to take \npart in the bidding process; and \n  \n(f)     whether SCCL has permitted joint ventures like Western Coal Fields Limited or Mahanadi \nCoal Limited or Central Coalfields Limited and if so, the details thereof? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF COAL, MINES & PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a)       No Sir. However, The Singareni Collieries Company Limited (SCCL) has floated enquiry \nfor selection of Mine Developer cum Operator (MDO) for the Naini Coal Mine in the State of \nOdisha. \n  \n(b)          The capacity of the Naini coal mine is 10 MTPA. \n  \n(c)         As per the Allotment Agreement of Naini coal block, in case the coal mine is developed \nthrough contractor(s) , the selection of the contractor(s)  in relation to coal mining operation shall \nbe through a competitive bidding process and allottee has to ensure that the criteria for bidding \nare not linked to Coal India Ltd.’s notified price. \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \n  \n(d)       Total Five Firms submitted bids. The details of the bidders are as follows: \n  \n1. M/s. Adani Enterprises Limited, Ahmadabad, Gujarat \n2. M/s. Jindal Power Limited, Raigarh, Chhattisgarh \n3. M/s. Ambey Mining Private Limited, Kolkata \n4. M/s. Karamjeet Singh and Company Ltd., Delhi \n5. M/s. Durga Infra Mining Private Limited, Gujarat \n  \n(e)      SCCL is selecting MDO through competitive bidding process. Five firms have submitted \nbids for selection of MDO for Naini coal mine. \n  \n(f)       SCCL has intimated that they have permitted single bidder having MDO experience with \ncertain financial and technical credential. Further, the Bidder is allowed   to form SPV with other \nagencies with a stake up to 49%, retaining 51% stake with the Bidder at the time of Project \nAgreement. \n  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |653", "t": "Rubber Act", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 653 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20th JULY, 2022 \n \nRUBBER ACT \n \n653.  SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज ्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री) be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether any objections and suggestions have been received on the draft published \non repeal of Rubber Act, 1947 and enactment of Rubber (Promotion and \nDevelopment) Bill, 2022;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the last date to submit the receipt of objections/ \nsuggestions;  \n(c) whether the Government has received any objections/ suggestions from the state of \nKerala; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a) & (b):  522 stakeholders and public have submitted their suggestions on the draft ‘Rubber \n(Promotion and Development) Bill 2022’. Last date to receive the objections/suggestions was 9 th \nApril 2022. \n \n(c) & (d): Government has received suggestions from various stakeholders from the state of Kerala \nincluding Minister for Agriculture, Government of Kerala suggesting modifications in some \nprovisions of the draft Rubber (Promotion and Development) Bill, 2022.  Suggestions have been \nreceived regarding definitions, objectives, constitution of the Board, functions, provision relating \nto minimum and maximum price for rubber, declaration of Minimum Support Price, provisions \nfor declaring status of rubber as agriculture prod uce, request not to repeal the Rubber Act 1947, \netc. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |654", "t": "Yarn  Prices", "head": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 654 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \nYARN PRICES \n \n \n654. SHRI T.R. BAALU: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) whether the viability of the textile industry is under serious threat due to the recent exorbitant hike in \nGST and import duty on cotton and yarns;  \n \n(b) if so, the details including the reasons for such a steep increase in GST etc. on yarns;  \n \n(c) whether the al ready besieged textile industry fighting for survival in the aftermath of Covid -19 \npandemic, has called for a shutdown in important textile centres like Tiruppur; and  \n \n(d) if so, whether the Government will intervene immediately and ensure remedial measur es as \nrequested by the textile industry to revive the exports and save the livelihood of lakhs of weavers? \n \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) & (b): GST rates are notified on the recommendation of the GST Council, which consist s of \nUnion Finance Minister and Ministers as nominated by State Governments. The GST Council in its \n46th meeting has recommended to defer the decision to change the rates in textiles recommend ed in the \n45th GST Council meeting. Therefore, there is no change/ increase in GST rate on cotton and yarns. As \nregards import duty, in view of rising cotton prices and to ensure adequate domestic availability of raw \ncotton, the Government has exempted 10%  import duty (5% Basic Custom Duty & 5% Agriculture \nInfrastructure And Development Cess) on raw cotton till 31.10.2022. Furthermore, there has not been \nany increase in the import duty on yarns.  \n \n(c) & (d): There is no shut down reported after Covid -19 pan demic from the important textile \ncentres like Tiruppur. The continuous price increase in raw -cotton had led to increase in cotton yarn \nprice from September 2021 to March 2022 which affected downstream industry. The cotton prices have \nsoftened and in turn c otton yarn prices have also declined during last three months. After Covid -19 \npandemic the textile industry has bounced back to normal in 2021-22. \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |655", "t": "BSNL Network in Aspirational Districts", "head": "1 \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 655 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \n \nBSNL NETWORK IN ASPIRATIONAL DISTRICTS \n \n655.  SHRI. RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) network in Aspirational Districts in \nOdisha is very poor;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken by the Government in this regard;  \n \n(c)  the name of the places in the State of Odisha where new mobile towers are proposed to be \nset up by the Government; and  \n \n(d)  the details of the plans to increase the internet speed in the said region? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) & (b)  Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) monitors the performance of the \nTelecom Service Providers (TSPs) including Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) against the \nbenchmarks for various Quality of Service (QoS) parameters laid down by TRAI by way of QoS \nregulations issued from time to time, through Quarterly Performance Monitoring Reports (PMRs) \nreceived from Telecom Service Providers. As per PMR for quarter ending March, 2022, BSNL is \nmeeting, in general, all QoS parameters for its cellular and basic services in Odisha Licensed \nService Area that includes Aspirational districts in the state. \n  \n  \n\n2 \n \n(c) In Odisha state, there are 2379 mobile towers planned to cover 3933 Villages acros s ten \nAspirational Districts of Odisha under Aspirational District scheme. Additionally, the 483 mobile \ntowers envisaged under LWE-II scheme of Universal Service Obligation Fund(USOF) also include \nAspirational districts in the state. District wise details are as follows:  \n \nDistrict wise details of planned 4G towers under Aspirational District Scheme \nS.No. State District \nNo. of villages to be \ncovered in scheme \nNo. of Mobile towers \nPlanned \n1 ODISHA Balangir 11 11 \n2 ODISHA Dhenkanal 11 11 \n3 ODISHA Gajapati 467 264 \n4 ODISHA Kalahandi 516 318 \n5 ODISHA Kandhamal 1094 643 \n6 ODISHA Koraput 520 342 \n7 ODISHA Malkangiri 306 196 \n8 ODISHA Nabarangapur 17 14 \n9 ODISHA Nuapada 29 26 \n10 ODISHA Rayagada 962 554 \n    Total 3933 2379 \n \nDistrict wise details of planned 4G towers under LWE-II scheme  \nS.No. State District No. of villages to be \ncovered in scheme \n1 ODISHA Kalahandi 66 \n2 ODISHA Kandhamal 96 \n3 ODISHA Koraput 85 \n4 ODISHA Malkangiri 128 \n5 ODISHA Nawarangapur 22 \n6 ODISHA Nuapada 49 \n7 ODISHA Rayagada 33 \n8 Odisha Boudh 2 \n9 ODISHA Baragarh 2 \n   Total 483 \n \n(d)  BharatNet project is being implemented in a phased manner through various implementing \nagencies to provide broadband connectivity to all Gram Panchayats(GPs) in the country including \nOdisha. The scope of BharatNet has been recently extended to cover all inhabited villages beyond \nGPs in the country, with the timeline as year 2025.  \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |656", "t": "Auction of Coal Mines", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 656 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n  \nAuction of Coal Mines \n  \n656.   SHRI KALYAN BANERJEE: \n  \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has planned for auction/allocation of coal blocks \ntill 2030; \n(b) if so, the details of overall auction/allocation schedule along with the \nenvisaged timeline of its operation; \n(c) the details of the progress made by Coal India Ltd to achieve  its one billion \ntonne production target by 2023-24; and \n(d) the details of auctioned coal blocks in the country since 2014? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF COAL, MINES & PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a) & (b) : Auction of coal blocks is a continuou s process. As per the prescribed \nprocedure, companies may apply as and when Notice Inviting \nApplication/Notice Inviting Tender is published by the Nominated \nAuthority/Ministry of Coal.  Applications, if any, are considered as per the \nnorms specified. \n  \n(c) : To achieve 1 BT coal production by FY 2024-25, the progress made so far \nby Coal India Ltd. (CIL) since April’ 2020 to June’ 2022 is as follows: \n  \n1. 53 project reports including greenfield and expansion projects have been \napproved by CIL &  its subsidiaries to create additional capacity of coal \nproduction. These projects will add additional capacity of about 278 Mty \n(million ton/ year) and projected to contribute additional production of about \n102 Mt by FY 24-25. \n2. Environmental Clearance g ranted for 60 proposals with an incremental \ncapacity of 104.86 Mty. \n3. Stage-I Forest Clearance granted for 09 proposals involving 934.96 Ha of \nforest land. \n4. Stage-II Forest Clearance granted for 14 proposals involving 2580.68 Ha \nof forest land. \n5. 6966.87 Ha of land have been possessed. \n6. 06 First Mile Connectivity Projects of CHP/SILO have been commissioned \nwith aggregate capacity of 82 Mty. \n\n2 \n \n7. 04 Rail Connectivity projects have been commissioned and 1 Rail Siding \nhave been commissioned. \n8. Rail Projects - Kharsia- Dharamjaigarh rail line of 87 Kms (CERL Ph -I) in \nChhattisgarh has been commissioned, Lingaraj SILO to Deulbeda rail \nconnectivity of 4.4 Km has been commissioned, Work of tripling of Tori - \nShivpur rail link in Jharkhand started and Doubling of  Jharsuguda- Barpali- \nSardega rail link in Odisha started. \n9. Work awarded for 06 MDO Projects with aggregate capacity of 96.74 Mty. \n  \n(d) : Details of coal mines Auctioned by Nominated Authority whose allocation \nis in force presently is given below:- \n  \nS. \nNo. \nName of Coal Mine State Successful Bidder / \nAllottee \nEnd \nUse \nVesting \norder/ \nAllotment \norder date \n1 Chotia Chhattisgarh Bharat Aluminium \nCompany Ltd. \nNRS 23-03-2015 \n2 Gare Palma IV/4 Chhattisgarh Hindalco Industries \nL