���������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�\n�\n\n�\n���������������� ��������������������������� ������������ ���� ���������� ��� ������ ��� ���� ��������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ��� ���������� �������� ��� ��������� �� ����� ��� ��������� ���� ����� ����������� ����������\n�������� ������� ������ ������� ������ ������� ������������ �������� ������� ������\n������������������������������������������������������������������ ��������������������\n��� ������ ����� ����� ����� ����������� ���� ������ ���������� �������� ������� �������� ������\n������� ��������� ��������� ����������� ���������� ������� �������� ���������� ������\n���������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ��������� ������ ������ ��������� ������ ����� ������� ����������� ��� ���� ��������� �����\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������� ��� ��������� �������� ��������������� ��������� ��� �� ����������� ������������������������� ������������\n�������������������� ���������� �������������� ���������� �������������� ������ �������� ������������������ �������� ���������������� ������������������ ������ �������������� ����������������������\n���������� ������������ �������������������������� ������������ ������������������ ������ ���� �������������� ��������� ��� ����������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ������ ���� ������������� ��� ������� ���� ������� ���� ������������ ������������ ����\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |670", "t": "Railway Projects in Bihar", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 670 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nRAILWAY PROJECTS IN BIHAR \n \n670.  DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the  details of railway projects in Bihar for which  survey has been \nconducted and approved but work is not underway as yet; and \n \n(b) the steps taken/being taken by the Government  to speed up the work \nof approved railway projects in Bihar? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) and (b): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.   \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFE RRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND  (b) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 670 BY DR. SANJAY JAISWAL  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2022 REGARDING RAILWAY \nPROJECTS IN BIHAR \n \n(a) and (b): Railway projects are sanctioned Zonal Railway wise and not \nState-wise/distrtict-wise as Indian Railways’ network spans across \nvarious State boundarie s.  However, as on 01.04.2022, 52 Railway \nprojects (32 new lines, 04 gauge conversions and 16 doublings), costing \n₹66,597 crore, covering total length of 5,004 Km falling fully/partly in \nthe State of Bihar are in different stages of planning/approval/execu tion, \nout of which 1,240 Km length has been commissioned and an \nexpenditure of ₹21,038 crore has been incurred upto March, 2022. \n \nProjects in the State of Bihar are covered by East Central Railway \n(ECR), Eastern Railway (ER), North Eastern Railway (NER) and Northeast \nFrontier Railway (NFR) Zones of Indian Railways.  The project -wise \ndetails including cost, expenditure and outlay are available in public \ndomain on Indian Railways website i.e. www.indianrailways.gov.in> \nMinistry of Railways> Railway Board > About Indian Railways> Railway \nBoard Directorates> Finance (Budget)> Pink Book (year) >Railways-wise \nWorks, Machinery & Rolling Stock Programme (RSP). \n \nSince 2014, there has been substantial increase in Budget allocation \nand commensurate commissioning of projects. Average Annual Budget \nallocation for Infrastructure projects & safety works, falling fully/ partly \nin State of Bihar, during 2014-19 has been enhanced to ₹3,061 crore per \nyear from  ₹1,132 crore per year during 2009 -14, which is 170% more \nthan Average Annual Budget allocation during 2009 -14. These \nallocations have been increased to ₹4,093 crore in Financial Year 2019 -\n\n20 (262% more than the Average Annual Budget allocation of 2009 -14), \n₹4,489 crore in Financial Year 2020 -21 (297% more than the Average \nAnnual Budget allocation of 2009-14) and ₹5,560 crore in Financial Year \n2021-22 (391 % more than the Average Annual Budget Outlay of 2009 -\n14). For Financial Year 2022 -23, highest ever Budget outlay of ₹6,606 \ncrore has been provided for these projects, which is 484% more than \nthe Average Annual Budget Outlay of 2009-14 (₹1,132 crore per year).  \n \nDuring 2014 -22, 1096 Km length (317 Km New Lines, 425 Km Gauge \nConversion and 354 Km Doubling) projects falling fully/partly in the \nState of Bihar have been commissioned at an average rate 137 Km/year, \nwhich is 115% more than the commissioning during  2009-14 (63.6 \nKm/year Km).  \n \nVarious steps being taken by the Government for effective and speedy \nimplementation of rail projects include (i) setting up of Gati Shakti \nDirectorate in the Ministry and Gati Shakti units in field, (ii)  \nprioritisation of projects, (iii) substantial increase in allocation of funds, \n(iv) delegation of powers at field level, (v) close monitoring of progress \nof project at various levels (vi) regular follow up with State \nGovernments and concerned authorities for expeditious land \nacquisition, forestry and Wildlife clearances and for resolving other \nissues pertaining to projects.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |671", "t": "Construction of Platforms", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.671 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF PLATFORMS \n \n \n†671. SHRI PRADEEP KUMAR CHAUDHARY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state : \n \n(a) the details of the targets achieved by the Railways in comparison to \nthe targets for the year 2022-23;  \n \n(b) the schemes proposed by the Government for Western Uttar Pradesh \nfor the year 2022-23;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has any scheme for the cons truction of \nplatforms at the stations, where needed;  \n \n(d) if so, whether Nanauta Railway Station near Saharanpur has been \nincluded in this scheme; and \n \n (e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.671 BY SHRI PRADEEP KUMAR CHAUDHARY \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2022 REG ARDING \nCONSTRUCTION OF PLATFORMS \n \n \n(a)   The target set for construction of platforms on Indian Railways for \nthe year 2022 -23 is 367, out of which 110 numbers of platforms have \nbeen constructed till June, 2022. \n \n(b)  Ministry of Railways does not make any developmental \nplans/schemes State-wise but zone wise.  However, for construction of \nplatforms and raising of the level of low/medium level platforms to High \nlevel platforms, Ministry of Railways have sanctioned Um brella works of \n` 1755/- crore for “Provision of Foot over Bridges and/or High -level \nPlatforms at stations”.  \n \n(c) Indian Railways reviews the requirement of additional platforms as \nwell as extension of platforms with growth in volume of traffic and other \nvarious relevant factors. For raising of platform levels, priority is \naccorded to a higher category station over a lower category station \nwhile sanctioning the work.   \n \n(d) and (e) Nanauta station is a Non -Suburban Grade-6 category Railway \nstation. The Railway station has 01  rail level platform, which is planned \nto be raised to high level according to the priority assigned to it under \nthe said policy. Moreover, sanction of the work depends upon the \nrelative priority of similar works proposed for various s tations and the \navailability of resources and technical feasibility. \n \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |672", "t": "Performance Audit on Functioning of UIDAI", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 672 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nPERFORMANCE AUDIT ON FUNCTIONING OF UIDAI \n \n672.  SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL: \n \nWill the Minister of  ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to \nstate: \n   \n(a) the status of Action Taken on the recommendations of CAG‟s Audit Report No. 24 of  \n2021 - (Meity) on “Performance Audit on Functioning of UIDAI” along with the details of \nrecommendations which have been implemented and those which are pending for \nimplementation;  \n \n(b) the expenditure incurred by UIDAI for issuance of Bal Aadhaars till 31 March 2022;  \n \n(c) whether UIDAI stands by the Supreme Court‟s 2018 Judgement on Aadhaar and \nproviding unconditional exemption to children and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) the number of Aadhaars which were cancelled by UIDAI for being duplicates along with \nthe steps taken to minimize, generating of multiple/duplicate Aadhaar numbers; and  \n \n(e) whether the Government has taken any steps to provide adequate facilities for Aadhaar \nenrolment, updating and retrieval and if so, the details thereof along with the expenditure \nincurred by UIDAI for providing these facilities till 31 March 2022. \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR   ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a):  Recommendations of the CAG‟s Audit Report No. 24 of 2021 -(MeitY) on “Performance \nAudit on Functioning of UIDAI” have been accepted for implementation. Action taken report \nhas been uploaded on Audit Para Monitoring System (APMS) (https://apms.nic.in/) . \n \n(b): Total expenditure incurred in the last three years on Aadhaar Enrolments & Updates are \nas follows: \n \n \n  \n  \n \n \n \nSince common infrastructure is used for generating Aadhaar for all age group, it is \ntechnically not feasible to re-generate the report as on date for a legacy period for expenditure \nincurred for issuance of Bal Aadhaar. \n \n(c): UIDAI has abided with the directions of the Hon‟ble Supreme Court in its judgment \ndated 26.09.2018, in the matter of Justice K.S Puttaswamy (Retd.) and Anr. Vs. UOI and Ors. \nThe necessary amendments have been made in the Aadhaar Act, 2016, by way of the \nAadhaar and Other Laws (Amendment) Act, 2019.  \n \nIn compliance of the aforesaid judgment, Section 3A (3) has been inserted in Aadhaar Act, \n2016 vide the Aadhaar and Other Laws (Amendment) Act, 2019 which provides that: \nBudget Head Expenditure Incurred (Rs. In Lakh) \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n113.04-E&U-OC Enrolment cost 14160.18 32009.82 51990.00 \n\n \n“(3) Notwithstanding anything in Section 7, a child shall not be denied any subsidy, benefit \nor service under that section in case of failure to establish his identity by undergoing \nauthentication, or furnishing proof of possession of Aadhaar number, or in the case of a child \nto whom no Aadhaar has been assigned, producing an application for enrolment” \n \n(d):  Aadhaars cancelled for being duplicate and for other reasons as of 31st  May 2022 are \n5,98,999. \nUIDAI has taken adequate steps to address duplicate/ multiple Aadhaar generation issue a nd \nregular efforts are made to upgrade systems/ procedures.Thedemographic matching \nmechanism has been further strengthened, biometric matching of all new enrolments is \nensured and„Face‟ has been included as a new modality (in addition to finger print and i ris) \nfor de-duplication. \n \n(e): Yes, Sir.  UIDAI through its Registrars ensures provision for Aadhaar enrollment/update \nto residents. As on 30th June 2022, more than 57000 Aadhaar centres are active across the \ncountry.  In addition to the above, around 34,500 Tablet/Mobile based machines are  also \nfunctional in the field which is used for updating mobile number/email id in Aadhaar and to \nprovide facility for Aadhaar enrolment for children under the age group of 0 -5. UIDAI also \npermits residents to update their demographic details such as Name (minor change), Date of \nBirth, Gender and Address through online myAadhaar portal.  \n \nWith regard to provisions available for retrieval of Aadhaar, residents can retrieve their lost \nEID/UID number by filling the required details on UIDAI myAadhaar portal under the link:  \nhttps://myaadhaar.uidai.gov.in/retrieve-eid-uid.  \n \nResidents can also retrieve their lost EID/UID by visiting the nearest Aadhaar Enrolment \nCentre or by calling UIDAI call centre 1947. For retrieving the EID/UID, residents are to \nprovide their demographic details properly.  \n \nThe expenditure incurred in the last three years on Aadhaar Enrolments & Updates are as \nfollows: \n \nBudget Head Expenditure Incurred (Rs. In Lakh) \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n113.04-E&U-OC Enrolment cost 14160.18 32009.82 51990.00 \n \n******* \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |673", "t": "Gauge Conversion from Udaipur to Ahmedabad", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 673 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nGAUGE CONVERSION FROM UDAIPUR TO AHMEDABAD \n \n†673.  SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n   SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \n \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) whether the Government has completed the work  of rail gauge \nconversion from Udaipur in Rajasthan to  Ahmedabad via Himmatnagar \nin Rajasthan; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the total number and names  of railway stations \nbetween Udaipur and Ahmedabad; \n \n(c) the details of the passenger/freight and express  trains on this \nroute; \n \n(d) the details of the names of the stations falling  into A and B \ncategories among these stations; \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes to build more  new stations on \nthis railway route and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(f) the reasons for the delay in the said work of  gauge conversion that \nstarted in 2009 along with the details thereof; \n \n(g) the total funds allocated and spent on this project so far; and \n \n(h) the time by which the Government proposes to  start the train \noperation on Udaipur to Ahmedabad via  Himmatnagar railway line \nafter this gauge conversion work is over? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (h): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*****  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (h) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 673 BY SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI \nAND SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON \n20.07.2022 REGARDING GAUGE CONVERSION FROM UDAIPUR TO \nAHMEDABAD \n(a) and (g): Ahmedabad -Himmatnagar-Udaipur gauge conversion \nproject including Modasa Shamlaji Road new line (318 km) has been \ntaken up. Gauge conversion of Ahmedabad -Himmatnagar-Dungarpur-\nJay Samand (234 km) and Kharwachanda -Udaipur (24 km) sections \nhas been completed and work has been taken up in balance section.  \n \nSo far , total fund allo cated and spent on Ahmedabad -Himmatnagar-\nUdaipur gauge conversion project including Modasa -Shamlaji Road \nnew line (318 km) is  `  3335.33 and ` 2136.44 crore respectively.  \n \n(b) and (d): There are total 36 stations between Udaipur and \nAhmedabad section, name ly Asarva, Saijpur (Halt), Sadargram (Halt), \nNaroda, Medra (Halt), Dabhoda, Nandol Dehgam, Jaliyamath (Halt), \nRalciyal, Kherola, Talod, Khari -Amarpur, Prantij, Sonasan, Hapa \nRoad, Himmatnagar, Umra, Kharwachanda, Zawar, Padla, Jaisamand \nRoad, Surkhand ka k hera, Semari, Kundalgarh, Rikhab dev road, \nKotana, Dungarpur, Shalashah thana, Shri Bhavnath, Bechhiwara, \nJagabor, Lusadiya, Shamlaji Road, Sunak, Raigadh Road and \nViravada. \n \n \n(c): To cater to the needs of passengers of Ahmedabad -Himmatnagar-\nDungarpur section, 01 pair of Passenger Special service viz. 09543/44 \nAsarva-Dungarpur DMU special is being operated since 17.01.2022.  \n \nHowever, no direct Passenger service or Mail/Express train service is \nbeing operated between Ahmedabad and Udaipur via Asarva -\nHimmatnagar-Dungarpur section, at present.  \nContd…2/- \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \nIn last three months i.e. April, May and June 2022, total 09 freight \ntrains have run on Ahmedabad -Himmatnagar-Dungarpur section.  \n \n(e): Work has been taken up as per approved plan. There is no such \nproposal to build more stations on Ahmedabad -Himmatnagar-Udaipur \nsection presently.  \n \n(f) To avoid sharp curves and speed restriction in the existing meter \ngaure section between Umra and Jaw ar stations, the alignment of \nthis section was detoured for 12.5 km. For this detour alignment, \n35.78 hectare forest land and construction of 820 metre long tunnel \nwas required. Land acquisition was completed in January, 2018.  \n \nHowever, timely completion o f any Railway project depends on \nvarious factors like quick land acquisition by State Government, \nforest clearance by officials of forest department, shifting of \ninfringing utilities, statutory clearances from various authorities, \ngeological and topographi cal conditions of area, law and order \nsituation in the area of project site, number of working months in a \nyear for particular project site due to climatic conditions, priority of \nprojects, etc. and all these factors affect the completion of the \nproject. \n \n(h): Introduction of trains on a new section is subject to mandatory \nsanction of Commissioner of Railway  Safety. Besides, introduction of \nnew train services is an ongoing process on Indian Railways.  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |674", "t": "National Handloom Development Programme", "head": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 674 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \nNATIONAL HANDLOOM DEVLOPMENT PROGRAMME \n \n \n674. SHRI GIRISH CHANDRA: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) the details of the funds allocated to various States for the implementation of all the schemes under \nNational Handloom Development Programme (NHDP) during the last three years, State-wise?  \n \n(b) the percentage of the funds allocated which has been utilised fo r the beneficiaries during the last \nthree years;  \n \n(c) the State-wise details of the number of beneficiaries who have been benefitted from these schemes? \nand  \n \n(d) whether the government is considering any new schemes under this programme and if so, the de tails \nthereof? \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) to (d): Ministry of Textiles is implementing National Handloom Development Programme from \n2021-22 to 2025- 26 with merger of  two schemes viz. Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development \nScheme and Handloom Weavers Comprehensive Welfare Scheme . Under this scheme, funds are not \nallocated State-wise. However, funds are released based on the proposals received from the States/UTs.  \nAlso, 2 nd instalment under the scheme is released after utilisation of 70% of funds released in               \n1st instalment. State-wise detail of funds released during last three years is annexed.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure \nStatement referred to in reply of part s (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No.674 to be answered on 20.07.2022 \n \nState/UT-wise detail of funds released during last three years \n \nS. No. State Funds released (Rs.in lakh) \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 2,216.73 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh 191.10 \n3.  Assam 1,680.52 \n4.  Bihar 491.44 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 376.99 \n6.  Gujarat 165.21 \n7.  Haryana 6.60 \n8.  Himachal Pradesh 72.98 \n9.  Jammu & Kashmir 361.11 \n10.  Jharkhand 475.27 \n11.  Karnataka 682.25 \n12.  Kerala 487.24 \n13.  Ladakh 6.65 \n14.  Madhya Pradesh 321.75 \n15.  Maharashtra 245.64 \n16.  Manipur 1,197.12 \n17.  Mizoram 782.86 \n18.  Nagaland 386.63 \n19.  Odisha 1,009.83 \n20.  Punjab 24.14 \n21.  Rajasthan 59.10 \n22.  Sikkim 126.58 \n23.  Tamil Nadu 753.74 \n24.  Telangana 570.19 \n25.  Tripura 469.60 \n26.  Uttar Pradesh 734.98 \n27.  Uttarakhand 64.12 \n28.  West Bengal 353.24 \n TOTAL 14,313.61 \n*** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |675", "t": "Digital outreach across the country", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 675 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \nDIGITAL OUTREACH ACROSS THE COUNTRY \n \n†675.  SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the private companies are reluctant to invest in rural India due to which the \ncondition of broadband penetration in rural areas is very poor;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and if not, the remedial measures taken by the Government in this \nregard;  \n \n(c)  the details of the measures taken by the Government for deeper digital outreach in urban \nand rural areas in the country;  \n \n(d)  whether any funds have been earmarked for providing in ternet broadband services \nseparately in urban and rural areas during the 12th Five Year Plan and if so, the details thereof; \nand  \n \n(e)  whether the Government proposes to improve the penetration of internet in rural areas \nespecially in Rajasthan and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) To (c)  As per latest “The Indian Telecom Services Performance Indicators” reports \nreleased by Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) dated 10 th January, 2022, internet \nsubscribers in rural and urban areas of the country are 336.60 million and 497.69 million \nrespectively at the end of September, 2021. \n Following steps have been taken by the Government for enhancing digital outreach in the \ncountry: \n(i) Mobile/Broadband/Internet services are provided in a phased manner by the \nGovernment and Telecom Services Provider (TSPs) in the uncovered villages of the \ncountry. The Government, with funding from Universal Service Obligation Fund \n(USOF), has schemes nam ely Comprehensive Telecom Development Plan for North \nEastern Region, Left Wing Extremism (LWE) Affected Area schemes, Aspirational \nDistricts Schemes, Comprehensive Telecom Development Plan for Islands, etc. to \n\n2 \n \nextend mobile connectivity through setting up of towers in the uncovered villages of \nthe country. \n(ii) BharatNet project is implemented in a phased manner to provide broadband \nconnectivity to all Gram Panchayats (GPs) in the country. The infrastructure created \nunder BharatNet project can be utilized by Ser vice providers, for provisioning of \nbroadband/internet services. The scope of BharatNet has been recently extended up to \nall inhabited villages beyond GPs in the country. \n     National Broadband Mission (NBM) was launched on 17th December 2019 with a \nvision to fast-track growth of digital communications infrastructure, bridge the digital \ndivide, facilitate digital empowerment and inclusion, and provide affordable and \nuniversal access of broadband for all. \nTo enhance proliferation of broadband services throu gh public Wifi, Prime Minister \nWireless Access Network Interface (PM-WANI) framework has been introduced.  The \nPM-WANI framework enables provision of broadband through a distributed \narchitecture and unbundling of functions. Under this framework entities ar e neither \nrequired to take any license nor pay any fee to the Government. The details of the same \nare available at website pmwani.gov.in \n(d) & (e)   Licensed Service providers provide broadband services in urban and rural areas of \nthe country as per their business plans. However, for expansion of broadband services in rural and \nremote areas of the country, there are various Government schemes with the support from \nUniversal Service Obligation Fund (USOF) under which total fund earmarked/disbursed for last 5 \nyears and current year till February, 2022 is about 31529 Cr. \n In addition to BharatNet project in Rajasthan, the Government has following schemes \nunder USOF for enhancing internet/broadband in the state: \n(i) Provision of mobile connectivity in 354 uncovered villages, Border area & other \npriority areas of various states including Rajasthan. \n(ii)  Provision of 4G mobile connectivity in 502 uncovered villages of Aspirational \nDistricts in four States including Rajasthan. \n \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |677", "t": "Restoration of Train Services in Kerala", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 677 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nRESTORATION OF TRAIN SERVICES IN KERALA \n677. SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN: \n  \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has restarted all the train services as in \npre-Covid situation;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(c) the details of train services that have been restarted as regular \ntrains after Covid restrictions;  \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to restore the dereservation \nfacility in trains and if so, the details thereof and the proposed date of \nrestoration of the dereservation facility in each train passing through \nKollam railway station; \n \n (e) whether the Government proposes to convert all trains into regular \ntrains after its restoration post Covid and if so, the details of trains \nproposed to be converted into regular trains from Kollam; and  \n \n(f) the details of stoppages proposed to be reinstated in Kollam \nconstituency? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW)  \n \n(a) to (f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*****  \n\nSTATEMENT  REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) of UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 677 BY SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 20.07.2022 REGARDING RESTORATION OF TRAIN SERVICES \nIN KERALA \n(a) to (f) T o contain the spread of COVID - 19 pandemic, Indian Railways had \ndiscontinued all regular passenger carrying trains w.e.f. 23rd March, 2020. \nSince November 2021, Mail/Express trains are being operated with regular \nnumber and as per rationalized time table.   As on 14.07.2022, on a daily \naverage basis, 9754 trains which includes 1867 Mail/Express, 5651 suburban \ntrains and 2236 passenger trains were operated over Indian Railways. \nBesides, passenger train services are being restored in a phased manner, \nsubject to operational feasibility and commercial justification. Further,  \nprovision / rationalization of stoppages is an ongoing process on Indian \nRailways subject to operational feasibility, commercial viability etc. \nFurther, with effect from 30.06.2022, unreserved coaches have been restored \nin all Mail/Express trains passing through Kollam station as per applicability \nduring Pre Pandemic Period. \nApart from above, it has also been decided to de -reserve sleeper class \ncoaches in the following trains:- \nSl. Train No. Details of \nCoaches  \nde-reserved \nSections          \nde-reserved \nw.e.f \n1 16382 Kanniyakumari-\nPune Express \nS5 \n \nKanniyakumari-\nErnakulam \n15.10.2022 \nS6 Kanniyakumari-\nPalakkad \n15.10.2022 \n2 12624 \nThiruvananthapuram-\nMGR Chennai Central \nExpress \nS7 Thiruvananthapu\nram-Ernakulam \n15.10.2022 \n3 16629Thiruvananthapu\nram-Mangaluru Central \nMalabar Express \nS8 Thiruvananthapu\nram-Kottayam \n19.10.2022 \n4 16630 Mangaluru \nCentral- \nThiruvananthapuram \nMalabar Express \nS5, S6 Kottayam- \nThiruvananthapu\nram \n19.10.2022 \n\n5 16723 Chennai \nEgmore-Kollam \nAnanthapuri Exprss \nS10, S11 Tirunelveli-\nKollam \n19.10.2022 \n6 16724 Kollam-Chennai \nEgmore Ananthapuri \nExpress \nS11 Kollam- \nTirunelveli \n20.10.2022 \n7 16525 Kanniyakumari- \nKSR Bengaluru \nExpress \nS6 Kanniyakumari-\nErnakulam \n07.08.2022 \nS7 Kanniyakumari-\nPalakkad \n07.08.2022 \n8 17229 \nThiruvananthapuram-\nSecunderabad Sabari \nExpress \nS11, S12 Thiruvananthapu\nram-Coimbatore \n14.10.2022 \n9 16346 \nThiruvananthapuram-\nLokmanya Tilak (T) \nExpress \nS8 Thiruvananthapu\nram-Ernakulam \n14.10.2022 \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |678", "t": "Ajmer-Sawai Madhopur Railway Project", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 678 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nAJMER-SAWAI MADHOPUR RAILWAY PROJECT \n \n†678.  SHRI BHAGIRATH CHOUDHARY: \n \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) whether Rajasthan Government has expressed its  inability to bear \n50 per cent cost and to provide land free of cost for 165 kilometer long \nAjmer-Sawai Madhopur  via Tonk railway project sanctioned in Rail \nBudget 2015-16 and has not signed the memorandum of Understanding \nfor the said project as a result of which the project has  been lying \npending till date; \n \n(b) if so, the year-wise details in this regard; \n \n(c) whether Railways has sanctioned projects on the  basis of gross \nbudgetary assistance in the country  during the last five years and if \nso, the year-wise details of the project; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to implement  the new rail \nproject Ajmer to Tonk via Sawai Madhopur  with gross budgetary \nsupport; and \n \n(e) if so, the time by which it is likely to be done if not, the reasons \ntherefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*****  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS  (a) TO (e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 678 BY SHRI BHAGIRATH CHOUDHARY  \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2022 REGARDING AJMER-\nSAWAI MADHOPUR RAILWAY PROJECT \n \n (a) to (e): Ajmer (Nasirabad) – Sawai Madhopur (ChauthKa Barwara) \nvia Tonk (165 km) new line project was included in the Budget 2015-16 \nwith the commitment from Government of Rajasthan to provide land \nfree of cost and to share 50% cost of the project. Accordingly, State \nGovernment was requested for funding 50% of the project cost and to \nprovide land free of cost. Government of Rajasthan has not agreed for \nproviding land free of cost and share 50% cost of project and \nexpressed their inability for cost sharing. \n \nSince Government of Rajasthan has expressed their inability in \ncost sharing of Ajmer (Nasir abad) – Sawai Madhopur (ChauthKa \nBarwara) via Tonk (165 km) new line project, Memorandum of \nUnderstanding (MoU) for the project has not been signed yet with \nGovernment of Rajasthan and therefore execution of project is held up \non this account. \n \nOn the request of Government of Rajasthan, Bankability study of \nproject was carried out by Railway. As per report Rate of Return of \nproject is (-) 7.85% with land cost and (-) 7.08% without land cost.   \n \nConsidering the huge shelf of ongoing projects and Rate of \nReturn (-)7.85% of the project, it is not possible for Railways to take \nup the Ajmer to Tonk via Sawai Madhopur new line project solely with \nGross Budgetary Support (GBS). \n \nPresently, 28 projects (11 new lines, 05 gauge conversion and 12 \ndoubling), costing ` 59,271 crore for 5,069 km length falling fully/partly \nin Rajasthan are under different stages of planning/approval/execution,  \nContd…2/- \n\n-2- \nout of which, 1,196 km length has been commissioned and an \nexpenditure of ` 11,207 Cr. has been incurred upto  March 2022. These \ninclude: \n \n 11 New Line Projects covering a length of 1,228 km at a cost of  \n` 16,942 crore, out of which 107 km length has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of ` 2,695 crore has been \nincurred upto March 22.  \n \n 05 Gauge Conversion Projects covering a length of 1,319 km at a \ncost of  ` 12,690 crore, out of which 654 km length has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of  ` 3,652 crore has been \nincurred upto March 22. \n \n 12 Doubling Projects covering a length of 2,522 km at a cost of   \n` 29,639 crore, out of which 434 km length has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of  ` 4,860 crore has been \nincurred upto March 22. \n \n Since 2014, there is substantial increase in Budget allocation for \ninfrastructure projects and commensurate commissioning.  Average \nannual Budget allocation on infrastructure projects and safety works, \nfalling fully/partly in Rajasthan during 2014 -19 has been increased to    \n` 2,951 crore per year from ` 682 crore per year during 2009 -14. Thus, \nthe average annual Budget allocat ion during 2014 -19 is 333% more \nthan average annual allocation of 2009 -14.  Budget allocation for \nthese projects in 2019 -20 has been increased to ` 4,686 crore (587% \nmore than average annual budget allocation of 2009 -14) and ` 4,582 \ncrore  in  financial   year  2020-21  ( 572%  more  than  average  annual  \n \nContd…3/- \n \n \n\n-3- \nBudget outlay of 2009 -14). Budget outlay of ` 6006 crore  has been \nprovided for these projects for financial year 2021 -22 which is 781% \nmore than average annual budget outlay during 2009 -14. Highest ever \nBudget outlay of ` 7565 crore has been provided for these projects for \nfinancial year 2022 -23 which is 1009% more than average annual \nbudget outlay during 2009-14. \nDuring 2014 -22, 2322 km sections (176 km New line, 771 km \nGauge conversi"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |679", "t": "Customer Service Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 679 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \nCUSTOMER SERVICE CENTRES \n \n679.     SHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: \n \nWill the Minister of  ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to \nstate:   \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to allow the Customer Service Centres of Public Sector \nBanks located in rural areas to issue Aadhaar cards for the convenience of the people of the \nrural areas in getting their Aadhaar card; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR   ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) and (b): Sir, no such proposal is under consideration. However, it is informed that UIDAI \nhas on-boarded Banks as Registrars to provide Aadhaar enrolment & update services to the \nresidents through their branches. As on 30th June 2022, around 11,700 Bank branches are \nproviding Aadhaar enrolment/ update service to the residents. \n  \nIn addition to the above, arou nd 10,000 Banking Correspondents functioning under CSC e -\nGovernance are providing demographic update service in Aadhaar. \n \n******** \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |68", "t": "Dual Degrees", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.68 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nDual Degrees \n   \n68. SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \nSHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of Indian Students from Tamil Nadu pursuing higher education in foreign \ncountries, country-wise,  \n(b) whether the Government has chalked out any  comprehensive policy to accommodate and \nsupport more students to pursue their higher education in the country  itself and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c) whether the Indian students can pursue dual  degrees conferred by Indian and foreign higher \neducation institutions separately and simultaneously and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the dual degree provision will be  applicable for online and distance learning  \nprogrammes too, and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(Dr. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a): The University Grants Commission (UGC) has informed that they do not have database of \nstudents pursing higher studies in foreign countries.  \n \n(b): The UGC has initiated several measures for internationalization of higher education system \nin India. In order to foster academic collaboration between Indian Higher Educational Institutions \n(HEIs) and fo reign HEIs, “University Grants Commission (Academic Collaboration between \nIndian and Foreign Higher Educational Institutions to offer Twinning, Joint Degree and Dual \nDegree Programmes) Regulations, 2022” have been notified on 2nd May, 2022.  \n \n(c): Under the provisions of the aforementioned Regulations, “Dual Degree Programme” shall be \na programme jointly designed and offered by the Indian and Foreign Higher Educational \nInstitutions in the same disciplines/subject areas and in the same level. These Regulatio ns are \navailable at https://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/4555806_UGC-Acad-Collab-Regulations.pdf . \n (d) & (e):  The UGC has informed that under the provisions of the aforementioned UGC \nRegulations, the Credits to be earned by the Indian students from the foreign institution and \ncredits earned by the foreign students from Indian institutions shall be obtained throug h \nconventional mode programmes.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |680", "t": "Schemes for Aspirational Districts in Madhya Pradesh", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 680 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nSCHEMES FOR ASPIRATIONAL DISTRICTS \n \n  †680   SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any special grant is being provided in tribal districts of Madhya Pradesh by Niti Aayog; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether any new scheme has been approved by Niti Aayog for aspirational districts; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether any special scheme is under consideration for Barmani district of Khargaun- \n \n(e) Barwani Parliamentary constituency which comes under the category of aspirational district; \n \n(f)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n      (g) the places in Madhya Pradesh for which new projects are under consideration of Niti Aayog; and \n \n(h)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS & PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF \nPLANNING AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \n (RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n(a) and (b) No. NITI Aayog does not provide special grants for tribal districts  in Madhya Pradesh. \nAs the main strategy of the programme is based on convergence of existing schemes which have \ntheir own funding arrangement, large infusion of additional fund is not envisaged. However, in \norder to foster a competitive spirit, and for addressing the critical ga ps, additional allocation on the \nbasis of performance is also envisaged. Every month, districts are assessed on the basis of monthly \nprogress and the best performing Districts in overall terms and in each of the five sectors are \nidentified. The first and s econd rankers in overall terms are awarded ₹10 crores and ₹5 crores \nrespectively. The first ranker from each of the five sectors is awarded ₹3 crores each. Under this \narrangement, the Aspirational Districts of Madhya Pradesh (Barwani, Rajgarh, Chhatarpur, Damoh, \nSingrauli, Khandwa, Vidisha, Guna) have so far been sanctioned a total of ₹60.95 crores. \n \n\n2 \n \n(c) and (d) The Aspirational Districts Programme is over and above the existing schemes of the Central \nand the State Governments concerned and as such it is in the  nature of additionality. The broad \nstrategy of the Aspirational Districts Programme rests on the 3 Cs – Convergence (between Central \nand State Schemes), Collaboration (between Centre, State, District Administration, Development \nPartners and Citizens) and Competition (between Districts).  NITI Aayog does not run any new \nscheme in Aspirational Districts.  \n(d) and (f) As stated in (c) and (d) above, NITI Aayog does not run any scheme in Aspirational \nDistricts.  \n \n(g) and (h) As stated in (a) and (b) above, every month, districts are assessed on the basis of monthly \nprogress and the best performing Districts in overall terms and in each of the five sectors are \nidentified and provided performance based grants. Based on the performance grant, it is the districts  \nwhich undertake specific projects to address critical need felt by them. Details of the projects \nundertaken by the Aspirational Districts of Madhya Pradesh is provided at Annexure. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n3 \n \nAnnexure \n \nDetails of projects under various stages of implementation in the Aspirational Districts of  \nMadhya Pradesh  \n \nDistrict  Projects   \nChhatarpur  \nUpgradation of Health Infrastructure  \nSmart Classes for 60 high schools  \nSkill development and capacity building through Industrial \nTraining Institutes (ITI) in District  \nInstallation of solar power system in 74   govt schools having \nsmart classes  \nReconceptualising Pre -Primary Education at Anganwadi \nCentres \nSkill development and capacity building through ongoing \nprojects on industrial training institutes  \nRejuvenation of Waterbodies \n \nDamoh  \nConstructions of 10 new Anganwadi buildings  \nEstablishment of Community Nutrition Hubs and Nutrition \nGardens  \nLivelihood improvement project through construction of two \ncheck dams  \nConstruction of Boundary wall in 6 schools  \nImproving School Infrastructure in order to improve \nattendance and enrollment   \nImprovement and Construction work in Health Facility of \nDistrict Hospital  \n \nBarwani  \nTransformation of 328 model schools  \nStrenghthening of ICDS and Health Services facilities  \nStrengthening of farming through techno -support and \neconomic empowerment of farmers. \nFinancial Empowerment of women through livelihood support \nby handloom unit \n \nVidisha  \nConstruction of 32 New Anganwadi Centre Buildings  \nTo improve institutional delivery by Expanding and \nStrengthening delivery points \n \nGuna  \nSafe and Organic Product Program  \nImproving ANC coverage through provision of conducive \nenvironment during antenatal checkups  \nUpgradation of Aaganwadis to ‘Anganwadi Pathshalas’   \nEnhancing the nutritional value of mid -day meal and \nempl"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |681", "t": "Construction of Underpasses in Uttar Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 681 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF UNDERPASSES IN UTTAR PRADESH \n \n†681. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR DUBEY: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of underpasses constructed in entire Uttar Pradesh \nduring the last ten years; \n(b) whether any technical shortcomings have been detected in the \nsaid underpasses and if so, the details thereof and the concrete \nsteps taken by the Government to rectify them; \n(c) whether traffic remains totally shut for six months in a year due \nto water logging in the underpasses constructed at places having \nhigh water level in Uttar Pradesh; \n(d) if so, whether the Government has any scheme to ensure traffic \nmovement throughout the year by rem oving the technical faults; \nand  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) & (b): 1016  underpasses  have been constructed in Uttar Pradesh \nduring the last ten years.  Total 143 underpasses have been indentified \nfor improvements,  out of which 70 Nos. have been attended by \n\nproviding cover shed,  grouting of joints, pumping arrangement to \npump out the acc umulated water and other drainage improvement \nworks etc.   For remaining 73 underpasses, improvement works are in \nprogress. \n- 2- \n \n(c): No, Sir. \n \n(d) & (e): Railways have taken several remedial measures to mitigate \nthe problem of water logging. Adequate dra inage arrangement has \nbeen made as integral part of planning of new Road Under Bridge \n(RUB)/Subways. In existing RUB/subways feasible remedial measures \nlike water flow diversion to nearest bridge and  nallahs, provision of \ncover shed on approach roads, pro vision of hump at entry to Road \nunder Bridge (RUB), provision of cross drains, sealing of joints etc. \nhave been made as per site suitability and requirements. In addition, \npumping arrangement has also been made for identified RUBs, to \ndrain out water expeditiously in case of emergency. \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |683", "t": "India's Share in Global Space Market", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n DEPARTMENT OF SPACE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 683 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, JULY 20, 2022 \nINDIA’S SHARE IN GLOBAL SPACE MARKET \n683.  SHRI RAMESH BIDHURI: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to transfer 10 in orbit \ncommunication satellites to New Space India Ltd. (NSIL) which \nis the commercial arm of Indian Space Research Organisation \n(ISRO); \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether the Government ha s taken any steps for increasing \nIndia’s Share in Global Space Market; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC \nGRIEVANCES & PENSIONS AND IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n****  \n(a) Yes, Sir. \n \n\n(b) 10 (Ten) in-orbit operational communication satellites viz. GSAT -\n8, GSAT -10, GSAT -12R (CMS -01), GSAT -14, GSAT -15, GSAT -16, \nGSAT-17, GSAT-18, GSAT-30 and GSAT -31 have been transferred \nfrom Government of India to M/s . New Space India Ltd (NSIL), a \nCPSE under Department of Space, at a written down value of \n`4697.60 crores against issue of equity to Government of India, \nwith 01.04.2021 as the effective date of transfer.  \n \nThe Board  of NSIL is authorized to price the tran sponder \ncapacity as per the global trends. NSIL shall  carry out the \nactivities related to offering and allocation of capacity, as per \nthe guidelines to be adopted by its Board. The transfer of \noperational satellites is part of the Space Sector reforms, aim ed \nat strengthening the role of NSIL in order to enhance the nation’s \nshare in global space economy. \n \n(c) & (d)  \nYes, Sir. Government has taken several steps to increase India’s \nshare in global space market, through the reforms undertaken in \n2020, which seek t o augment the space sector in the country with \ngreater participation of Non-Governmental Entities [NGEs].  \nThe Indian National Space Promotion & Authorization Centre [IN -\nSPACe] has been created as a single window agency to promote, \nhandhold and authorize t he activities of NGEs in the sector, thus \nproviding them with a level playing field.  \n\nAs a part of these reforms, NSIL has been mandated to task the \nbuilding of launch vehicle through Indian Industry and launch as \nper satellite customer requirements.  \nWith the operationalization of IN -SPACe, the progressively evolving \nregulatory environment, a greater private sector participation in \nend-to-end space activities is expected, which may result in \nincreased share of India in the global space economy. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |684", "t": "Supply of Coal to Power Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.684 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n  \nSupply of Coal to Power Plants \n  \n684. SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA: \n       \nWill the Minister of Coal be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether  the Government has run special trains for supply of coal to \npower plants in the beginning of the current year and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has chalked out any special policy to insure \nuninterrupted supply of coal in various states that are likely to be affected \nby flood during the coming rainy season and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the details of steps taken by the Government to improve the living \nstandard of workers engaged in coal mines; \n(d) whether the Government is wo rking on providing clean drinking water \nunder Jal Jeevan Mission to people living nearby coal mines; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES  \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n \n(a):  As per information received from Ministry of Railways informed that the \ntransportation of coal by Railways is through open wagons. During first \nquarter of 2022-23, Indian Railways has transported average 434 rakes per \nday (Domestic + Imported) to power sector which is about 31% more than \nthe corresponding period of the last year.  \n  \n(b): To address the issues of coal supplies to power sector, an Inter -\nMinisterial Sub Group comprising of representatives from Ministries of \nPower, Ministry of Coal, Ministry of Railways, CEA, CIL and SCCL meet  \nregularly to take various operational decisions to enhance supply of coal to \nthermal power plants as well as for meeting any contingent situations \nrelating to Power Sector including to alleviate critical coal stock position in \npower plants. In addition to  this, an Inter -Ministerial Committee (IMC) has \nbeen constituted comprising of Chairman, Railway Board, Secretary, \nMinistry of Coal, Secretary, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate \nChange and Secretary, Ministry of Power to monitor augmentation of c oal \nsupply and power generation capacity. Secretary, Ministry of New and \nRenewable Energy and Chairperson, CEA are co-opted as Special Invitees as \nand when required by the IMC.  Coal dispatch from the captive coal blocks \nis also being monitored regularly. \n\n  \nCoal India Limited, the largest supplier of coal in the country,   has \ndispatched 152.49 MT of coal to Power Sector in the first quarter of the \ncurrent fiscal surpassing all the previous highs of the same period and \nachieving a growth of 19% over last yea r same period. CIL has already \nstarted building stock at its railway sidings along with Good shed and \nprivate Washery sidings & ports (CIL sidings: 1.82 MT, Good Shed: 1.45 MT, \nPvt. Washery: 0.95 MT  & Port: 1.39 MT, Total: 5.61 MT) to facilitate \nadequate supply for power sector. As per Central Electricity Authority (CEA), \ncoal stock at the power plants has improved from the level of 25.6 MT as on \n31.03.2022 to 28.34 MT on 14.07.2022.  \n  \n(c):  In order to improve the living standard of the Coal Miners, Coal India \nLimited/Singareni Collieries Company Limited are paying utmost attention \ntowards welfare amenities/facilities (Statutory & Non -statutory) to all \nemployees including contractual workers working i n Coal Mines. The \namenities/facilities is being provided in Coal Mines areas are Housing, \nEducation, Medical, clean water, Sports & Recreational, Banking and post \noffices, Co-operative stores and credit societies, Child care leave for female \nemployees.  \n  \nCoal India Limited/Singareni Collieries Company Limited are also \nmaintaining various statutory welfare facilities for the coal mines such as \nCanteen, Rest Shelters etc. in accordance with the provision of the Mines \nAct 1952 and Rules and Regulations framed there-under,  \n  \n(d) & (e):  Since August, 2019, Government of India, in partnership with \nStates, is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) to make provision of \npotable tap water supply to every rural household of the country, including \nthose near coal mines, by 2024. The total estimated outlay of the mission is \nRs. 3.60 lakh Crore. \n  \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, out of 18.93 Crore rural \nhouseholds, 3.23 Crore (17%) households were reported to have tap water \nconnections. So far, more than 6.56 Crore (34%) rural households have been \nprovided with tap water connections in last thirty -five months. Thus, as of \ndate, out of 19.20 Crore rural households in the country, 9.79 Crore (51%) \nhouseholds are reported to have tap water supply in their homes.  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |685", "t": "Suspension of MPLAD Funds", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.685 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n  \nSUSPENSION OF MPLAD FUNDS \n  \n685. SHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV: \n           \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the total amount of MPLAD funds made unavailable/suspended by the \nGovernment during the financial year 2019-20, 2020-21 and 2021-22; \n(b) whether the Government has main tained any data about the \npurposes for which these funds have been utilized; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n(d) the amount of these funds that have remained unutilized; \n(e) whether the Government intends to spend MPLAD funds in each \nMember of Parliament constituency in proportion to the funds \nsuspended/ taken away; and  \n(f) if so, the details in this regard? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION, MINISTER OF \nSTATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS  (RAO \nINDERJIT SINGH) \n  \n(a)  For the FY 2019-20 the entire MPLADS fund was made available and \nnothing was suspended. For the FY 2020-21 the entire MPLADS fund of \nRs.5 Crore per Member of Parliament was suspended  and nothing was \nmade available. For the FY 2021 -22 a partial of MPLADS fund of Rs.3 \nCrore per Member of Parliament was suspended and rest of Rs. 2 crore \nper MP was made available. \n\n(b), (c) & (d) The above funds to the amount of Rs.8 Crore per Member \nof Parliament for the year 2020-21(full) and 2021-22(partial) were placed \nat the disposal of the Ministry of Finance for managing the health and \nadverse impacts of Covid -19 on the society. Hence,  the Ministry of \nStatistics and Programme Implementation has no data/details about the \npurposes for which these funds have been utilized or whether any part \nof the same has remained unutilised. \n(e) & (f) Question Does not arise. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |686", "t": "Double Decker Train between Anand Vihar and Gorakhpur", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 686 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nDOUBLE DECKER TRAIN BETWEEN ANAND VIHAR AND GORAKHPUR  \n \n \n †686. SHRI HARISH DWIVEDI : \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to run double decker train \nafresh from Anand Vihar and extend to Gorakhpur which earlier used \nto run between Anand Vihar to Lucknow;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the stoppages of the said train;  \n  \n(c)  whether the Government has fixed the timetable and fare for the \nproposed double decker train between Anand  Vihar and Gorakhpur; \nand  \n \n (d)  if so, the details thereof ? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW)  \n \n \n(a) At present, there is no proposal to extend 12583/12584 Lucknow -\nAnand Vihar (T) Double Decker Express upto Gorakhpur. \n \n(b)  to  (d) Do not arise. \n             ***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |687", "t": "Revival of Closed Textile Mills", "head": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 687 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \nREVIVAL OF CLOSED TEXTILE MILLS \n \n \n687. SHRIMATI NAVNEET RAVI RANA: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) whether the Government is considering to restart the Finlay Textile Mill in Amravati, Maharashtra \nwhich has been lying closed since Corona period and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government is formulating any scheme for the artisans/employees working in the said \nMill as they have neither got the salary/arrears nor there was any increase in their salary and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government  proposes to formulate any action plan/start any centre for skilled workers \nin entire Maharashtra including the Parliamentary Constituency of Amravati under the Handicrafts \nscheme and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) & (b):   Production activity in all the National Textile Corporation (NTC) mills including Finlay \nTextile Mill in Amravati, Maharashtra were suspended due to Covid-19 pandemic and nationwide \nlockdown imposed by the various State Governments from March, 2020.    Normal operations were \nresumed in some of the NTC mills including Finlay Textile Mill from January, 2021, which could not \nbe continued due to unavailabil ity of working capital and other financial constraints. In light of the \ncontinued poor economic viability of NTC mills, Ministry of Textiles is preparing an action plan for a \nway forward in the matter in consultation with NITI Aayog and Department of Publi c Enterprises, \nMinistry of Finance.   \n(c):  The Office of Development Commissioner (Handicrafts), Government of India extends benefits \nto the Handicraft artisans under “National Handicraft Development Programme (NHDP)” and \n“Handicrafts Cluster Development Scheme (CHCDS)” to provide sustainable live lihood opportunities \nto the artisans throughout the country including Maharashtra.  During the last three years, Rs. 647.44 \nlakhs was sanctioned benefitting 11,820 artisans in the State of Maharashtra.    \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |688", "t": "Digital Village Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 688 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 20.07.2022 \n \nDIGITAL VILLAGE SCHEME \n  \n688.  SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT: \nSHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \nSHRI BHOLANATH (B.P. SAROJ): \nSHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \n \nWill the Minister of ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether Digital Village Scheme introduced by the Government is working to its \ndesired objectives and if so, the details thereof including the salient fea tures of the \nscheme; \n(b) the details of funds approved, allocated and utilized so far in Uttar Pradesh and \nMaharashtra under the scheme, district -wise including Barabanki, Jaunpur and \nVaranasi districts of Uttar Pradesh and the progress made therein so far; \n(c) the details of the number of villages and districts identified under the scheme in Uttar \nPradesh, Maharashtra and Andhra Pradesh including Gokavaram block in East \nGodavari district; \n(d) the details of targets fixed and achievements made by the Govern ment including the \nservices proposed to be offered under the said scheme; \n(e) whether the Government is planning to link some more villages under the scheme and \nif so, the time by when these villages are likely to be linked and made fully digital \nwith identified services; and \n(f) whether this scheme is benefiting in the creation of job opportunities for the rural \npeople and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a): Yes, Sir. T he Digital Village project titled “Pilot Project - Implementation of Digital \nVillage” is working to its desired objectives. The Digital Village Project was initiated by \nGovernment of India (MeitY) on a pilot scale in 700 villages (One Gram Panchayat / village \nfrom each district of the State/ UT) on 31st October 2018 for the duration of 3 years. The \nlist of the 700 villages is at \nhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1ODney0gMJHrhaO57izoOIHymwXxY9_et/edit?usp\n=sharing&ouid=114104362247024536597&rtpof=true&sd=true). The implementation \nstatus of the Digital Village project is enclosed at Annexure-I.  \n \nThe salient features of the Digital Village Pilot project are: \n \n 700 Gram Panchayat/villages with atleast one village from each district of the State/UT \nare covered under the pilot implementation of the project.  \n Digital Seva Portal (DSP) of CSCs in villages across the country is being leveraged for \nthe delivery of services to the rural citizens.  \n Education Service: Courses offered under this project are as - Basic Computer Course \n(BCC), Course on Computer Concept (CCC), Tally – Kaushal Praman Patra \n Health Services – Tele-Health & Tele-Veterinary consultation  \n Financial Inclusion- Financial inclusion awareness programme   \n Solar panel powered street lights (8 Solar lights per village) \n\n Skill Development - skill development in - Automotive Technician, Handset Repairing, \nField Technician- Home Appliances, and Electrical Technician. \n Government to Citizen Services (G2C) - online registration for professional entrance \nexaminations, Pan Card Services, new Aadhaar enrolment, its updation/modification, \nonline registration for service selection board, online registration for public service \ncommission, etc. \n B2C Services – Mobile / DTH recharge, ticket booking (Rail/Air), etc. \n \n(b):  Under the Digital India Central Sector Scheme of MeitY, a total outlay of Rs 98.32 \nCrore is allocated for implementation of Digital Village Pilot project. The funds allocated for \nthe project implementation in the State(s) of Uttar Pradesh and Maharashtra is Rs. 7.46 crore \nand Rs. 3.398 crore (Rs. 9.99 lakh per village) respectively out of which a total of Rs. 4.91 \ncrore (in Uttar Pradesh) and Rs. 2.272 crore (in Maharashtra) has been utilized so far for \nimplementation of the project in Uttar Pradesh and Ma harashtra State(s) respectively.  The \ndistrict wise details for Uttar Pradesh and Maharashtra State(s) including Barabanki, Jaunpur \nand Varanasi districts of Uttar Pradesh are at Annexure-II. \n \n(c): The details of the number of villages and districts covere d under Digital Village Pilot \nproject in Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra and Andhra Pradesh including Gokavaram block in \nEast Godavari district, are at Annexure-III. \n \n(d): The details of targets and achievements made under Digital Village pilot project are at \nAnnexure-I. \n \n(e):  The project is in pilot phase of implementation in which 700 villages/Gram Panchayat \nwith at least one village from each district of the State/UT are covered. \n \n(f):  This scheme is benefiting in job opportunities for rural people through Skill development \nprogram. In the pilot phase, the current target and achievement for each skill course are as \nfollows: \n \nS.No. Skill Service Target (number) Achievement \n(number) \n1 Automotive Service Technician  1,400  1,400  \n2 Handset Repair Engineer Registration 1,400  1,400"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |689", "t": "e-Filing system", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS \n(DEPARTMENT OF ADMINISTRATIVE REFORMS & PUBLIC GRIEVANCES) \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 689 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022) \n \ne-FILING SYSTEM  \n \n†689. SHRI BALAK NATH:  \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether e -filing system is in force in all the Ministries of the Government, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, whether the Government proposes to implement the said \nsystem;  \n(b)  whether the Governme nt proposes to set up an e -filing tracking system for providing \ninformation regarding the status of the proposals related to development works in \nParliamentary constituencies submitted by Members of Parliament along with the action \ntaken by the concerned Ministries thereon; and  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES \nAND PENSIONS AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH) \n \n(a) to  (c):  As a part of Digital Secretariat, e -Office has bee n implemented in all \nMinistries/Departments in Government of India. Central Registry Units in the Ministries have \nalso been digitized. In addition several Departments like Ministry of Environment, Fo rest and \nClimate Change, Ministry of Finance, Department of Justice, Ministry of Corporate Affairs, \nDepartment of Administrative Reforms & Public Grievances provide facility to Citizens to \nsubmit their applications and grievances at portals.  The website of  the Members of Parliament \nLocal Area Development Scheme (MPLADS) has features to facilitate Hon’ble Members of \nParliament to make online recommendations of works and track progress thereon. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |69", "t": "Projects undertaken in North East", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *69 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nManagement and Control of Flood \n†*69  SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI  MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of project proposals pending or san ctioned by the Government for management and contro l of \nflood situation in parts of Bihar, Northern Odisha,  West Bengal, Madhya Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir and \nJharkhand due to Bagmati, Budhi Gandak, Lal Bakiya, Kosi, Tawi and Subarnarekha rivers; \n(b) whether the Government has conducted any study to control and manage flood situation in the said r ivers; \nand \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the recom mendations made in this regard and the reaction of the \nGovernment thereto? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI   \n \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*69 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 21.07.2022 REGAR DING “MANAGEMENT AND \nCONTROL OF FLOOD”.  \n(a)  Flood management, including erosion control, falls within the purview of the States. Flood management \nand anti-erosion schemes are formulated and impleme nted by concerned State Governments as per their \npriority. The Union Government supplements the effo rts of the States by providing technical guidance a nd \nalso promotional financial assistance for management of floods in critical areas. \n \nFlood protection and flood management measures are broadly classified as under- \n \n(i) Structural Measures - The structural measures f or flood control which bring relief to the flood pr one \nareas by reducing flood flows and thereby reducing the flood levels. \n \n(ii) Non-Structural Measures- Facilitating timely e vacuation of the people and shifting of their movab le \nproperty to safer grounds by having advance warning  of incoming flood through setting up a flood \nforecasting system. Discouraging creation of valuab le assets/settlement of the people in the areas sub ject to \nfrequent flooding i.e. enforcing flood plain zoning regulation. \n \nIntegrated flood approach aims at adopting judiciou s mix of structural and non-structural measures to \nprovide a reasonable degree of protection against flood damages at economic cost.. \n \nTo strengthen the structural measures of flood mana gement, Government of India had implemented during \nXI & XII Plan Flood Management Programme (FMP) for providing Central Assistance to States for works \nrelated to river management, flood control, anti-erosion, drainage development, anti-sea erosion, etc. which \nsubsequently continued as a component of \"Flood Man agement and Border Areas Programme\" (FMBAP) \nfor the period from 2017-18 to 2020-21 and has been  further extended up to September 2022. So far \nCentral Assistance amounting to Rs. 6,686.79 crore has been released to Union Territories/State \nGovernment under this programme since inception. Th e 415 projects completed projects under this \nprogramme have given protection to an area of aroun d 4.99 mha and protected a population of about 52.2 1 \nmillion.  The details of projects completed in the States of Bihar, Odisha, West Bengal, Jammu & Kashm ir \nand Jharkhand under FMBAP (no project has been implemented in the State of Madhya Pradesh) are- \n  \nSl. No State Projects in \nnumbers \nArea benefitted \n(Lakh Hectares) \nPopulation benefitted \n \n(In Lakh) \n1 \nBihar 42 28.67 223.46 \n2. \nJ&K 19 2.32 15.32 \n3. \nJharkhand 3 0.18 1.97 \n4. \nOdisha 66 1.94 11.54 \n5. \nWest Bengal 16 0.94 23.57 \n  \n\nFor non structural measures, Central Water Commissi on (CWC) is the nodal organisation entrusted with \nthe task of flood forecasting and early flood warnings in the country. Presently, CWC issues flood forecasts \nfor 331 forecasting stations (199 river level forec ast stations & 132 dam/ barrage inflow forecast sta tions). \nThese stations cover 20 major river basins in 23 St ates and 2 Union Territories. In order to provide m ore \nlead time to the local authorities to plan evacuati on of people & take other remedial measures, CWC ha s \nrecently developed basin wise flood forecasting mod el for 5 days' advance flood forecast advisory at \nidentified flood forecasting and inflow forecasting stations.  \n \n(b) & (c)  In order to formulate the strategy for flood manag ement works in the entire country and river \nmanagement activities and works in the border areas ,  a Committee was constituted by NITI Aayog under \nthe chairmanship of Vice Chairman, NITI Aayog with officials from various Departments/ Ministries of \nGovernment of India, experts from the field and Pri ncipal Secretaries from States of Jammu & Kashmir, \nUttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, Punjab, Assam, A runachal Pradesh, Tripura, Madhya Pradesh and \nKerala were included as the members of this Committ ee. The Final Report of the"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |690", "t": "Ease  of Doing Business", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 690. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 20TH JULY, 2022. \n \nEASE OF DOING BUSINESS \n \n690.  SHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                    वाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to launch the  next phase of Ease of Doing \nBusiness as announced in this year‘s budget; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the reforms that are likely to be undertaken; \n \n(c)  the estimated timeline for operationalisation of the next phase of Ease of Doing \nBusiness; \n \n(d)  whether the Government has undertaken any national and State-level analysis \nof Ease of Doing Business to formulate appropriate strategies; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्रालय में राज्य मंत्री (श्री सोम प्रकाश)   \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI SOM PARKASH) \n \n(a) to (c):  Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT) is \nspearheading the next phase of Ease of Doing Business which is aimed \nat reducing compliance burden on businesses and citizens. The exercise \nis an on -going effort, aimed at achieving a conducive regulatory \nenvironment. The key focus areas of the initiative are: \n  \n(i)  Simplification of procedures related to applications, renewals, \ninspections, filing records, etc., \n(ii)  Rationalization by repealing, amending or subsuming redundant \nlaws, \n(iii)  Digitization by creating online interfaces eliminating manual forms \nand records, and \n(iv)  Decriminalization of minor violations. \n  \n(d) & (e):    DPIIT holds regular meetings with C entral Ministries/Departments to \nidentify and implement reforms for enhancing Ease of Doing Business. \n\nRegular interactions are also held with industry associations to \nunderstand concerns of the industry and facilitate resolutions of these \nissues. \n  \nBusiness Reform Action Plan (BRAP) exercise has commenced in \nDecember, 2014. For this, consultations are held with States/UTs while \npreparing BRAP for implementation at State level. Under BRAP \nexercise, all States/UTs in the country are assessed on the basis of \nreforms implemented by them on designated parameters. BRAP covers \nreform areas such as access to information and transparency, single \nwindow system, land administration and transfer of land and property \netc. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |691", "t": "Promotion of Bio-fuels", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 691 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21 st July, 2022 \nPromotion of Bio-fuels  \n691. SHRI MANOJ KOTAK:  \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n                               [ [ \n(a) whether the Government is promoting biofuels with the broader objectives of \nreducing import dependency in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government is working towards achieving target of ethanol \nblended petrol as mentioned in the Biofuel Policy, 2018, and if so the details \nthereof; \n(c) whether the Government is providing financial support to integrated bio-\nethanol projects for setting up second generation ethanol projects in the \ncountry, if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the details of the target achieved for reducing import dependency in the country \nalong with the foreign currency saved during the last three years, year-wise? \nANSWER \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h  \n¼Jh jkes'oj rsyh½ \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & \nNATURAL GAS  \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): For biofuels, the Government is implementing the Ethanol Blended \nPetrol (EBP) Programme and Sustainable Alternative Towards Affordable \nTransportation (SATAT) with the broader objectives of promoting domestic \nagriculture sector, ensuring environmental sustainability, reducing import \ndependency and saving foreign exchange. \nGovernment had earlier notified the National Policy on Biofuels – 2018 which laid \ndown indicative target of 20% blending of ethanol in petrol and 5% blending of \nbiodiesel in diesel by 2030 in the country. Government has now amended the \nNational Policy on Biofuels – 2018 which has advanced the ethanol blending target \nof 20% blending of ethanol in petrol to ESY 2025-26 from 2030. For the ongoing \nEthanol Supply Year (ESY 2021-22), Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) have \nalready achieved 10.16% blending as on 10 th July, 2022. \n\n \nWith a view to promoting the use of Compressed Bio Gas (CBG) as automotive \nfuel, Sustainable Alternative Towards Affordable Transportation (SATAT) \ninitiative has been launched on 01.10.2018 under which OMCs have been inviting \nExpression of Interest (EoI) from potential entrepreneurs to produce CBG. Till \n28.2.2022, Oil PSUs have released Letter of Intent (LoIs) for establishment of \n3160 CBG plants for procurement of CBG. \nUsed Cooking Oil (UCO) has been identified as a potential raw material for \nbiodiesel production and public sector OMCs have floated EoIs for procurement of \nBiodiesel produced from UCO across 300 locations. OMCs have received offers \nfor setting up 61 plants with a production capacity of approximately 36 crore litres \nper annum for manufacturing Biodiesel from UCO. \n(c): In March, 2019, Government has notified the “Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN (Jaiv \nIndhan - Vatavaran Anukool fasal awashesh Nivaran) Yojana” for providing \nfinancial support to integrated bio-ethanol projects for setting up Second \nGeneration (2G) ethanol projects in the country using lignocellulosic biomass and \nother renewable feedstock. The total financial outlay for the scheme is Rs. 1969.50 \ncrore for the period 2018-19 to 2023-24.  Under the PM JI-VAN Yojana, the \nmaximum financial assistance of Rs. 150 crore per project for commercial projects \nand Rs. 15 crore per project for demonstration projects has been prescribed. \n(d): Under the EBP programme, the Government has already achieved the \nintermediate target of 10% average blending in the country in June, 2022 ahead of \nthe target date of November, 2022. \nThe details of estimated foreign currency (in Rs.)  saving during last three Ethanol \nSupply Year are as follows: \nEthanol Supply Year \n(1st December – 30th \nNovember) \nTotal Saving (Rs. Cr.) \n2018-19 4145.27 \n2019-20 3697.02 \n2020-21 10605.98 \nTotal 18448.27 \n             Source: Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC) \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |692", "t": "Management of Plastic Waste", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.692 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nMANAGEMENT OF PLASTIC WASTE \n  \nNO. 692.  SHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL:  \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA:  \n \nWill the Minister of State for HOUSING AND URBAN AF FAIRS be \npleased to state: \n   \n (a)  the measures being taken by the Government fo r the \nManagement of plastic and other items which are not  \nbiodegradable;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government provides additional cen tral assistance \nto the States and UTs for the Management of solid w aste \nincluding plastic waste under the Swachh Bharat Mission; and  \n \n(c)  if so, the details of the assistance provided to the State of \nGujarat in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) : Measures taken by Government for management o f plastic and \nother non-biodegradable waste are as under :- \n \n(i) Ban on single use plastic (SUP) :  Ministry of Environment, \nForest and Climate Change (MoEF&CC) has imposed nat ion-wide \nban on import, stocking, distribution, sale and use  of SUP items \nfrom 01st July, 2022. \n \n(ii) Government funding : Additional Central Assist ance (ACA) is \nprovided to States/Union Territories for setting up  of waste \nprocessing plants and also for remediation of legac y waste \ndumpsites. \u00017,365.82 Crore was allocated for Solid Waste  \n…2/- \n\n-2- \nManagement component under Swachh Bharat Mission (S BM-U) \nwhile \u000110,168 Crore has been allocated for Solid Waste \nManagement (SWM) component in the ongoing SBM-U 2.0.  \n \n(iii) Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) : MoEF &CC has \nnotified “Guidelines on EPR” on plastic packaging u nder Plastic \nWaste Management Rules, 2016. The guidelines have c ome into \neffect from 18 th  February, 2022.  \n \n(iv) Survekshan and protocols : Swachh Survekshan (SS) and Star \nRating protocols, issued under Swachh Bharat Missio n-Urban \nhave been aligned with Plastic Waste Management (Am endment) \nRules, 2021, to encourage cities to phase out singl e-use plastic. \nIndicators for Swachh Survekshan 2021 and 2022 incl ude ban \nimposed by Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) on SUP up to 5 0 microns \nfor SS 2021 and up to 75 microns for SS 2022. \n \nThe Star Rating protocol for Garbage Free Cites (GF C) mandates \nban on storage, supply, transport, sale or distribu tion of single \nuse/ throw-away plastic, non-woven bags, styrofoam and \nthermocol, for 5 star and 7 star levels. \n \n(v) Public awareness campaign : SBM-U 2.0 lays spec ial \nemphasis on reduction of plastic waste generation a nd \ncompliance of Plastic Waste Management (PWM) Rules,  through \nmass awareness campaign.  Source segregation of was te, their \nsegregated collection and transportation and proces sing of \nsegregated waste will be a key priority of focus un der the \nongoing SBM-U 2.0. \n \n(vi) Issue of Advisory : Government has also develo ped a “Plastic \nWaste Management Advisory”, capturing the plastic w aste \ngeneration, plastic waste management rules and vari ous ways to \nmanage plastic waste through reduce, reuse, recycle  and \nrecovery techniques. \n \n(b) & (c) : Government provides Additional Central Assistance (ACA) \nto States/ UTs for setting up of SWM projects as pe r the following \nschedule : \n \n…3/- \n \n\n \n-3- \nStatus of States/ Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) % ACA \n \nNorth-East and Himalayan States 90% \nUTs without Legislatures  100% \nUTs with Legislatures  80% \nULBs with population with more than 10 Lakh 25% \nULBs with population between 1 to 10 Lakh 25% \nULBs with population less than 1 Lakh 50% \n \nThe details of ACA provided to Gujarat under SBM (U ) and SBM (U) \n2.0 is as under: \n   (in Rs. Crore) \nPhase of Swachh \n \nBharat Mission- Urban \n(SBM-U) \nStatus of Central Share (CS) funds  \nOverall Allocation Allocation for SWM \ncomponent \nAllocation  Claimed \nby State \nAllocation \n Claimed \nby State \nSBM-U      (02.10.2014 \nto 30.09.2021) \n834.15  817.00  536.22  536.22  \nSBM-U 2.0   (01.10.2021 \nto 30.09.2026) \n1918.90  64.45  701.40 57.85 \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |694", "t": "Collateral free Loan under Pradhan Mantri Rojgar Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 694\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022\n \n \nCOLLATERAL FREE LOAN UNDER PRADHAN MANTRI ROJGAR YOJANA\n \n694. \n \nSHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:\n \n \nSHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to increase the collateral free loan of rupees one lakh \nprovided under the Pradhan Mantri Rojgar Yojana to rupees two lakhs; \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the\n \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government also proposes to extend the subsidy of fifteen per cent on the project \ncost, up to Rs.15,000 to Andhra Pradesh at par with North\n-\nEastern regions, Himachal Pradesh and \nUttarakhand; and\n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) to (d):\n \n \nPradhan Mantri Rojgar Yojana\n \n(PMRY) was earlier being implemented by the \nGovernment and it was discontinued with effect from \n0\n1.\n0\n4.2008. A new scheme namely Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP) was formulated and put into operation \nfrom 2008\n-\n09 by merging two empl\noyment generation schemes viz., PMRY and Rural Employment \nGeneration Progamme (REGP). Khadi & Village Industries Commission (KVIC) is the single \nnational level nodal implementing agency for PMEGP. The scheme is implemented by KVIC, Khadi \nand Village Indust\nry Boards (KVIBs), Districts Industries Centers (DICs) and Coir\n \nBoard (for coir \nunits) at State level. \n \n \n \nUnder PMEGP, financial assistance (loan) is admissible for maximum project cost up to \nRs.50 lakh under manufacturing sector and Rs.20 lakh under servi\nce sector. General Category \nbeneficiaries can avail of Margin Money subsidy of 25 % of the project cost in rural areas and 15% \nin urban areas. For special categories such as SC/\n \nST/\n \nOBC\n/\n \nM\ninorities\n/ \nW\nomen/\n \nT\nransgender\n/ \nE\nx\n-\nserviceman/ \nD\nifferently \nA\nble/\n \nNER/\n \nHill, Border areas and Aspirational districts etc. the Margin \nMoney subsidy is 35% in rural areas and 25% in urban areas.\n \n \n \n \nReserve Bank of India (RBI) instructions provide for collateral free loans to MSMEs upto \nRs.10.00 lakh. Accordingly, PMEGP scheme \nguidelines also provide that “no collateral security will \nbe insisted upon by banks in line with the guidelines of RBI for projects involving loan up to Rs.10 \nlakh forwarded to them by the agencies under PMEGP”.\n \n \n \nPMEGP Scheme is applicable across the coun\ntry including the State of Andhra Pradesh.\n \n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |695", "t": "Rejuvenation of Rivers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 695 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \nREJUVENATION OF RIVERS \n695  SHRI MARGANI BHARAT \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:- \n(a) whether it is true that the Ministry has prepared DPRs for rejuvenation of thirteen major rivers in the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details of the rivers identified from the State of Andhra Pradesh; \n(c) the details of components under which the above DPRs of identified rivers are to be taken up; and \n(d) the expected time by which the proposed works a re likely to be completed with particular reference  to \nAndhra Pradesh? \n  \nANSWER  \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a) to (d) For conservation and rejuvenation of rivers, this M inistry has been supplementing efforts of the \nStates/Union Territories (UTs) by providing financi al and technical assistance for abatement of pollut ion in \nidentified polluted stretches of rivers in the coun try through the Central Sector Scheme of Namami Gan ge \nfor River Ganga and its tributaries, and the Centra lly Sponsored Scheme of National River Conservation  \nPlan (NRCP) for other rivers. Sewerage infrastructu re and setting up of sewage treatment plants (STPs)  in \npolluting towns/cities along the rivers are the important components of these programs. \n          In addition, Ministry of Environment, For est & Climate Change (MoEF&CC) has recently release d \nDetailed Project Reports (DPR) for Rejuvenation of 13 major rivers prepared by Indian Council of Fores try \nResearch and Education (ICFRE), Dehradun in consultation with the State Forest Departments and other line \nDepartments. These 13 rivers are Sutlej, Beas, Ravi , Chenab, Jhelum, Luni, Yamuna, Mahanadi, \nBrahmaputra, Narmada, Cauvery, including Godavari a nd Krishna in Andhra Pradesh. Proposed outlay of \nthese 13 DPRs is Rs.19,342.63 crore under 4 major c omponents viz: implementation of forestry \ninterventions, strengthening knowledge management &  national capacity development, maintenance phase \nincluding scaling-up replication of successful mode ls, and national coordination for forestry interven tions \nand river conservation.  \n          Works proposed under these DPRs include a fforestation on river banks leading to increased gr een \ncover, measures to contain soil erosion, recharge g round water table, sequester carbon dioxide, catchm ent \narea treatment, ecological restoration, moisture co nservation, livelihood improvement & income generat ion, \necotourism by developing river fronts, eco-parks an d bringing awareness amongst the masses for \nimprovement of water quality & flow in rivers.  \n           These works are expected to be executed through the State Forest Departments together with State \nAgriculture, Horticulture, Urban Municipal Bodies & Rural Development, etc.  \n \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |696", "t": "Drinking Water Projects in Andhra Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO -696 \nANSWERED ON - 21/7/2022 \n \nDRINKING WATER PROJECTS IN ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n696. SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:- \n(a) the details of the drinking water projects taken  up from 2014 till date in the State of Andhra  \nPradesh, district-wise; and \n(b) the details of total funds allocated and utilized under various schemes in the said State? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JALSHAKTI \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b) Water is a State subject. Government of India supplements the efforts of State \nGovernments by  providing financial and technical assistance. It is States, who plan, approve, \nimplement, operateand maintain drinking water supply schemes.  \nTill 2019,  the Government of India in partnership with States, was implementing   National \nRural  Drinking  Water Program  (NRDWP) for improving the coverage of drinking water \nsupply in the rural areas of the countr y. Thereafter, Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) was launched in \n2019 to provide tap water supply to every rural household of the  country by 2024. The details of \nindividual scheme/ projects under JJM  (erstwhile NRDWP) in States are approved, implemented \nand maintained by the respective State Government or itsagency, responsible for the \nimplementation in the field. \nThe year -wise details of total fund allocation made and the quantum of fund utilized under  \nNRDWP/JJM, as reported by State of Andhra Pradesh, are at Annex. \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nAnnex \n \n \nAnnex referred to in Lok Sabha unstarred Q.No. 696 for answer on 21/7/2022 \n \nDetails of funds allocated, drawn and utilized by State from 2014 to 2018 (under NRDWP)  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nFinancial \nYear \nCentral share Expenditure \nunder State \nshare \nOpening \nBalance  \nFund \nallocated \nFund \ndrawn \nAvailable \nfund \nReported \nutilization \n2014-15 82.74 372.43 369.65 452.39 418.96 111.22 \n2015-16 33.43 156.69 170.05 203.48 190.60 224.00 \n2016-17 12.88 181.02 204.00 216.88 157.38 159.72 \n2017-18 59.50 159.51 233.09 292.59 267.03 205.71 \n2018-19 26.17 197.56 185.85 212.02 186.28 167.61 \n \n \n \nDetails of funds allocated, drawn and utilized by State from 2019 to 2022 (under JJM)  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nFinancial \nYear \nCentral share Expenditure \nunder State \nshare \nOpening \nBalance  \nFund \nallocated \nFund \ndrawn \nAvailable \nfund \nReported \nutilization \n2019-20 25.74 372.64 372.64 398.38 121.62 54.80 \n2020-21 276.76 790.48 297.62 574.38 427.73 180.97 \n2021-22  146.65   3,182.88   791.06   937.71  234.76 235.39 \n2022-23 (As \non 18.7.2022) \n702.95 3,458.20 0.00 702.95 122.38 0.00 \n \nSource:JJM-IMIS \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |697", "t": "Solar Energy System to Farmers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 697 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nSOLAR ENERGY SYSTEM TO FARMERS \n \n+697. SHRI RAHUL KASWAN \n  \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has decided to provide solar energy system to farmers on subsidy \nfor irrigation; \n(b)   if so, the details along with the present status thereof; \n(c)    whether any target was fixed by the Government to provide solar energy system to the \nfarmers in the country on subsidy for irrigation during the last two years and if so, the details \nthereof, State-wise including Rajasthan; \n(d) whether after approval of the applications of the consumers through this scheme, due to \nnon-deposit of farmer’s share amount by the farmers during the stipulated time period, the \nGovernment has set any guidelines and action plan to extend the period of cancellation of \ntheir application/keep them included in the selection list; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND  CHEMICALS \nAND FERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n \n(a) to (c)The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy is implementing Pradhan Mantri Kisan \nUrja Suraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM -KUSUM), which inter -alia includes providing \nstandalone solar pumps and solarization of the existing grid -connected agriculture pumps. The \nscheme has three components: \n \n(i) Component-A: installation of aggregated 10,000 MW capacity of small solar power plants, \neach of capacity up to 2 MW on the barren/fallow land of the farmers; \n(ii) Component-B: installation of 20 lakh standalone solar water pumps; and \n(iii) Component-C: solarization of 15 lakh existing grid-connected agriculture pumps including \nthrough feeder level solarisation. \n \nFor Component -B and Component -C, Government is providing central financial assistance \n(CFA) of 30% of the benchmark cost or cost discovered through tender, whichever is lower. In \ncase of North-Eastern States including Sikkim, hilly States of Himachal and Uttarakhand, UTs \nof Jammu and Kashmir, Ladakh, Andaman & Nicobar Island and Lakshadweep a higher CFA of \n50% of benchmark cost or cost discovered through tender, whichever is lower, is provided. \n  \nAs PM-KUSUM is a demand -driven scheme, targets are not fixed by the Central Government. \nAllocations under the Scheme are made to states based on demands received. State -wise and \ncomponent-wise capacities sanctioned and capacity achieved under Component -B a nd \nComponent-C, including for the State of Rajasthan, are placed at Annexure. \n \n(d) & (e) In view of the challenges faced on account of COVID-19 pandemic, completion period \nunder all three components of PM -KUSUM has been increased to 24 months from the date of \nsanction. Further, PM-KUSUM Guidelines do not provide any specific time period within which \nfarmers have to submit their share. Also, in order t o ease implementation, Ministry is regularly \nmonitoring the progre ss of implementation in the States and making the efforts to remove  \nconstraints identified from time to time.  \n***** \n \n \n\nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 697 to be answered on 21.07.2022 \n \nDetails of Pumps Sanctioned and Installed/ Solarized under PM-KUSUM Scheme \nas on 30.06.2022 \nS. \nNo. State \nComponent-B (Nos) Component-C (Nos) \nSanctioned  Installed Sanctioned \n(IPS)*  \nSanctioned \n(FLS)#  Installed \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 50 0 0 0 0 \n2 Assam 1000 0 603 0 0 \n3 Chhattisgarh 0 0 0 43265 0 \n4 Gujarat 2459 459 7000 39832 0 \n5 Goa 200 0 11000 0 0 \n6 Haryana 39326 36793 0 37142 0 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 1180 359 0 0 0 \n8 Jammu & Kashmir 5000 323 0 0 0 \n9 Jharkhand 16717 6717 208 10000 0 \n10 Karnataka 10314 314 0 250000 0 \n11 Kerala 100 0 9448 2000 21^ \n12 Ladakh 600 0 0 0 0 \n13 Madhya Pradesh  57000 7234 0 187455 0 \n14 Maharashtra  100000 5822 0 250000 0 \n15 Manipur 78 28 0 0 0 \n16 Meghalaya 35 35 0 10000 0 \n17 Nagaland 50 0 0 0 0 \n18 Odisha 5741 997 8310 0 0 \n19 Punjab  12000 10131 39 38111 0 \n20 Rajasthan   76210 44340 10764 25000 1026^ \n21 Tamil Nadu 6200 1766 20000 1311 0 \n22 Tripura 3021 947 2600 0 0 \n23 Uttar Pradesh  21842 6842 0 30000 0 \n24 Uttarakhand 338 73 200 0 0 \n25 West Bengal 0 0 5478 0 0 \n  Total 359461 123180 75650 925427 1047 \n \n* IPS : Solarization of individual grid connected agriculture pumps \n# FLS: Solarization of agriculture feeders \n^ Installed under Individual Pump Solarization (IPS) \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |698", "t": "Uranium Contamination of Ground Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA \nREJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 698 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \nURANIUM CONTAMINATION OF GROUND WATER  \n698  SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA DR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:- \n(a) whether the Government has conducted a study to  map areas with uranium contaminated ground \nwater in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the remedial action plan being prepared by the Government in Uranium contaminated areas; \n(d) whether uranium mining project of UCIL at Tummalapalle has caused groundwater pollution and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(e) whether the Government has paid adequate compen sation for damage caused to borewells and \ncrops due to uranium at Tummalapalle; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof?  \nANSWER  \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n \n(a) & (b)  Water being State subject, study on groundwater qu ality and making available safe water to \npublic falls under States’ mandate.  However, Centr al Ground Water Board (CGWB) has carried out a \nstudy to map areas with Uranium contaminated ground  water during 2019. As per this study, the \noccurrence of Uranium in ground water beyond the BI S permissible limits (0.03 mg/l) of water was \nobserved in around 409 groundwater samples against 14,377 samples analysed by the CGWB  in \naround 18 States.  \n(c)  Water being a State subject, initiatives for water  management, including its quality is primarily the  \nresponsibility of the States. The data generated by  CGWB has been shared with respective State \nGovernments for taking suitable remedial action. Fu rther, Government of India in partnership with \nStates is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) – H ar Ghar Jal since August, 2019 to make provision \n\nof potable tap water supply in adequate quantity, o f prescribed quality and on regular & long-term \nbasis to every rural household by 2024. In addition , under JJM, while allocating the funds to States/ \nUTs, 10% weightage is given to the population resid ing in habitations affected by chemical \ncontaminants including heavy metals and while plann ing for potable water supply to household \nthrough tap connection, priority is to be given to quality-affected habitations. \n(d)  As per Department of Atomic Energy (DoAE), hydroge ological and stable isotope tracer \ninvestigations carried out in the groundwater aroun d the Tummalapalle uranium mining project have \nconfirmed that there is no association between uranium mining industry and elevated uranium levels in \nground water. The Uranium occurrence is natural which has been confirmed from the investigations.  \n(e) & (f)  As per DoAE, it has been established by the scientific investigations that no damage is caused \nto borewells and crops due to UCIL operations at Tu mmalapalle. Hence, there is no question of \npayment of any compensation in this regard. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |699", "t": "Allocation of Kerosene", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 699 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2022 \n \nAllocation of Kerosene \n699.  ADV. A.M. ARIFF:  \n पे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government allocates Kerosene for the use of Outboard Motor \nfishing boats in the country under the Public Distribution System (PDS) and \nif so, the allocation made during the last five years, Statewise; \n \n(b)  the details of the changes in the prices of the Kerosene supplied for fishing \nboats under PDS during the said period; \n \n(c)  whether the Union Government has received any representation from the \nState Government of Kerala for enhancing the allocation of non-subsidised \nKerosene for the use of fishing boats in the State and if so, the details and \ncurrent status thereof; and \n \n \n(d)  whether the Union Government would consider entrusting Matsyafed, the \napex federation of the fishermen co-operatives under the State \nGovernment of Kerala with the wholesale distribution of Kerosene for the \nfishing boats in Kerala as it would help to streamline the distribution \nprocess and if not, the reasons therefor? \nA N S W E R \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n(\u0000ी रामेश्वर तेली)  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS  \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) Government makes allocation of Public Distribution System (PDS) \nKerosene for cooking and lighting purpose on quarterly basis to States/UTs. \nVide order dated 21 st August 2012, Government has  empowered States/ \nUTs to draw an allocation of one month’s quota of PDS Kerosene at Non- \nSubsidized rates during each financial year for special needs such as natural \nCalamities, religious functions, Fisheries, Various yatras etc. Distribution \nplan of Non-Subsidised PDS Kerosene is decided by the respective State/UT. \n \nAnnual allocation of Non-Subsidised PDS Kerosene is done by \nPetroleum Planning & Analysis Cell (PPAC) in the beginning of each financial \n \nPage 1 of 2 \n \n\n  \n \nyear. States/UTs may seek further additional allocation after exhausting \navailable quota. Details of state-wise yearly final allocation for non-\nsubsidised PDS SKO during the last five years is attached as Annexure. \n \n(b) Ex-Marketing Installation price of Non Subsidized PDS SKO (for special needs \nincluding fisheries) Ex- Kochi depot (excluding taxes and incidental charges \ndepot onwards) for last 5 year is as under : \n \nYear  01.04.2017 01.04.2018 01.04.2019 01.04.2020 01.04.2021 \nPrice \n(Rs/KL) \n26924.55 33749.78 36512.78 22262.00 32746.00 \n \n \n(c) Government has received requests from Government of Kerala for additional \nallocation of Non Subsidized PDS Kerosene. Considering the request of \nGovernment of Kerala, in addition to already allocated quota of 3084 KL, \nadditional allocation of Non Subsidized PDS Kerosene has been done making \nan overall allocation of 21888 KL non subsidized PDS SKO for year 2021-22. \nFurther, for the year 2022-23, an allocation of 2160 KL non subsidized \nkerosene has been made to the state of Kerala.   \n \n(d) No Sir. As per policy, there is no provision to award direct Kerosene \ndealership by Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs). \n \n   \n***** \nPage 2 of 2 \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha unstarred question No. 699 asked by  Adv. A.M. \nAriff for answer on 21.07.2022 regarding Allocation of Kerosene \nIndustry Non-subsidized PDS SKO allocation  (Qty in KL) \nName of  the State / UT 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22   \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands 420 336 336 156 0 \nAndhra Pradesh 18804 4692 4692 0 0 \nArunachal Pradesh 768 660 660 528 396 \nAssam 24096 20484 20484 15156 12960 \nBihar 59220 41280 27708 21912 17724 \nChandigarh 0 0 0 0 0 \nChattishgarh 11280 9588 9588 6864 4848 \nDadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & \nDiu 192 0 0 0 0 \nDelhi 0 0 0 0 0 \nGoa 384 180 168 60 24 \nGujarat 41076 28704 20736 11568 6996 \nHaryana 5520 0 0 0 0 \nHimachal Pradesh 1500 1200 1200 948 816 \nJammu and Kashmir 6084 4872 4872 3744 3744 \nJharkhand 18300 15564 15564 11268 7500 \nKarnataka 23952 12996 12996 8822 10620 \nKerala 7356 16884 15108 15108 21888 \nLadakh 0 0 0 288 288 \nLakshadweep 72 60 60 60 60 \nMadhya Pradesh 36864 28752 28008 21720 17784 \nMaharashtra 46968 32052 23880 8868 3876 \nManipur 1752 1488 1488 1104 924 \nMeghalaya 1896 1620 1620 1596 1572 \nMizoram 492 396 396 300 264 \nNagaland 1248 1008 852 768 708 \nOdisha 24348 20700 20700 17544 16188 \nPuducherry 312 0 0 0 0 \nPunjab 6192 0 6192 0 0 \nRajasthan 30948 21660 11496 3324 1176 \nSikkim 348 288 288 204 180 \nTamil Nadu 25620 17040 16140 12108 10764 \nTelangana 12948 8604 8160 4716 1332 \nTripura 2880 2448 2448 2196 2088 \nUttar Pradesh 97344 75924 73980 43152 15132 \nUttrakhand 2640 2112 2112 660 180 \nWest Bengal 65736 58668 58668 58668 58668 \nTotal 577560 430260 390600 273410 218700 \nSource: Petroleum Planning & Analysis Cell \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |7", "t": "Students Trained in ITIs", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.7 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \nSTUDENTS TRAINED IN ITIs \n \n7.  COL. RAJYAVRDHAN RATHORE \nWill the Minister of Skill Development and Entrepre neurship be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of students being trained in ITIs ( Industrial Training Institute) and \nGovernment and Private polytechnics for the last th ree years, state-wise including \nRajasthan district-wise; \n(b) the status of setting up Indian Institutes of S kills across the country and the \nproposed timeline for them becoming operational; \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal of sett ing up a national level ITI to \nbridge the gap between supply and demand of skilled  workforce and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal for inc luding Institutions of National \nImportance under Ministry of Home Affairs as for co ntributing towards skilling, reskilling, \nupskilling of the workforce? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir. The details of students trained in I ndustrial Training Institutes and \nGovernment and Private Polytechnics for last three years, state-wise including Rajasthan \ndistrict-wiseareat Annexure I&Annexure IIrespectively. \n(b)  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Skill Development and En trepreneurship has taken initiative to \nset up Indian Institute of Skills (IIS) in three lo cations viz. Ahmedabad, Kanpur and \nMumbai. The vision behind setting up IIS is to buil d world class skill training centres by \nlearning from and imbibing best practices from inte rnationally renowned existing skill \ninstitutions.The status of three Indian Institute of Skills is as follows: \nSI No Name of IIS Status \n1.  IIS Ahmedabad For setting up of IIS at Ahmedabad in  PPP model, TATA \nEducation and Development Trust (TEDT) is selected as \nprivate operating partner through RFP process. \n2.  IIS Kanpur Construction work for ISS building is in progress a nd the \ndiscussions for operations including agency are under way. \n3.  IIS Mumbai For setting up of IIS at Mumbai, Tata Ed ucation and \nDevelopment Trust (TEDT) was selected as private \noperating partner through RFP process.  Short-term courses \nhave started for IIS Mumbai at Pune (in partnership  with \nFesto). \n \n\n \n(c) Setting up of Industrial Training Institutes (I TIs) is a concurrent subject, wherein the \nCentral Government is entrusted with the responsibi lity \nof formulation of policy, laying down training stan dards, norms, whereas opening of \nITIs, implementation of norms and day to day admini stration rests with the respective \nState Governments. Whenever such proposals are rece ived for setting up of ITIs, these \nare examined and decided as per the affiliation sta ndards and norms by Directorate \nGeneral of Training (DGT). \n(d) As on date no such proposal has been received. \n\n+ \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sa bha Unstarred Question No. 07 to \nbe answered on 18.07.2022. The number of students t rained in Industrial Training \nInstitutes (ITIs)for last three years, state-wise are as given below: \nSl. no.  State  Enrolment 2019  Enrolment 2020  Enrolment 2021  \n1. 