��������� ��� ����������� ������ ���\n���������������� ���������������� ���������������� ������������������ �������������������� �������������������������� ������\n���������������������\n���\n������������������������������� ��������������������������������� ����\n����������������������������������������������������������� ����������\n�� ������ �\n�� ��������������������������� ��������������������������� ���������������������������\n���\n���\n��\n������������\n��\n������������\n��������\n���������������������������\n��������\n������� ��������������������\n��������\n���������������\n�� �����������\n����\n������ ���������� ����� ��������� ����� ���������\n�� ����� � ���� �������� ���� �������� ���� ��������\n�� ������ ����� ��������� ������ ���������� ������ ����������\n�� ���� � ����� ���������� ����� ���������� ������ ����������\n�� ������������������ ��������� ����� ��������� ����� ���������\n�� ����� � ���� �������� ��� �������� ���� ��������\n�� ��� � ��� �������� ��� ������� ��� �������\n�� �������� ����� ��������� ����� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |700", "t": "Impact of Coal Shortage on Power Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.700 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nIMPACT OF COAL SHORTAGE ON POWER PLANTS \n \n700.  SHRI DAYANIDHI MARAN: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has prepar ed any report  or analysis on the impact of \ncoal shortage on the power  crisis being faced by many States across the country;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the steps  being taken to increase the \navailability of domestic coal; \n \n(c)  whether media  reports that coal inventories at  power plants declined \nsignificantly since April were true  and if so, the current details of the actual and \nnormative stocks of the different power plants in the country; \n \n(d)  whether media reports that authorities conside red cutting domestic coal supply \nto power plants that were  reluctant to import coal at elevated prices were true and  if \nso, the details thereof; and \n \n(e)  the details of the States which were most affected  by the coal shortage and \npower crisis along with t he steps being taken by the Government to provide relief to  \nthem? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e) :  The coal stock available in the power plants is monitored by Central \nElectricity Authority (C EA) on daily basis. As on 31.03.2022, the domestic coal \nstock was 24.18 Million Tonnes (MT) which depleted to 19.80 MT as on \n30.04.2022 but increased during the months of May and June, 2022 and has now \nreached 23.126 MT as on 14.07.2022, which is about 40% of the normative stock \nrequirement of 56.6 MT during the month of July, 2022. As on 14.07.2022, the \ncoal stock is sufficient for an average of 10 days at a requirement of 85% PLF. \nPlant-wise coal stock position along with their normative requirement is at  \nAnnexure-I. \n \nMinistry of Power vide letter dated 18.05.2022 to Gencos stated that \ndomestic coal would be allocated proportionately to all Gencos based on likely \navailability of domestic coal from Coal India Limited (CIL) /Singhareni Collieries \nCompany Lim ited (SCCL) from 01.06.2022 and the shortfall is to be met from \nblending of the imported coal. \n..........2. \n\n \n- 2 - \n \nGovernment has constituted a sub-group comprising of officers from \nMinistry of Power, Coal & Railways to take decision on supply of coal fro m CIL. \nSub-Group takes into account factors like progress of lifting of coal by GENCOs \nfrom CIL subsidiaries, captive mines, blending percentage for allocating coal \nsupplies.  \n \nThe comparative details of State/UT -wise actual power supply \nposition in the co untry during the months of April, 2022 to June, 2022 vis -a-vis \nthe corresponding months of 2021, are given at Annexure-II. During the period \nApril, 2022 to June, 2022, All India average gap between the Energy \nRequirement and Energy Supplied was only 1%. Ga p between energy demand \nand supply is generally on account of factors other than inadequacy of power \navailability in the country e.g. constraints in distribution network, financial \nconstraints, commercial reasons, forced outage of generating units etc. \n \nGovernment has taken the following steps to increase the \navailability of domestic coal:- \n \n(i) An Inter -Ministerial Sub Group comprising of representatives from \nMinistry of Power, Ministry of Coal, Ministry of Railways, Central \nElectricity Authority (CEA) , Coal India Limited (CIL) and Singareni \nCollieries Company Limited (SCCL) meet regularly to take various \noperational decisions to enhance supply of coal to thermal power plants \nas well as for meeting any contingent situations relating to Power \nSector including to alleviate critical coal stock position in power plants. \n \n(ii) As per the decision taken in the meeting held under the Chairmanship of \nCabinet Secretary on Augmentation of coal supply and power generation \ncapacity, a Secretary level Inter Ministerial co mmittee has been set up \nto ensure that the medium and long term requirements of coal are met. \nThe IMC comprises of Chairman Railway Board, Secretary, Ministry of \nCoal, Secretary, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change as \nmembers and Secretary, Ministry of Power as convener. \n \n(iii) CIL has allocated about 16 MT coal on Road cum Rail (RCR) mode to \nTPPs. \n \n(iv) Railways has issued order to give preference to Power Sector for loading \nof coal from Good Shed Siding (GSS) and Private Washery. \n \n******** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 700 ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 21.07.2022   \n************** \n DAILY COAL STOCK REPORT \nAs on 14.07.2022 \nRegion/State Mode of \nTransport \nName of Thermal Power \nStation \nSector Utility Capacity (MW) Normative \nStock  Reqd. \n(Days) \nRequirement for the \nday @85% PLF (In '000 \nTonnes') \nNormative Stock \nRequired (In '000 \nTonnes') \nActual \nStock \n% of Actual \nStock vis-à-vis \nNormative  Stock \nIn '000 Tonnes' \n         Indigen\n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |702", "t": "Objectives of PM-KUSUM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 702 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nOBJECTIVES OF PM-KUSUM \n702.SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE \n       DR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR. S \n       SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE \n       DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE \n       SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has achieved the objective for which Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja Suraksha \nevam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM-KUSUM) was launched and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the target fixed and achieved under PM-KUSUM during the last three years and the current year;  \n(c)    the reasons for inability to install solar water pumps under PM-KUSUM;  \n(d) the number of solar water pumps that have been installed under PM-KUSUM during the said period \nin the State/Union Territory of Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu and Andaman & Nicobar Islands; \n(e) the steps taken by the Government to increase the country’s solar water pump manufacturing capacity; \n(f) whether the Government has requisite surveillance mechanisms and is taking active measures to \nensure solar water pumps do not disrupt the ground water table in districts with depleted levels of ground \nwater and if so, the details thereof; and \n(g) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to decentralised solar power production? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND  CHEMICALS AND   \n      FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)  \n  \n(a) & (b) The objectives of the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja Suraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM -\nKUSUM) include de-dieselisation of the farm sector, providing water and energy security to farmers, \nincreasing the income of farmers and curbing environmental po llution. To achieve these objectives, \nfollowing targets have been kept under the Scheme:  \nComponent-A: 10,000 MW of Decentralized Ground Mounted Grid Connected Solar Power Plants.  \nComponent-B: Installation of 20 lakh standalone Solar Powered Agriculture Pumps.  \nComponent-C: Solarisation of 15 Lakh Grid -connected Agriculture Pumps including through Feeder \nLevel Solarisation.   \nPM-KUSUM is a demand driven scheme and capacities are allocated based on demand received from \nthe states. Component-wise capacity allocated and achieved in the last three years and current year, till \n30.06.2022, is as given below: \n \nComponent Capacity \nsanctioned \nCapacity \nInstalled \nComponent-A  4906 MW 56 MW \nComponent-B  3.59 lakh nos. 1.23 lakh nos. \nComponent-C  10.01 lakh nos. 1047 nos. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n(c) Although installation of solar pumps is being carried out continuously, the pace of implementation \nhas been significantly affected due to the Covid-19 pandemic. To compensate the implementing agencies \nfor challenges faced due to supply chain disruptions and capital & liquidity issues, Ministry has provided \nmultiple extensions. Despite the above-mentioned challenges, 1.23 lakh solar pumps have been installed \ntill 30.06.2022 under Component -B of PM -KUSUM Scheme and 1047 nos. of grid -connected pumps \nhave been solarized under Component ‘C’. \n \n(d) Till 30.06.2022, under Component ‘B’ of the PM-KUSUM Scheme, 5822 nos. of solar pumps have \nbeen installed in the State of Maharashtra and 1766 nos. of solar pumps have been installed in the State \nof Tamil Nadu. No existing agriculture pump has been solarized under component ‘C’ of the Scheme in \nthe states of Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu. Further, in absence of demand, no sanction could be made \nfor the installation of solar pumps under PM-KUSUM in the Andaman & Nicobar Islands. \n \n(e) Following provisions of the PM -KUSUM Scheme aim to increase country’s solar water pump \nmanufacturing capacity: (i) Target of installation or solarisation of 35 lakh pumps through central \nfinancial support under the Scheme provides visibility of demand in the coming years. (ii) Condition of \ndomestic content requirement for participation in Component -B and Component -C. (iii) Direct \nparticipation of manufacturers of solar pumps/ solar photovoltaic modules/ solar pump controller either \nas sole bidder or member of a Joint Venture, in bidding under Component-B and Component-C. \n \n(f) To avoid disruption of the groundwater table, particularly in the districts with depleted levels of \nground water, installation of new solar pumps is not allowed under the Scheme in the dark zones/ areas \nnotified by Central Ground Water Board (CGWB), which monitors and regulates groundwater \ndevelopment and extraction. Only the existing diesel pumps can be replaced with solar pumps under \nComponent-B and existing electric pumps can be solarized under Component-C in these areas provided \nthey use micro-irrigation techniques to save water. Additionally, to conserve groundwater, the following \nprovisions are included in the PM -KUSUM Scheme: (i) Preference for installation of standalone solar \npumps and solarisation of existing agriculture pumps is given to the farmers using mi"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |703", "t": "Recall of Vehicles Due To Safety Defects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 703 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2022 \n \nRECALL OF VEHICLES DUE TO SAFETY DEFECTS \n \n703.  SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \nSHRI S. MUNISWAMY: \nSHRI B.Y. RAGHAVENDRA: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \nSHRI SHA.BRA.DR.JAI SIDDESHWAR SHIVACHARYA MAHASWAMIJI: \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \nSHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n     \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number with class/type of vehicles which were recalled in \nthe country due to safety defects during the last three years and the \ncurrent year; \n(b)  the total number of road accidents involving four wheelers/Light \nMotor Vehicles (LMVs) in the country during the said period; \n(c)  whether there is a mandatory recall policy for vehicles which fail to \nmeet the prescribed safety standards and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether the Government has conducted any study regarding road \naccidents in the country involving four wheelers/LMVs during the said \nperiod and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)  the steps taken by the Government to minimise the number of road \naccidents in the country? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) As per data maintained by the Society of Indian Automobile \nManufacturers (SIAM), under Voluntary Recall Code of SIAM, the  total \n\nnumber, with class/type of vehicles recalled in the country due to safety \ndefects during the last three years and the current year, are as under:- \n \nYear Two wheelers  Passenger cars \nFY 2019 - 2020              53,324              1,61,597 \nFY 2020 - 2021                 1,286              3,38,652 \nFY 2021 - 2022           8,64,557              4,67,311 \n1st April 2022 - Till 15th \nJuly, 2022 \n          1,60,025                  25,142 \nTotal        10,79,192              9,92,702 \n \n(b) As per the data received from police department of all States/UTs, the \ntotal number of road accidents that occurred, involving categories of \nCars, Taxis, Vans & LMVs  in the country during the last four calendar \nyears 2017 to 2020, are given in the following table:- \n \n Year Total Number of Road Accidents \ninvolving  Cars, Taxis, Vans & LMVs \n2017 113737 \n2018 113490 \n2019 92196 \n2020 60986 \n  \n(c)  Section 110A of the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 is related to recall of \nmotor vehicles. It empowers the Central Government to direct a \nmanufacturer to recall motor vehicles of a particular type or its variants, \nif- \n  \n(a) a defect in that particular type of motor vehicle may cause harm to \nthe environment or to the driver or occupants of such motor vehicle or \nother road users; and \n(b) a defect in that particular type of motor vehicle has been reported to \nthe Central Government by— \n(i) such percentage of owners, as the Central Government may, by \nnotification in the Official Gazette, specify; or \n(ii) a testing agency; or \n(iii) any other source. \n  \nFurther, sub-section (6) of Section 110A of the Motor Vehicles Act, \n1988 empowers the Central Government to make rules for regulating the \nrecall of motor vehicles, of a particular type or its variants, for any defect \nwhich in the opinion of the Central Gov ernment, may cause harm to the \n\nenvironment or to the driver or occupants of such motor vehicle or to \nother road users. Accordingly, Ministry of Road Transport and Highways \nvide G.S.R. 173(E) dated 11 th March, 2021 has inserted a new rule 127C \nin Central Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989 which prescribes the procedure for \nrecall of defective motor vehicles and recall notice. \n  \n(d) and (e)  On the basis of the data on road accidents from States/UTs, \nthe Ministry of Road Transport & Highways published the Annual repor t \ntitled “Road Accidents in India”. \n  \nThe analysis of data from  States/UTs reveals that road accidents \nare multi-causal and are the result of interplay of various factors which \ncan broadly be categorized into human error, Road condition/environment \nand vehicular condition.  \n          Ministry has formulated a multi -pronged strategy to address the \nissue of road safety based on Education, Engineering (both of roads and \nvehicles), Enforcement and Emergency Care. Accordingly, various \ninitiatives have been taken by the Ministry, as detailed below:- \n1. Education:  \ni. To create effective public awareness about road safety, the Ministry \nundertakes various publicity measures and awareness campaigns on \nroad safety through social media, electronic media, and print media. \nFurther, Ministry implements a road safety advocacy scheme to \nprovide financial assistance to various agencies for administering \nRoad Safety Advocacy. \nii. Observance of National Road Safety Month/Week every year for \nspreading awareness and strengthening road safety. \niii. A Certification Course for Road Safety Auditors has been commenced \nin the Indian Academy of Highway Engineers (IAHE). \niv. To impart driving"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |704", "t": "National Policy on Biofuels", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 704 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21 st July, 2022 \nNational Policy on Biofuels  \n704.  DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \n SHRI BHOLA SINGH: \n SHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK: \n SHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA): \n SHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO: \n DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY: \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n                               [ [ \n(a) whether the Government has approved the Amendments to the National Policy \non Biofuels -2018 and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has achieved the target set under the Policy and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is implementing Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN (Jaiv \nIndhan- Vatavaran Anukool fasal awashesh Nivaran) Yojana and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(d) whether the Government is implementing the Ethanol Blended Petrol (EBP) \nprogramme and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the other steps being taken by the Government for becoming “energy \nindependent” by 2047? \nANSWER \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h  \n¼Jh jkes'oj rsyh½ \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & \nNATURAL GAS  \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): The Government has amended the National Policy on Biofuels -2018. \nThe amendments inter-alia include: \ni) allowing more feedstocks for production of biofuels, \nii) advancing the ethanol blending target of 20% blending of ethanol in petrol \n to ESY 2025-26 from 2030, \n\n \niii) promotion of the production of biofuels in the country, under the Make in \n India program, by units located in Special Economic Zones (SEZ)/ Export \n Oriented Units (EoUs), and \niv) grant of permission for export of biofuels in specific cases.  \n \nUnder the programme, the Government has already achieved the intermediate \ntarget of 10% average blending in the country in June, 2022 ahead of the target \ndate of November, 2022. \nFor the ongoing Ethanol Supply Year (ESY 2021-22), Oil Marketing Companies \n(OMCs) have achieved 10.16% blending as on 10 th July, 2022. \n(c): In March, 2019, Government has notified the “Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN (Jaiv \nIndhan - Vatavaran Anukool fasal awashesh Nivaran) Yojana” for providing \nfinancial support to integrated bio-ethanol projects for setting up Second \nGeneration (2G) ethanol projects in the country using lignocellulosic biomass and \nother renewable feedstock. The total financial outlay for the scheme is Rs. 1969.50 \ncrore for the period 2018-19 to 2023-24.  Under the PM JI-VAN Yojana, the \nmaximum financial assistance of Rs. 150 crore per project for commercial projects \nand Rs. 15 crore per project for demonstration projects has been prescribed. \n(d): The Government is implementing the Ethanol Blended Petrol (EBP) \nProgramme with the broader objectives of reducing import dependency, generating \nemployment, providing better remuneration to farmers, promoting better waste \nmanagement practices, etc.  \n(e): The Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas is taking various steps towards \nenergy independence such as increasing domestic production of crude oil and \nnatural gas; promoting biofuels and renewables; promoting energy efficiency and \nconservation measures; reducing imports through improvement in refinery process; \nadopting demand substitution measures; creating favorable environment for \nadditional investment through facilitating policy changes etc.    \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |706", "t": "LPG Connections under PMUY", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 706 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  21st JULY, 2022 \n  \nLPG Connections under PMUY \n \n706. SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total number of LPG connections provided under Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) \n1.0 and 2.0, State/UT-wise and district-wise for Telangana;  \n \n(b) the total number of refills taken by beneficiaries of PMUY during the year 2021-22, State/UT and \ndistrict wise for Telangana;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has conducted any survey on the number of refills being taken by the \nbeneficiaries, if so, the details on the number of beneficiaries who have taken four or fewer refills \nduring 2021-22, State/UT-wise and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has identified the number of beneficiaries who are back to cooking with \nfirewood and cow dung cakes and if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; and  \n \n(e) whether the Government conducted any study on low refills because of rising prices of LPG \ncylinders and if so, the steps taken by the Government to increase refills among beneficiaries of \nPMUY? \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर  तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a)  State/UT-wise details of LPG connections released under Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala \nYojana(PMUY Phase 1.0 and Ujjwala 2.0) are at Annexure-I and district-wise details of LPG \nconnections released under PMUY in the State of Telangana are at Annexure-II.  \n \n(b)   State/UT-wise details of refills taken by PMUY beneficiaries during Financial Year  2021-22 \nare at Annexure-III and district-wise details of  refills taken by PMUY beneficiaries during \nFinancial Year  2021-22 in the State of  Telangana are at Annexure-IV. \n \n(c)  LPG consumption of Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana(PMUY) beneficiaries is monitored on \nregular basis. State/UT-wise details of  PMUY beneficiaries who have taken four or less refills \nduring Financial Year 2021-22 are at Annexure-V.  \n \n(d) & (e) Per capita consumption of PMUY beneficiaries has increased from 3.01 (FY 2019-20) to \n3.66 (FY 2021-22). Further, the Government has taken several steps to encourage consumption of \nLPG by PMUY beneficiaries, which include announcement of subsidy of ₹ 200/- per 14.2 Kg refill \nupto 12 refills/year for PMUY beneficiaries for year 2022-2023, option of 5 Kg Double Bottle \nConnection(DBC), swap option from 14.2 kg to 5 Kg, upto 3 free refills to PMUY beneficiaries \nunder Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Package from April to December 2020 etc. \n \n--------- \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) Lok Sabha Unstarred Parliament Question No. 706 for 21.07.2022 \nasked by Shri Arvind Dharmapuri  regarding “ LPG Connections under PMUY” \nState/UT PMUY (Phase 1) Ujjwala 2.0 \nTotal (As on \n01.07.2022) \nAndaman and Nicobar \nIslands 12,592 850 13,442 \nAndhra Pradesh 3,91,613 58,122 4,49,735 \nArunachal Pradesh 44,361 4,142 48,503 \nAssam 34,68,089 7,44,704 42,12,793 \nBihar 84,79,729 19,60,641 104,40,370 \nChandigarh 87 5 92 \nChhattisgarh 29,77,339 4,63,042 34,40,381 \nDadar & Nagar Haveli \nand Daman & Diu 15,010 39 15,049 \nDelhi 76,118 48,676 1,24,794 \nGoa 1,081 - 1,081 \nGujarat 28,97,487 7,43,608 36,41,095 \nHaryana 7,25,168 25,892 7,51,060 \nHimachal Pradesh 1,36,317 3,283 1,39,600 \nUT of Jammu and \nKashmir 12,28,675 14,367 12,43,042 \nJharkhand 32,55,415 3,26,132 35,81,547 \nKarnataka 31,38,029 4,83,603 36,21,632 \nKerala 2,56,028 71,208 3,27,236 \nUT of Ladakh 11,067 29 11,096 \nLakshadweep 292 11 303 \nMadhya Pradesh 71,42,063 9,41,079 80,83,142 \nMaharashtra 44,15,945 3,85,743 48,01,688 \nManipur 1,56,367 37,490 1,93,857 \nMeghalaya 1,50,514 39,124 1,89,638 \nMizoram 28,122 2,214 30,336 \nNagaland 55,134 28,349 83,483 \nOdisha 47,32,490 5,34,459 52,66,949 \nPuducherry 13,563 1,007 14,570 \nPunjab 12,18,572 33,935 12,52,507 \nRajasthan 63,54,604 4,36,741 67,91,345 \nSikkim 8,750 4,564 13,314 \nTamil Nadu 32,35,850 3,65,215 36,01,065 \nTelangana 10,71,118 60,418 11,31,536 \nTripura 2,66,077 10,404 2,76,481 \nUttar Pradesh 147,13,112 24,62,797 171,75,909 \nUttarakhand 4,03,017 84,582 4,87,599 \nWest Bengal 88,08,751 31,47,083 119,55,834 \n TOTAL  798,88,546 135,23,558 934,12,104 \n \nSource: Indian Oil Corporation Ltd. on behalf of Oil Marketing Companies \n\nAnnexure-II \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) Lok Sabha Unstarred Parliament Question No. 706 for 21.07.2022 \nasked by Shri Arvind Dharmapuri  regarding “ LPG Connections under PMUY” \nDistrict PMUY (Phase 1) Ujjwala 2.0 Total (as on 01.07.2022) \n Adilabad  34806 2127 36933 \n Bhadradri Kothagudem  64974 3341 68315 \n Hyderabad  1244 14 1258 \n Jagitial  39936 3960 43896 \n Jangaon  15441 950 16391 \n Jayashankar Bhupalpa  27611 473 28084 \n Jogulamba Gadwal  31710 1078 32788 \n Kamareddy  42125 1860 43985 \n Karimnagar  20882 5461 26343 \n Khammam  60383 7964 68347 \n Komram Bheem \nAsifaba  26390 2156 28546"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |708", "t": "Dam Related Accidents", "head": "भारत सरकार \nजल शǒƠ मंğालय \nजल संसाधन, नदȣ ǒवकास और गंगा संर¢ण ǒवभाग \nलोक सभा \nअतारांǑकत Ĥư संÉया 708 \nǔजसका उƣर 21 जुलाई, 2022 को Ǒदया जाना है। \n..... \nबांध संबंधी दुघ[टनाएं \n708. Įी राजमोहन उÛ नीथन: \n डॉ. अमर िसंह: \n Įी बालूभाऊ उफ[ सुरेश नारायण धानोरकर: \n Įी एंटो एÛ टोनी: \n डॉ. ए. चेã लाक ु मार: \n Įी के. सुधाकरन: \nÈया जल शǒƠ मंğी यह बताने कȧ कृपा करɅगे Ǒकः \n(क) 2021 मɅ बांध सुर¢ा अिधिनयम क े  लागू होने के  बाद से बांध से संबंिधत राÏ य-वार Ǒकतनी दुघ[टनाएं और मौत हुई हɇ; \n(ख) È या सरकार के पास बांध से संबंिधत दुघ[टनाओं के  कारण नुकसान उठाने वाले या ǒवè थाǒपत होने वाले लोगɉ क े  िलए \nमुआवजे का कोई Ĥावधान है; \n(ग) यǑद हां, तो त× संबंधी Þ यौरा È या है और यǑद नहȣं, तो इसक े  È या कारण हɇ;  \n(घ) È या कɅ ġ सरकार ने बांध सुर¢ा अिधिनयम, 2021 को तैयार करने से पहले राÏ य सरकारɉ क े  साथ परामश[ Ǒकया है; और  \n(ङ) यǑद हां, तो त× संबंधी Þ यौरा È या है और यǑद नहȣं, तो इसक े  È या कारण हɇ? \nउƣर \nजल शǒƠ राÏय मंğी (Įी ǒबƳेƳर टूडू) \n(क): Ǒदसंबर, 2021 मɅ बांध सुर¢ा अिधिनयम क े  लागू होने क े  बाद से, बांधɉ संबंधी Ǒकसी दुघ[टना और इससे होने वाली मौत क े \nसंबंध मɅ बांध सुर¢ा राÏय सिमितयɉ या राÏय बांध सुर¢ा संगठनɉ या बांध मािलकɉ Ʈारा कोई सूचना नहȣं दȣ गई है। \n(ख) और (ग): राÏय सरकारɉ Ʈारा उनके अपने संसाधनɉ और Ĥाथिमकता के अनुसार जल संसाधन पǐरयोजनाओं कȧ योजना \nबनाना, उनका ǒवƣ पोषण, िनçपादन और अनुर¢ण Ǒकया जाता है। संबंिधत राÏय सरकारɉ Ʈारा हȣ ऐसी पǐरयोजनाओं का भूिम \nअिधĒहण, पुनवा[स और पुनèथा[पन (आर एंड आर) काय[ भी Ǒकया जाता है। \nइसक े  अलावा, बांध सुर¢ा अिधिनयम 2021 कȧ पहली अनुसूची मɅ अÛय बातɉ क े  साथ-साथ राƶीय बांध सुर¢ा सिमित मɅ \n\"बांध सुर¢ा मूãयांकन, जोǔखम आकलन और जोǔखम Ĥबंधन क े  सुरǔ¢त आƳासन क े  वांिछत èतर तक; और बांध ǒवफलताओं से \nĤभाǒवत लोगɉ कȧ बीमा कवरेज क े  मुआवजे कȧ संभावनाओं का पता लगाने क े  åयापक बांध सुर¢ा Ĥबंधन Ǻǒƴकोण ǒवकिसत \nकरना\" Ĥदƣ है। \n(घ) और (ड): जी हां। बांध सुर¢ा ǒवधेयक 2016 का मसौदा जल संसाधन, नदȣ ǒवकास और गंगा संर¢ण ǒवभाग Ʈारा सभी राÏयɉ \nऔर संघ राÏय ¢ेğɉ को 09.08.2016 को इनपुट और सुझावɉ के  िलए पǐरचािलत Ǒकया गया था। भारतीय Ĥौƭोिगकȧ संèथान, \nǽड़कȧ मɅ Ǒदनांक 18.02.2017 को आयोǔजत राƶीय बांध सुर¢ा सिमित (एनसीडȣएस) कȧ बैठक मɅ राÏयɉ से ĤाƯ इनपुट/ǑटÜपǔणयɉ \nपर ǒवचार-ǒवमश[ Ǒकया गया, ǔजसमɅ 18 राÏयɉ ने भाग िलया। राÏयɉ/संघ राÏय ¢ेğɉ क े  ǒवचारɉ और सुझावɉ का Ú यान रखा गया \nथा। बाद मɅ, मसौदा ǒवधेयक को संशोिधत और अंितम Ǿप Ǒदया गया था। इसके  बाद, संसद मɅ Ǒदनांक 12.12.2018 को बांध सुर¢ा \nǒवधेयक, 2018 पेश Ǒकया गया जो सोलहवीं लोकसभा के ǒवघटन क े  साथ समाƯ हो गया। उपरोƠ ǒवधेयक क े  आधार पर, बांध \nसुर¢ा ǒवधेयक 2019 को संसद मɅ Ǒफर से जुलाई 2019 मɅ पेश Ǒकया गया, जो Ǒदसंबर, 2021 मɅ एक अिधिनयम बन गया। \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |709", "t": "Parameters for Setting Up of CNG and Petrol Pumps", "head": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 709 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2022 \nParameters for Setting Up of CNG and Petrol Pumps \n \n709. DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \n        SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI:  \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of norms and parameters fixed for setting up of CNG and petrol pumps in the \ncountry;  \n(b) whether CNG pumps are likely to be installed in all the districts of the country;  \n(c) if so, the details of the action plan and target set for this purpose in Rajasthan, district-wise \nincluding Sikar, Karauli and Dholpur districts;  \n(d) the details of CNG and petrol pumps operating in the above districts of Rajasthan; and  \n(e) the details of consumption of petrol, diesel and CNG in Rajasthan during the last three years, \nyear and district-wise?  \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर  तेल\u0000  )  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL \nGAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) Establishment of CNG Stations is part of the development of City Gas Distribution (CGD) \nnetwork and the same is carried out by the entities authorized by Petroleum and Natural Gas \nRegulatory Board (PNGRB). These CNG stations are set up by the entities as per the Minimum \nWork Plan determined by PNGRB and their techno-commercial feasibility.  \n \nPublic Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) have implemented new “Dealership Selection \nGuidelines” for Retail Outlets (ROs) w.e.f. 24.11.2018. \n \nRetail Outlets (ROs) are set up by Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) at identified \nlocations based on field survey and feasibility studies. Locations having sufficient potential and \nwhich are economically viable are rostered in the Marketing Plans for setting up ROs.  \n \n \n\nSelection of RO dealer is done through public advertisement released by Public Sector OMCs \nfollowed by online process of draw of lots from eligible applicants maintaining full transparency \nand minimum human interference. The applications so received against the advertisement are \nprocessed in line with laid down procedures. Detailed Norms/Criteria for allotment of RO \ndealerships in the country are available at www.petrolpumpdealerchayan.in. Private sector \nOMCs establish ROs as per their marketing plan. \n \n(b) to (d)  After completion of 11A CGD bidding round, PNGRB has authorized 295 \nGeographical Areas (GAs) in the country including 17 GAs in the state of Rajasthan for \ndevelopment of CGD network. Details of CNG stations, as on 31.05.2022, in the state of \nRajasthan including Sikar, Karauli and Dholpur Districts are at Annexure-I. As on 01.07.2022, \ntotal number of petrol pumps operation in Sikar, Karauli and Dholpur districts are 187, 78 and 64 \nrespectively. \n \n(e) Details of consumption of Petrol, Diesel and CNG in Rajasthan during the last three years are \nas under: \n FY 2019-2020 FY 2020-2021 2021-2022 \nPetrol (in MT) 1428489 1315334 1400104 \nDiesel (in MT) 3872623 3159477 3220429 \nCNG (in MMSCM) 16.63 20.57 67.41 \nMT: Metric Tonne \nMMSCMD: Million Metric Standard Cubic Meter \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 709 TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 REGARDING PARAMETERS FOR \nSETTING UP OF CNG AND PETROL PUMPS. \n \nDetails of CNG Stations in Rajsathan as on 31.05.2022 \nS.No. Geographical Area/CGD Networks State No. of CNG Stations \nMWP \ntargets \nAchievement as \non 31.05.2022 \n1. Kota Rajasthan 6 9 \n2. Barmer, Jaisalmer& Jodhpur Districts 80 20 \n3. Alwar (Other than Bhiwadi) and Jaipur \nDistricts \n132 27 \n4. Kota District (EAAA)*, Baran and \nChittorgarh (Only RawatbhataTaluka) \nDistricts \n27 14 \n5. Bhilwara and Bundi Districts 25 12 \n6. Chittorgarh (Other than \nRawatbhataTaluka) and Udaipur Districts \n70 25 \n7. DholpurDistruct 14 5 \n8. Jhabua, Banswara, Ratlam and \nDungarpur Districts \nMadhya Pradesh \nand Rajasthan \n54 21 \n9. Ajmer, Pali and Rajsamand Rajasthan 198 44 \n10. Jalore and Sirohi Districts 30 18 \n11. Agar Malwa, Neemuch, Mandsaur and \nJhasawar Districts \nMadhya Pradesh \nand Rajasthan \n126 0 \n12. Fazilka (EAAA)*, Ganganagar and \nHanumangarh Districts \nPunjab and \nRajasthan \n49 0 \n13. Bikaner and Churu Districts  \nRajasthan \n81 0 \n14. Jhunjhunu, Sikar and Nagaur Districts 171 0 \n15. Dausa, Karauli, SawaiMadhopur and \nTonk Districts \n124 0 \n16. Bhiwadi (in Alwar District)  NA 1 \n17. Firozabad (Taz Trapezium Zone) Uttar Pradesh and \nRajasthan \nNA 36 \n*EAAA: Except Area Already Authorized. \nNA stands for Targets not assigned. \nSource: PNGRB \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |71", "t": "Financial Assistance to North East to deal with Annual Floods", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *71 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nTAWI AND KOSI-MECHI RIVER PROJECTS \n†*71  SHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA  SHRIMATI NAVNEET R AVI RANA \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the time by which the Tawi and Kosi-Mechi river projects are likely to be started and completed; \n(b) whether the Government is taking cognizance of the rivers and nallahs affected by these projects; \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the measures taken for the same; \n(d) whether the Government is taking cognizance of the people likely to be affected who would require \nrehabilitation or to whom compensation is being deferred; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI   \n \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED QUESTION \nNO. *71 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 21.07.2022 R EGARDING “TAWI AND KOSI-\nMECHI RIVER PROJECTS”  \n  \n(a)  The Kosi-Mechi intra-State link envisages diversio n of part of surplus water of river Kosi for \nextending irrigation in new command areas of Mahana nda basin lying in the districts of Bihar by \nextending existing Eastern Kosi Main Canal up to ri ver Mechi so that the rivers that flow through Biha r \ncould be linked together. The techno-economic clear ance and investment clearance of the project has \nbeen accorded by Department of Water Resources, Riv er Development and Ganga Rejuvenation. The \nclearances of Ministry of Environment, Forest and C limate Change (MoEFCC) have also been accorded \nto the project. Further, High Powered Steering Comm ittee of Government of India has recommended this \nproject for inclusion in the scheme of National Pro jects. The project is to be implemented by the Stat e \nGovernment of Bihar and completion period as per DPR of the project is five years.  \nThe Government of UT of Jammu & Kashmir has taken u p project viz. Development of Tawi Riverfront \nProject which involves construction of embankments with stepped retaining wall and diaphragm wall for \na length of 2.7 km on both sides of river. The project has completion period of eighteen months.  \n(b) & (c)  During planning stage itself and while formulation  of Detailed Project Report (DPR) of  river \nvalley project, cognizance of the rivers, nallahs, water bodies, etc. is taken into consideration and \nprovision for cross drainage structures such as sip hons, aqueducts, river over bridge is accordingly m ade \nin the project. This aspect has been duly considere d while according techno-economic clearance of Kosi -\nMechi intra-State Link. Jammu Smart City Limited, executing project on river Tawi, has informed that a ll \nthe drainage nallahs (3 on the right bank and 1 on the left bank) carrying rainwater and effluent lade n \nwater are planned to be diverted to STPs through in terceptor drains to be laid parallel to the flow on  both \nsides of the riverbanks. \n \n(d) & (e)  In respect of Kosi-Mechi link project, the State G overnment of Bihar has informed that \nprovisions of compensation to affected/ rehabilitat ed people is incorporated in the DPR of the project  as \nper the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency  in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and \nResettlement Act, 2013. While for the Tawi river Pr oject, Jammu Smart City Limited executing project \nhas informed that this project is being executed in  the river basin entirely on the State Land and as such \nno private land is being acquired. There are 13 str uctures located on the left bank of river Tawi. Out  of \nthese 4 structures belong to the government agencie s and 9 structures belong to private individuals fo r \nwhich compensation as per norms is required to be paid.  \n  \n***  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |710", "t": "Naya Savera Scheme", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 710 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \n \nNAYA SAVERA SCHEME \n \n710. SHRI PALLAB LOCHAN DAS : \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether Naya Savera Scheme has been implemented in Assam; \n(b) if so, the number of beneficiaries under this scheme in Assam especially in Tezpur \nParliament Constituency, year-wise; \n(c) the details of funds allocated to North Eastern States under various schemes \nimplemented by the Government during the last three years; and \n(d) the details of funds allocated to and spent by North Eastern States especially Assam \nfor the said schemes during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS  \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Minority Affairs implements Free Coaching and Allied Scheme \n(Naya Savera) to assist students/candidates belonging to the six notified minority communities \nnamely Sikh, Jain, Muslim, Christian, Buddhist and Parsi by way of special coaching for \nqualifying examinations for admission in technical/professional courses and competitive \nexamination for recruitment to Group ‘A’, ‘B’, & ‘C’ services and other equivalent posts under \nthe Central and State Governments including public sector undertakings, banks, insurance \ncompanies as well as autonomous bodies. The scheme is implemented across the country \nincluding Assam through empaneled Project Implementing Agencies (PIAs)  / Non-\nGovernment Organizations (NGOs) since 2007-08.  \n  \n\n \n \n(b) 100 students were allocated for two centers in Kamrup district of Assam State during 2017-\n18 and another 100 students in 2019-20 under the Naya Savera scheme. \n(c)& (d).Details of funds allocated and released to North Eastern states including Assam for various \nschemes implemented by North Eastern Council (NEC), Ministry of Development of North Eastern \nRegion, during the last three (3) years are given at Annexure-I. \nApart from the above, Ministry of Minority Affairs also implements various schemes for \nsocio-economic empowerment for the six notified minority communities. The details of funds \nreleased by the Ministry in the North Eastern region including Assam, during the last three years \nare given in Annexure-II. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (c) & (d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 710 ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 ASKED BY SHRI PALLAB LOCHAN \nDAS REGARDING “NAYA SAVERA SCHEME” \nSummary of funds allocated for various schemes implemented by NEC during the last three \nyears w.e.f 2019-20 to 2021-22. \n(Rs. in crore) \nSl No FY Budget \nEstimates \n(BE)          \nActual \nAllocation (AA) \n \nActual \nExpenditure \n(AE) \n \nExpenditure \nover Allocation \n(%) \n1. 2019-20 1476.28 1243.28 1243.28 100 \n2. 2020-21 1474.49 852.52 852.52 100 \n3. 2021-22 1135.04 1133.55 1133.27 99.98 \n Total 4085.81 3229.35 3229.07 99.99 \n \nSummary of funds released to North Eastern States from 2019-20 to  2021-22 under various \nschemes  of NEC \n(Rs. in crore) \nS. No. State Name FY 2019-20 FY 2020-21 FY 2021-22 Total \n1 Arunachal \nPradesh \n51.26 19.35 94.00 164.61 \n2 Assam 14.54 16.89 66.19 97.62 \n3 Manipur 45.13 22.10 97.41 164.64 \n4 Meghalaya 54.18 15.76 206.42 276.36 \n5 Mizoram 52.82 57.40 97.82 208.04 \n6 Nagaland 36.91 35.63 239.74 312.28 \n7 Sikkim 33.02 5.85 71.73 110.60 \n8 Tripura 17.14 14.03 184.93 216.10 \n9 Other Agencies  938.28 665.51 75.03 1678.82 \nTotal 1243.28 852.52 1133.27 3229.07 \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXUREREFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (c) & (d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 710 ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 ASKED BY SHRI PALLAB LOCHAN \nDAS REGARDING “NAYA SAVERA SCHEME” \nDetails of funds released under various schemes of Ministry of Minority Affairs in the \nNorth Eastern Region during the last three years w.e.f. 2019-20 to 2021-22.  \nS.No. Name of Scheme Fund released in NER \n(Rs in Cr) \nFund released in \nAssam state(Rs in \nCr) \n1 Pre-Matric Scholarship 565.56 352.11 \n2 Post -Matric Scholarship 156.30 109.06 \n3 Merit-cum-Means Scholarship 87.18 63.26 \n4 PMJVK 1182.83 32.77 \n5 Nai Manzil 24.77 14.19 \n6 Naya Savera  2.17 0.26 \n7 NMDFC 30.57 - \n Total 2049.38 571.65 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |712", "t": "Black Spots on NHs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 712 \nANSWERED ON 21st JULY, 2022 \n  \nBLACK SPOTS ON NHs \n  \n712. DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY:  \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details and the number of National Highways (NHs) in the State \nof Tamil Nadu;  \n(b) the number of black spots on NHs in the State as on date;  \n(c) whether it is a fact that the State of Tamil Nadu has the most number \nof accident prone locations on NHs;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and  \n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to reduce the number \nof such black spots/locations? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) There are 50 number of National Highways (NHs) with a total length \nof 7000 Km in the State of Tamil Nadu.   \n\n(b)to(d) Total 5803 nos. of Roa d accident Black spots have been \nidentified in the Country during the period 2016 to 2018 by Transport \nResearch Wing of this Ministry. Out of these, 753 nos. of Black spots \nhave been identified in the State of Tamil Nadu which is the highest in \nthe Country.  \nRoad Accidents occur due to multiple causes such as over \nspeeding, drunken driving/consumption of alcohol, driving on wrong \nside/lane indiscipline, fault of the driver of motor vehicle, use of mobile \nphones during driving, mechanical defect/fault of the vehicle defect in \nroad condition, weather condition and lack of knowledge on road \nsignage and poor geometric of roads, non-existing of VUP, street light, \ncrash barrier etc. which form black spots. These various factors can \nbroadly be categorized into huma n error, road \ncondition/environment/Engineering and vehicular condition that lead to \naccidents on roads.  \n(e) Ministry has taken following major steps to improve road safety \ninfrastructure through its implementing agencies i.e NHAI, NHIDCL and \nNH wings of State PWDs:- \ni. To carry out all stage (Design, construction, and operation) road \nsafety audits for all National Highways; \nii. Provision of Rumble strips or bar markings at the approaches \nof junctions on NHs; \niii. Provision of Speed Limit signs at desired locations of NHs \nnetwork; \niv. Provision of Speed breakers and associated signage on side roads; \nv. Provision of amber beacons for traffic approaching a junction as \nper IRC; \nvi. Installation of Crash barriers on High embankments and in hilly \nterrain; \nvii. Encouraging engineers for certificate course in road safety audit \nfor which MOUs are signed with IITs and other leading technical \neducation/research institutions; \n\nviii. Developed a Black spot MIS portal where details of all black spots, \nids, photographs, and rectification status  and post rectification \nfeedback shall be collected and monitored. \n  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |713", "t": "Oil and Gas Drilling Operations", "head": "Page 1 of 1 \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 713 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON21st July, 2022 \n \nOil and Gas Drilling Operations \n \n713. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n SHRI MANOJ KOTAK: \n     \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम एवं \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to ease out restrictions imposed on oil & natural Gas \ndrilling operations in the ecologically fragile zone along coastal stretches;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the proposed plan for drilling operations in the Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ)? \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर  तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b):  Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEF&CC) has issued \nCoastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) Notification on 18 thJanuary, 2019 for regulating activities in \nCoastal stretches of the Country. Exploration and extraction of oil and natural gas and all \nassociated activities and facilities are covered under Para 5.1.2 sub Clause (xiii) relating to \nRegulation of permissible activities in CRZ.For further streamlining the process of granting \nCRZ clearance, MoEF&CC has issued a draft Notification in November,2021 for amending the \nexisting notification.  \n \n(c):  Oil India Limited and Oil & Natural Gas Corporation Limited has identified 13sites for \ndrilling wells in the Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) of Andhra Coast / KG Offshore basin. \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |714", "t": "Number of Slums", "head": " \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 714 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nNUMBER OF SLUMS \n \n \nNO. 714. SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK:  \nSHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK: \nSHRIMATI GEETA KORA: \nSHRIMATI RAMA DEVI: \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA: \nSHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \nMS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI JYOTIRMAY SINGH MAHATO: \nSHRIMATI NAVNEET RAVI RANA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)   the number of people living in slums in the country, State-wise including \nWest Bengal, Maharashtra and Parliamentary Constituency Amravati;  \n(b)  the number of slums demolished during the last  three years and the \namount of rehabilitation compensation given, State and year-wise and \nthe details in respect of Parliamentary Constituency Amravati;  \n(c)  the details of the funds used for the construc tion of low cost houses \nduring the last three years and the number of house s constructed so \nfar, State and year-wise; and \n(d)  the amount of funds allocated, approved and re leased to Parliamentary \nConstituency Amravati for the construction of house s and the number \nof houses constructed during the last three years, State and year-wise? \n       ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a):  As per Census 2011, 6.54 crore people are liv ing in slums in the country. \nThe State-wise details of people living in slums in cluding West Bengal \nand Maharashtra including Amravati are at Annexure-I.  \n...2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \n(b): Land and colonization are State subjects. Ther efore, schemes related to \nslums are implemented by State/ Union Territories (UTs). Data pertaining \nto number of slums demolished and amount of rehabil itation \ncompensation released in the country is not maintained by this Ministry.  \n(c):  The details of the fund used for the construc tion of low cost houses \nunder Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (PMAY-U) during th e last three years \nState-wise and year-wise and the number of houses c onstructed is at \nAnnexure-II. \n(d): The details of funds sanctioned and released t o Parliamentary \nConstituency Amravati for the construction of house s during the last \nthree years and the number of constructions is at Annexure- III.  \n***** \n\n \nAnnexure-I referred in reply to LS USQ No. 714 due for answer on 21.07.2022 \nState-wise details of people living in slums including West Bengal and Maharashtra as per \nCensus of India 2011 Slum Population as per Census \n \n \nNote: Estimate not presented as the number of sample slum is less than 10.  \n ‘NS’ indicates slum not reported. \n* Number of slums in small States/UTs is not availa ble, however the total number of slums in the count ry as per NSSO \nsurveys (58 \nth  & 69 th ) is 51,688 and 33,510 respectively.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nStates/ UTs  Slum Population as per Census 2011 \nAndhra Pradesh 1,01,86,934 \nArunachal Pradesh 15,562 \nAssam  1,97,266 \nBihar 12,37,682 \nChhattisgarh  18,98,931 \nGoa  26,247 \nGujarat 16,80,095 \nHaryana 16,62,305 \nHimachal Pradesh 61,312 \nJammu & Kashmir  6,62,062 \nJharkhand  3,72,999 \nKarnataka      32,91,434 \nKerala 2,02,048 \nMadhya Pradesh 56,88,993 \nMaharashtra 1,18,48,423 \nManipur NS  \nMeghalaya 57,418 \nMizoram 78,561 \nNagaland 82,324 \nOdisha      15,60,303 \nPunjab 14,60,518 \nRajasthan     20,68,000 \nSikkim 31,378 \nTamil Nadu 57,98,459 \nTripura 1,39,780 \nUttar Pradesh 62,39,965 \nUttarakhand 4,87,741 \nWest Bengal 64,18,594 \nA& N Islands 14,172 \nChandigarh 95,135 \nD & N Haveli NS  \nDaman & Diu NS  \nDelhi 17,85,390 \nLakshadweep NS  \nPuducherry  1,44,573 \nAll India * 6,54 ,94 ,604  \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure-II referred in reply to LS USQ No. 714 due for answer on 21.07.2022 \nState/ UT-wise details of houses completed and Cent ral Assistance released during each of \nthe last three                        years (FY 2019-20 to 2021-22) under PMAY-U \n \nS. No.        State/ UT  Houses Completed (Nos.)    Central Assistance Released \n(₹ in Crore) \n2019 -20  2020 -21  2021 -22  2019 -20  2020 -21  2021 -22  \n1 A&N Islands  - 23  1 0.17  0.46  1.06  \n2 Andhra Pradesh  30,100  98,115  64,352  918.78  2,419.06  2,475.25  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  385  1,222  556  21.31  8.57  27.70  \n4 Assam  3,953  10,245  15,663  494.46  125.57  180.48  \n5 Bihar  13,229  23,628  13,184  528.23  572.14  93.37  \n6 Chandigarh  363  406  144  8.24  9.18  3.45  \n7 Chhattisgarh  35,423  48,442  13,575  724.64  690.18  380.89  \n8 D&NH and D&D  1,483  1,811  1,127  35.90  45.57  26.06  \n9 Delhi  6,320  6,311  1,748  144.27  145.09  44.65  \n10  Goa  425  1,579  358  9.82  37.00  9.17  \n11  Gujarat  1,11,871  1,64,759  1,62,709  2,254.24  3,241.67  4,192.91  \n12  Haryana  10,644  19,008  7,074  247.72  290.17  172.77  \n13  Himachal Pradesh  1,268  1,877  1,681  29.96  32.81  46.49  \n14  J&K  1,877  3,643  3,758  99.78  131.54  43.67  \n15  Jharkhand  12,775  24,029  10,985  331.12  535.22  260.35  \n16  Karnataka  30,591  66,857  27,1"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |715", "t": "Construction of Underpass on NH-44", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 715 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2022 \n  \nCONSTRUCTION OF UNDERPASS ON NH-44 \n  \n715.    SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n  \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n                                                 सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government/NHAI has received any request for \n construction of underpass on National Highway -44 in the \n Parliamentary Constituency Karnal and Panipat district at various \n locations such as Bhopura Road Hathwala, Jhattipura, Samalakha \n railways station and Radha Swami Satsang Bhawan, Nilokheri, \n Kambopura, Chhajpur Kalan and adjoining villages; \n(b) if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to undertake the said projects on \n priority basis and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the time by which the construction work of the underpass at the said \n locations is likely to be started and completed? \nANSWER \n THE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) to (d)        Yes Sir.  The status of Vehicular Underpasses (VUP) on the \ndemanded locations is as under: - \n  \nSr. \nNo. \nLocation Status \n1 Bhopura Road Hathwala The Hathwala  Road T -junction falls at Km \n72.400 of NH -44.  The VUP has already been \nconstructed and opened for traffic. \n\n2 Jhattipura The Jhattipur village road T -junction falls at \nKm 80.00 of NH -44.  VUPs at Km 78.778 and \nat Km 81.938 have already been constructe d \nand opened for traffic. \n3 Samalakha Railway \nStation \nT-junction of NH -44 and Samalkha Railway \nRoad is at Km 73.200 of NH -44. Samalkha \nRailway Road is not a National Highway. A \nFlyover has already been constructed at Km \n73.200 of NH-44. \n4 Radha Swami Satsang \nBhawan \n  \nThe analysis to provide VUP at this location \nhas been initiated \n5 Nilokheri \n  \nThe requirement of additional underpass was \nnot found feasible as 2 flyovers at Km 143.200 \nand Km 147.160 were already constructed in \naddition to 3 Foot over Bridges (FoB) adjacent \nto Village Butana. \n6 Kambopura \n  \nConstruction of Foot over Bridge (FoB) at Km \n117.900 is scheduled for completion by \nSeptember, 2022. \n7 Chhajpurkalan \n  \nVillage Chhajpur Kalan is located about 800 m \nfrom NH 709AD. VUP has already been \nconstructed at said location in the work of \nNH-709AD (Package-I). \n8 Adjoining Village -\nGanjbar \n  \nConstruction of Foot over Bridge (FoB) at Km \n99.680 near Ganjbar  Village is scheduled for \ncompletion by September, 2022. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |716", "t": "Skill Development Initiative Scheme", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 716 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT INITIATIVE SCHEME \n \n  \n716.   SHRI RAJU BISTA \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of Skill Development Initiative Scheme (Seekho Aur Kamao) \ninitiated in the State of West Bengal; \n(b) the number and names of project implementing agencies engaged with the \nscheme in the State, district-wise; and \n(c) whether there is any data on the number of trained youth who have been \nabsorbed in employment or entrepreneurial venture and if so, the details thereof \nespecially in Darjeeling and Kalimpong districts ?  \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a)   The Scheme ‘Seekho aur Kamao’ (Learn & Earn) was launched on 23rd September, \n2013. It is a Central Sector Scheme (CSS) seeking to upgrade the skills of minority youth in \nvarious modern NSQF compliant job roles. The scheme is implemented across all the \nStates of the Union of India including West Bengal through empanelled Project \nImplementing Agencies (PIAs). The beneficiaries  are from  the six notified  minority \ncommunities between 14-45 years of age  who have completed Class V.  33% seats are \nearmarked for girls/women. Stipend  is provided at the rate of  Rs. 2,000/- per month for \nnon-residential trainees and Rs. 1000/- per month for residential trainees.  Post Placement \nSupport of Rs. 2000/- per month for two months is paid to trainees who have received \nplacement, to help the candidates in settling down and taking care of their needs in the \nstarting months of employment. \n \n(b)   46 empanelled  PIAs have implemented the scheme in West Bengal. District-wise list is \nas follows : \n \n  \n\nDistrict PIA Projects District wise \n24 Paraganas North \nAdwings Consultancy And Solutions Private Limited \nAisect \nAl-Ameen Charitable Fund Trust \nAscensive Educare Private Limited \nAshpra Skills Private Limited \nAssocom India Private Limited \nBright Future.Com \nBritti Prosikshan Pvt. Ltd. \nAlipurduar Britti Prosikshan Pvt. Ltd. \nBankura Britti Prosikshan Pvt. Ltd. \nBirbhum \nBritti Prosikshan Pvt. Ltd. \nCradle Life Sciences Pvt Ltd \nDum Dum Nikhil Bangiya Vidyapeeth \nCoochbehar Dum Dum Nikhil Bangiya Vidyapeeth \nDarjeeling \nEdujobs Academy Pvt Ltd \nElectronics Corporation Of India Limited \nGhousia Industrial And Engineering Trust \nDinajpur Dakshin \nIacm Smart Learn Limited \nIca Edu Skills Pvt Ltd \nIl And Fs Skill Development Corporation Ltd. \nDinajpur Uttar \nIl And Fs Skill Development Corporation Ltd. \nInderprastha College Of Engineering And Management \nIndira Gandhi Computer Shakshrta Mission \nHooghly Indira Gandhi Computer Shakshrta Mission \nIndus Integrated Information Management Limited \nHowrah \nIndus Integrated Information Management Limited \nInfovalley Educational And Research Privatelimited \nJanhit Sanskritik Kala Kendra \nJatuya Education Foundation \nJalpaiguri \nJatuya Education Foundation \nJitm Skills Private Limited \nKaruna \nKalimpong Karuna \nKolkata \nKeertika Academy Pvt. Ltd \nMalda Education Guide Society \nMarg Erp Limited \nMartyr Flight Leiutinent Maheesh Trikha Foundation \n \n\nMaldah \nMartyr Flight Leiutinent Maheesh Trikha Foundation \nMicrochip Solutions \nNic Institute Of Technology \nMedinipur East Now Nurturing Ones Willpower Foundation \nOrion Edutech, \nMedinipur West Orion Edutech, \nMurshidabad \nOrion Edutech, \nPipal Tree Ventures Pvt. Ltd \nPower To Empower Skills Pvt. Ltd. \nPyzek Polytechnic \nRasulpur Institute Of Study \nSarvshresth Times \nShaktipada Bhattacharjee Technical Institute \nNadia \nShaktipada Bhattacharjee Technical Institute \nShri Krishna Gramouthan Samiti \nSmd Technologies \nPaschim Bardhaman \nSmd Technologies \nSociety For Charity Obligation Technology And Training \nSociety For The School Of Medical Technology \nPurba Bardhaman Society For The School Of Medical Technology \nTaori Trust \nPurulia Vivekanand Paryavaran Evam Arogya Mission \nVlcc Healthcare Limited, \nSiliguri Vlcc Healthcare Limited, \nYashi Films Pvt. Ltd. \n  \n(c)  As per data available,  32492 beneficiaries have been trained in  employable \nand entrepreneurial skills in the State of West Bengal. In the Districts of Darjeeling \nand Kalimpong,1122 & 88 beneficiaries have been trained respectively,  out  of \nwhich 823 in Darjeeling  & 77 in Kalimpong have opted for placement and were \nprovided employment \n**** \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |717", "t": "Oxygen Plants Set-up by Oil and Gas Companies", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 717 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st JULY, 2022 \n \nOxygen Plants Set-up by Oil and Gas Companies \n \n†717.  SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI:  \n SHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of Oxygen plants set up by the oil and gas companies in the country during \nthe last three years and are operational as on date;  \n \n(b) the number of plants operational in Rajasthan and the number of plants likely to be made \noperational; and  \n \n(c) the quantity of oxygen being produced in these plants everyday along with the details of \nthe sectors including hospitals to whom the oxygen is being supplied? \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी   \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर  तेल\u0000) \n \n       MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n \n(a) to (c)     Public Sector Oil & Gas Companies have set up 122 Pressure Swing \nAdsorption (PSA) Oxygen Plants in various Hospitals across  the country including 8 in \nRajasthan.  As per information received from them, out of these 122 Oxygen Plants, 121 \nOxygen Plants of total 99210 LPM capacity are operational including all 8 in Rajasthan.   \n \n            \n**** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |718", "t": "Unauthorised Colonies", "head": " \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 718 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \nUNAUTHORISED COLONIES \nNO. 718.  SHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK:  \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased \nto state: \n(a)  whether the Government is aware of the rapidly incr easing \nnumber of unauthorised/unplanned colonies in the city of Delhi;  \n(b)  if so, whether the Government had studied the compl iance of \nthe previous master plan before starting the new ma ster plan \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  the total number of unauthorised colonies regulariz ed in Delhi \nduring the last five years;  \n(d)  whether the Government has made any effort to asses s the \nsocio-economic impact due to the emergence of unaut horised \ncolonies and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  whether the Government has held talks with the Stat e \nGovernment to bring any proposal in this regard and  if so, the \ndetails thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI  KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)&(b):   Delhi Development Authority (DDA) has st ated that 1797 \nUnauthorised Colonies have been identified under th e \nNational Capital Territory of Delhi (Recognition of  Property \nRights of Residents in Unauthorised Colonies) Regul ations, \n2019. \n \n...2/- \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \n \nDDA has informed that in the baseline study for Mas ter Plan \nfor Delhi- 2041, the study for unauthorised colonie s was \ndone under the shelter head. \n \n(c)to(e):  No socio-economic impact assessment due to emergence of \nunauthorised colonies has been carried out. \n \nOn the recommendation of the Committee chaired by t he \nHon’ble Lieutenant Governor of Delhi, the Governmen t has \nframed the Prime Minister – Unauthorised Colonies i n Delhi \nAwas Adhikar Yojana (PM-UDAY) to confer the ownersh ip or \ntransfer/ mortgage rights to the residents of Unaut horised \nColonies in Delhi. Further, Development Control Norms (DCN) \nfor unauthorised colonies has been notified on 08.0 3.2022. \nImplementation of these measures will result in pla nned \nredevelopment and regularisation of unauthorised colonies. \n \nThe DCN has been prepared after due consultation wi th all \nrelevant stakeholders under provisions of the Delhi  \nDevelopment Act, 1957. \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |72", "t": "Increasing Cases of Forest Fires", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 72 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nSOLAR PUMPS INSTALLED UNDER PM-KUSUM \n+*72.SHRIMATI DELKAR KALABEN MOHANBHAI \n       SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of solar pumps installed under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja \nSuraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM-KUSUM) in Union Territories of \nDadra and Nagar Haveli, Daman and Diu and Maharashtra, especially in Nagpur-\nRamtek region;  \n(b)  the details of funds provided to the farmers for installation of solar pumps; \n(c)  the quantum of surplus electricity sold to the power distribution companies by the \nfarmers in the said State/UTs; \n(d)   the details of the size of agricultural land of farmers who installed such solar \npumps under the PM-KUSUM; \n(e)  the details of the ‘dark zones’ identified under the PM-KUSUM along with the \ncriteria laid down for the identification of such zones, State/UT-wise and the \nnumber of solar pumps installed/converted in the said ‘dark zones’; and \n(f)   the quantum of electricity generated by the farmers in these zones in the said \nState/UTs along with the revenue earned therefrom? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \nAND  CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n(a) to (f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (f) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. 72 (Priority No. 12) to be answered on 21.07.2022 regarding Solar Pumps \nInstalled under PM-KUSUM \n(a) to  (f) Under PM -KUSUM Scheme support for solar pumps is provided under \nComponent-B and Component -C. Under Component -B, support is provided for \ninstallation of standalone solar pumps. Under Component -C, support is provided for \nsolarization of existing grid -connected agriculture pumps, including agriculture feeder \nsolarization.  \nPM-KUSUM is a demand driven Scheme and therefore quantities of solar pumps are \nallocated to the States/ UTs based on demand received from the State Government/ UT \nAdministration. In absence of demand, solar pumps have not so far been  allocated to the \nUT of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu. In the State of Maharashtra, 100,000 \nnos. of solar pumps have been sanctioned under the Component-B of the Scheme, out of \nwhich 7713 nos. of solar pumps have been installed as on 18.07.2022. Of these, 92 nos. \nof solar pumps have been installed in Nagpur and 11 nos. of solar pumps have been \ninstalled in Ramtek taluka of Nagpur.  \nUnder Component -B & C of the Scheme, 30% of the benchmark cost or t he price \ndiscovered through the tender, whichever is lower, is provided by the Central \nGovernment and at least an equivalent amount is provided by the concerned State \nGovernment. Till date, Ministry has released Rs. 672 Crore to various States for \ninstallation of standalone solar pumps.  \nAs solar pumps installed in the State of Maharashtra are off -grid i.e. these are not \nconnected to the grid, no electricity could be sold to the power distribution companies \nand, therefore, no revenue is earned by the farmer s through selling surplus electricity to \nthe power producing companies.  However, farmers save through avoided diesel \nconsumption by installing standalone solar pumps.  \nBased on the information provided by the State of Maharashtra, average land holding \nsize with farmers who have installed solar pumps under PM-KUSUM Scheme is 3.2 acre.  \nDark zones (areas with over-exploited ground water resources) are identified by Central \nGround Water Board (CGWB) in association with the State ground water organisations.  \nThe ground water resources are assessed in units i.e blocks/ talukas/ mandals/ \nwatersheds. These assessment units are categorized for ground water development based \non two criteria - a) stage of ground water development, and b) long -term pre and post \nmonsoon water levels. CGWB has notified 162 Over -exploited areas in the country for \nregulation of ground water development and management.  State/ UT-wise details of the \npumps installed/ converted in the dark zones is placed at Annexure. \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) to (f) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. 72 (Priority No. 12) to be answered on 21.07.2022 \n \nSolar Pumps Installed in Notified Areas of Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) \n \nS. No. State No. of Pumps Installed in \nNotified Areas \n1 Gujarat 135 \n2 Haryana 456 \n3 Madhya Pradesh  185 \n4 Punjab  642 \n5 Rajasthan 8165 \n6 Tamil Nadu 480 \nTotal 10063 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |721", "t": "Social Audit for Housing Schemes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 721 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nSOCIAL AUDIT FOR HOUSING SCHEMES \n \n \nNO. 721. SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI:   \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)   whether the Government has conducted any social aud it for the \nhousing scheme Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana – Urban ( PMAY-U) to \nprevent the misappropriation of funds under the scheme;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, State-wise includi ng implementation status \nand performance of the scheme; and  \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n           ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)   to (c): Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoH UA) is implementing \nPradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urban (PMAY-U) since 2 015 to provide \nall weather pucca houses with all basic amenities f or all eligible \nfamilies/ beneficiaries. As per the Scheme guidelin es, Social Audit of \nthe sanctioned PMAY-U projects is mandatory before the release of \nthird installment of Central Assistance and is unde rtaken by States/ \nUnion Territories (UTs)/ Urban Local Bodies (ULBs).  Social Audit \nprovides the beneficiaries and other stakeholders a n opportunity to \nscrutinize the procedures and benefits of the Missi on. It affects better \nimplementation, ensures accountability, transparenc y and \nparticipation. Social Audit guidelines are availabl e at \nhttps://www.mohua.gov.in/upload/uploadfiles/files/7PMAY_Social_Audit_ \nGuidelines_2017$2017Apr25181455.pdf / \n...2/- \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \nMoHUA provides 100% financial assistance to States/  UTs for \nundertaking Social Audit. So far, this Ministry has  released funds to 21 \nStates/ UTs namely Andhra Pradesh, Arunachal Prades h,  Assam, Bihar, \nChhattisgarh, Gujarat, Jharkhand, Karnataka, Kerala , Madhya Pradesh, \nManipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Odisha, Pudu cherry, Tamil \nNadu, Tripura, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand and West Bengal for \ncarrying out Social Audit. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |723", "t": "Problems faced by Indian Muslims", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 723 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n  \nPROBLEMS FACED BY INDIAN MUSLIMS \n  \n723.  SHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government agrees with the view that the strategy to handle the \nproblems of social, political, economic and religious nature faced by Indian Muslims \nshould be a multi-pronged one; and \n(b) if so, the details of the initiatives proposed to be taken by the Government to \nidentify and analyze the issues, which afflict the community and seek out pragmatic \nsolutions, which may bode well both for the community and the country? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) & (b) The Government  implements various schemes for the welfare and upliftment \nof every section of the society including Muslims.  The various schemes being \nimplemented include the Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PMJAY), Pradhan Mantri \nMudra Yojana (PMMY), Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM KISAN), Pradhan \nMantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY), Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (PMAY), Beti Bachao Beti \nPadhao Yojana, etc. Also minorities belonging to EWS category can avail the benefit of \nten percent reservation in employment and admission in educational institutions. \n  \nFurther, the Ministry of Minority Affairs has adopted a multi-pronged strategy \nby way of implementation of various schemes which aim at educational empowerment, \nemployment-oriented skill development, infrastructure support, etc. for the upliftment \nof living standards of economically weaker & under privileged  sections of all notified \nminorities which include Muslims as well. The details of the schemes are: \n \nA. Educational Empowerment Schemes:- \n  \n(1) Pre-Matric Scholarship Scheme, Post-Matric Scholarship Scheme, Merit-cum-Means \nbased Scholarship Scheme - For educational empowerment of students through Direct \nBenefit Transfer (DBT) mode. \n\n(2) Maulana Azad National Fellowship Scheme - Provide fellowships in the form of \nfinancial assistance. \n(3) Naya Savera - Free Coaching and Allied Scheme - The Scheme aims to provide free \ncoaching to students/candidates belonging to minority communities for qualifying in \nentrance examinations of technical/ medical professional courses and various \nCompetitive examinations. \n(4) Padho Pardesh - Scheme of interest subsidy to students of minority communities on \neducational loans for overseas higher studies. \n(5) Nai Udaan - Support for students clearing Prelims conducted by Union Public \nService Commission (UPSC), State Public Service Commission (PSC) Staff Selection \nCommission (SSC) etc. \n  \nB. Employment oriented schemes:- \n  \n(6) Seekho Aur Kamao - Skill development scheme for youth of 14 - 35 years age group \nand aiming at providing employment and employment opportunities, improving the \nemployability of existing workers, school dropouts etc. \n(7) USTTAD (Upgrading the Skills and Training in Traditional Arts/Crafts for \nDevelopment). Hunar Haat are being organised across the Country to provide \nemployment opportunities and markets to artisans/craftsmen. \n(8) Nai Manzil - A scheme for formal school education & skilling of school dropouts. \n(9) Nai Roshni - Leadership development of women belonging to minority \ncommunities. \n  \nC. Infrastructure Development Programme:- \n  \n(10) Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK) – Being implemented to \nprovide infrastructure in the identified areas of the Country. Since inception of the \nscheme, the Ministry has sanctioned more than  49,000 major projects which includes 49 \nDegree Colleges, 179 Residential Schools, 3016 School Buildings, 42,269 Additional \nClass Rooms/ Libraries/ Laboratories/Halls etc, 15,659 Teaching Aids & Smart \nClassrooms, 7452 toilets in schools, 1393 Hostels, 28 Working Women Hostels, 207 ITI \nBuildings and additional facilities in 41 ITIs, 56 Polytechnics, 84 Skill Centres/ Hunar \nHubs, 6016 Health Projects, 591 Sadhbhav Mandaps/ Common Service Centres, 610 \nMarket Sheds, 3100 Sanitation/ Public Toilet projects, 92 Sports Facilities, etc. \n           \n          The details of the schemes (S. No.1 to 10) mentioned above and status of their \nimplementation are available on the website of this Ministry \n(www.minorityaffairs.gov.in). \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |724", "t": "Creativity and Entrepreneurship in MSME Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 724\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022\n \n \nCREATIVITY AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP IN MSME SECTOR\n \n \n724.\n \n \nSHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nMICRO, SMALL AND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has taken steps to promote creativity and entrepreneurship in \nMSME sector; \n \n(b)  \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has provided fi\nnancial assistance for innovation, design and \nprotection of Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) in MSME sector; and \n \n(d)  \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n(a)\n \n& (b): \n \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium \nEnterprises \n(MSME) has \ntaken \nseveral initiatives for providing an impetus to promote creativity, innovation and \nentrepreneurship. These inter\n-\nalia include \nEntrepreneurship and \nSkill Development \nProgramme (ESDP)\n,\n \nMSME Champions Scheme\n \nand \nPrime Minister`s Employment \nGeneration Programme (PMEGP) t\no motivate youth representing different sections of the \nsociety to consider self employment or entrepreneurship\n \nand  \nenhancing competiti\nveness of \nmanufacturing MSMEs in the country. \n \n(c) & (d): \n \nFinancial Assistance \nis being\n \nprovided for innovation, design and \nprotection of \nIntellectual Property Rights (IPR) in MSME sector under MSME Innovative component of \nMSME Champions Scheme launched o\nn 10.03.2022. \nDuring the current Financial Year \n2022\n-\n23, \n \n257 ideas \nunder\n \nMSME Idea Hackathon 2022, reimbursement of 1196 Trademarks, \n186 Patents, 53 Design and 11 Geographical Indication (GI) have been approved for \nproviding financial assistance \nwith an e\nstimated cost \nof Rs. \n34.00\n \nCrore.\n \n \n****\n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |725", "t": "Special Training Program for Youth", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 725 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022\n \n \nSPECIAL TRAINING PROGRAM FOR YOUTH\n \n†725. \n \nSHRIMATI SHARDABEN ANILBHAI PATEL:\n \n \nSHRI MITESH RAMESHBHAI PATEL \n(BAKABHAI):\n \nSHRI RAJAN VICHARE:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has organised/ conducted any special programme to impart \nentrepreneurship training for the youth especially for young wom\nen including those \nbelonging to Scheduled Castes/Scheduled Tribes for running small businesses in the country \nincluding Maharashtra ; and\n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP \nSINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a):\n \nThe objective of the Entrepreneurship and Skill Development Programme (ESDP) \nscheme of the Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises \nis to motivate youth \nrepresenting different sections of the society including SC/ST/Women, differently \nabled, Ex\n-\nservicemen and BPL persons to consider self employment or entrepreneurship as one of the \ncareer options. The main objective is to promote new enterprises, capacity building of \nexisting MSMEs and inculcating entrepreneurial culture in the country.\n \nIt is a Pan India \nScheme which also includes \nMaharashtra\n.\n \n \n(b):\n \nThe Year\n-\nwise details of the ESDP scheme training programmes conducted along \nwith the no. of beneficiaries are given in tabulated form.\n \n \nFinancial \nYear\n \nNo. of ESDP  \nprogrammes \nconducted\n \nTotal No. \nof \nBeneficiaries\n \nTotal No. of  \nScheduled \nCaste \nBeneficiaries\n \nTotal No. of  \nScheduled \nTribe \nBeneficiaries\n \nTotal no. of \nWomen \nBeneficiaries\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n265\n \n9505\n \n905\n \n351\n \n2781\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n5858\n \n233507\n \n11505\n \n4148\n \n101234\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n527\n \n27544\n \n1065\n \n600\n \n13640\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n703\n \n58659\n \n240\n \n1312\n \n24734\n \n \n \n******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |727", "t": "Accidents in ONGC Plants", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 727 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2022 \n \nAccidents in ONGC Plants \n  \n †727.  SHRI SHANKAR LALWANI: \n SHRIMATI QUEEN OJA: \nDR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL: \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the number of accidents has been rising in the ONGC plants during the last three \nyears; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and; \n \n(c) the details of the safety measures taken/likely to be taken by the Government in this \ndirection? \n \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n(\u0000ी रामे\u0000र तेली) \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b):  No Sir. \n \n(c): Oil Industry Safety Directorate (OISD), a technical directorate under the Ministry of \nPetroleum & Natural Gas (MoP&NG) formulates safety standards.  Oil and Gas companies \nfollow such standards and take suitable measures to ensure safety. OISD carries out regular \nsafety audits and update Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) regularly. MoP&NG has also \nreleased revised Disaster Management Plan (DMP) – 2021 incorporating lessons learnt from \nrecent incidents. Further, OISD has conducted webinars on ‘Safety Management System’ and \n‘Awareness program’ in 2021-22 for E&P operators.   \n  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |729", "t": "Manufacturing of Video Security Products", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 729\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022\n \n \nMANUFACTURING OF VIDEO SECURITY PRODUCTS\n \n \n729.\n \n \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has promoted the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises \n(MSMEs) manufacturing video security products under 'Make\n-\nIn\n-\nIndia' programme and \n'Atmanirbhar Bharat\n \nAbhiyan' to transform India into a global video security product design \n& manufacturing hub;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof,\n \n \nState\n-\nwise and if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has taken any steps to set\n-\nup video security product cl\nusters \nin the country; and\n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n \n(a) to (d): \n \nThe Government has started many programmes\n \nto facilitate investment, foster \ninnovation and \nmake India \na hub for manufacturing, design and innovation. Under the \n'Make\n-\nIn\n-\nIndia'\n,\n \nDepartment for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT) coordinates \naction plans for 15 manufacturing sectors, wh\nile Department of Commerce coordinates 12 \nservice sector plans. \nMinistry of Electronics & Information Technology (MeitY) runs \nModified Special Incentive Package Scheme (M\n-\nSIPS)\n, wherein it\n \nprovides 20\n-\n25% subsidy \nfor investments in capital expenditure for \nsetting up of an electronic manufacturing facility \n(20% for SEZ Units and 25% for non\n-\nSEZ Units). The incentives are available for 44 \ncategories of electronic products and product components on reimbursement basis.\n \n \n \n \nMinistry of Micro, Small and Medium En\nterprises (MSME) has no specific \nprogramme for manufacturing\n \nof \nvideo security products \nbut runs various schemes where \nsuch products can be designed, incubated, manufactured and marketed. These schemes \ninclude inter\n-\nalia MSME Innovative, \nMicro and Small Enterprises Cluster Development \nProgramme (MSE\n-\nCDP) and Prime Ministers’ Employment Generation Programme \n(PMEGP).\n \n \n \n****\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |73", "t": "Development of Tourist Locations in NER", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.73 \nTo be answered on \nMonday, July 18, 2022/Ashadha 27, 1944 (Saka) \nQUESTION \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST LOCATIONS IN NER \n \n73.      SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \nWill the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state: \n(a)  whether the Government has any plans to develop  tourist locations in the North-\nEast Region (NER) particularly in Assam, and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government p roposes to consider  connecting important tourist \nlocations in NER, thus creating a tourism trail, if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government has any development  plan centring around Dokreswar  \nDewalay, Chenga in  Barpeta District and Bageshwari Temple at Bongaigaon,  if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n(d)  the efforts made/being made by the Government to promote the region abroad. \n \nANSWER \nThe Minister of  the Ministry of Development of  North Eastern Region \n[SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY] \n \n(a) & (b) The Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region (DoNER) under the \nNorth East Special Infrastructure Development Scheme (NESIDS) has  sanctioned \ninfrastructure projects relating to water supply, power, connectivity to support the  \ntourism sector in the north east region (NER) . Under NESIDS, 51 projects amounting to \n₹1,647 crore connecting tourist locations in the NER have been sanctioned . Of these, \n10 projects amounting to ₹637.14 crore are in the state of Assam. \nThe North Eastern Council (NEC), Shillong undertakes  activities for the  \npromotion of NER and regional tourism , which are priority sectors under the ‘Schemes \nof NEC’. Under this, the NEC has sanctioned 57 projects amounting ₹249.96 crore in \nthe tourism sector  in the last seven years . Further, 29 tourist circuits have been \n\nidentified by NEC and shared with the Ministry of Tourism. \nThe Ministry of Tourism  has sanctioned tourist circuit projects, amounting to \n₹1,254.07 crore in the NER under its ‘Swadesh Darshan’ scheme since 2014 -15. One \nproject has been sanctioned for Inland Waterways Authority of India (IWAI) under the \nscheme ‘Assistance to Central Agencies for Tourism Infrastructure Development ’ by \nthe Ministry of Tourism , for development of nine points of river cruise on national \nwaterways no. 1 & 2, out of which , four points, namely Neamati, Pandu, Jogighopa and \nBiswanath ghat are in Assam. Further, since 2014 -15 the Ministry of Tourism has \nprovided financial assistance to State Governments/Union Territory (UT) Administrators \nfor organizing fairs and festivals and tourism related events in the  NER. Under the \nCentral Sector Scheme of ‘National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, \nHeritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD) ’, the Minist ry of Tourism funds projects for \nthe integrated development of identified pilgrimage and heritage destinations. As on \ndate 69 destinations/sites have been identified for development under the scheme in \n30 states/UTs. Of these, 20 sites are in the  NER, which include Kamakhya temple and \nSrikrishnaguru Sevashram, Nasatra in Assam. \n(c) No Sir, currently, the central government has no tourism development plans in \nthese areas. \n(d) India Tourism Offices Overseas along with Indian Missions abroad endeavour  to \nposition India as a preferred tourism destination and promote various tourism products \nand destinations including those in the NER and to increase India’s share in the global \ntourism market. The Ministry of Tourism under the Marketing Development Assis tance \n(MDA) programme extends financial support to approved tourism service providers and \nState Governments/UT Administra tors for undertaking tourism promotion in the \noverseas markets. On the request of the Ministry of Tourism, the Ministry of External \nAffairs has Tourism Officers in the Indian Missions of top 20 source markets in 2021. \nThe Ministry of Tourism has also conducted virtual road shows under the ‘Incredible \nIndia Reconnect 2022’ in Oman, United States of America, Germany, Australia, \nSingapore, T hailand, Spain and the United Kingdom to project India as a preferred \ntourism destination. \nVarious tourism promoting events and festivals etc. such as the ‘Sangai Festival’, \nManipur’, ‘Behdienkhlam Festival ’, Meghalaya’ and ‘Hornbilll Festival ’, Nagaland are \nsupported by NEC to attract tourists from different parts of the country as well as \nabroad.  \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |730", "t": "Upgradation of SHs into NHs", "head": "  \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  No. 730 \nANSWERED ON 21st JULY, 2022 \n  \nUPGRADATION OF SHs INTO NHs \n  \n730.    SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \n  \n       Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्गमंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of roads/State Highways (SHs) identified for upgradation \nas National Highways (NHs) in the State of Karnataka during the last \nthree years and the current year;  \n(b) the present status of work on upgradation of roads/SHs in the State;  \n(c) the target set for completion of the said upgradation work; and  \n(d) the details of funds allocated and spent thereon during the said \nperiod?  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n  \n(Shri NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n (a) to (c)   Ministry keeps on receiving proposals for declaration of State \nRoads as National Highways from different States and Union Territories \nincluding from the State of Karnataka.  Ministry considers some of these \nproposals for declaration as National Highways depending on the \nrequirements of connectivity, traffic levels, inter -se priority and \navailability of resources.   During the last three years and the current \nyear, 3 State Road/ Green Field Alignments received in 2021 -22 with \n\nlengths of 54 km, 77km and 177 km falling in the  state of Karnataka \nhave been declared as NH-167N, NE-7 and NH-150C respectively.   \n  \n(d) On NE-7, works in 77.21 km length costing Rs. 4444.39 crore are in \nprogress in 3 packages as part of Bangalore -Chennai Expressway \ncorridor in the State of Karnataka b y NHAI and two of these packages \nare   scheduled to be completed by September, 2023 and the third one \nby  January 2024. An expenditure of Rs 2112.64 Crore has so far been \nincurred on these 3 packages. On NH 150C, 2 works covering a length \nof  112 km  costing Rs 2425.40 crore have been recently awarded and \none work in 65.5 km length costing Rs 2104 crore as part of Surat -\nChennai Expressway corridor in the State of Karnataka is in bidding \nstage by NHAI. \n ***** \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |731", "t": "Removal of Slums", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 731 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nREMOVAL OF SLUMS \n \n \nNO. 731. DR. HEENA GAVIT:   \nDR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \nDR. KRISHNA PAL SINGH YADAV: \nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \nPROF. RITA BAHUGUNA JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)   the details of programmes introduced by the Governm ent for the \nremoval of slums in the country during the last three years, year-wise;  \n(b)  whether the Government has achieved its target  by successfully \nimplementing the programmes and if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government is taking any steps to bring in effective laws \nand policies to handle large scale migration from r ural areas, slum \nrehabilitation, etc. in the country; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the rea sons therefor; and \n(e) whether the Government is contemplating any fur ther steps to tackle \nthese issues and if so, the details thereof? \n           ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)   to (e): Land and colonization are State subjects. Therefore, schemes \nrelated to slum rehabilitation and affordable housi ng for addressing \nmigration from rural areas and promoting inclusive urban development \nare implemented by States/ Union Territories (UTs) through their \nimplementing agencies. In addition, Ministry of Hou sing and Urban Affairs \n(MoHUA) is supplementing the efforts of States/ UTs by providing Central  \n...2/- \n \n \n\n \n-2- \nAssistance under Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana – Urban  (PMAY-U) since \n25.06.2015 for construction of houses for all eligi ble beneficiaries \nincluding slum dwellers and rural migrants. Based o n the project \nproposals submitted by States/ UTs, so far more tha n 122 lakh houses \nhave been sanctioned in all urban areas, out of whi ch more than 101 lakh \nhave been grounded and more than 62 lakh have been completed/ \ndelivered to the beneficiaries. Data pertaining to removal of slums in the \ncountry is not maintained by this Ministry.  \nFurther, the Ministry has launched Affordable Renta l Housing Complexes \n(ARHCs), a sub-scheme under PMAY-U. ARHCs aim to pr ovide low-cost \ndignified rental accommodation with necessary civic  amenities for the \nurban migrants/ poor near their workplace. ARHCs wi ll create a \nsustainable ecosystem of affordable rental housing solutions for \nmigrants, thereby preventing the growth of future s lums. The Ministry has \nalso shared the Model Tenancy Act (MTA) 2021 with t he States/ UTs for \nadoption by either legislating the new laws or amen ding existing rental \nlaws on the lines of MTA for adoption. It will enab le creation of adequate \nrental housing stock for all the income groups incl uding migrants from \nrural areas.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |732", "t": "Creation of Jobs by KVIC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 732\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2022\n \n \nCREATION OF JOBS BY KVIC\n \n \n†732.\n \nSHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \n \n \n \nSHRI SANJAY JADHAV: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether jobs have been created as a result of the efforts made by the Khadi and \nVillage Industries Commission (KVIC); \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof especially in Maharashtra with special reference to Parbha\nni \nParliamentary Constituency; \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has any record of the number of artisans who lost their job \nduring lockdown; \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof and the number of artisans who again got their job; \n \n(e) \n \nwhether any scheme has been \nimplemented by KVIC to provide financial assistance \nto BPL artisans and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f) \n \nthe initiative taken by the KVIC to create more jobs?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH \nVERMA)\n \n \n(a):\n \nYes Sir, the \nMinistry of MSME, through \nKhadi and Village Industries Commission \n(KVIC) promotes and assists Khadi and Village Industries which are engaged in production \nand sales of KVI products thereby generating employment opportunities for r\nural artisans \nthrough implementation of various schemes and programmes.\n \n \nThe cumulative employment generated by Khadi and Village Industries upto 31.\n0\n3.2022 is \ngiven below: \n \n \nYear\n \nCumulative Employment\n \n(in lakh persons)\n \nKhadi\n \nVillage Industries\n \nKVI\n \nAs \non 31.03.2022 \n(Provisional)\n \n4.97\n \n162.63\n \n167.6\n0\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nThe cumulative employment generated by Khadi and Village Industries in \nMaharashtra State upto 31.\n0\n3.2022 is given below:\n \n \nYear\n \nCumulative Employment in Maharashtra State\n \n(in lakh persons)\n \nKhadi\n \nVillage Industries\n \nKVI\n \nAs on 31.03.2022 \n(Provisional)\n \n0.03\n \n11.65\n \n11.68\n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \nFurther, Ministry of MSME\n \nthrough KVIC\n \nis implementing\n \nPrime Minister’s \nEmployment Generation Programme\n \n(PMEGP), which is a major credit\n-\nlinked subsidy \nprogramme, aimed at generating self\n-\nemployment opportunities\n \nfor the rural and urban \nunemployed persons through establishment of micro\n-\nenterprises in non\n-\nfarm sector, in the \ncountry including Parbhani Distri\nct of Maharashtra. Banks provide the loan, and subsidy \n(also called Margin Money subsidy) is provided by the Government of India through KVIC.\n \n \nUnder PMEGP, general category beneficiaries can avail benefit of margin money \nsubsidy of 25% of the project cost\n \nin rural areas and 15% in urban areas. For beneficiaries \nbelonging to special categories such as ST/SC/OBC/women/minorities/transgenders etc. the \nmargin money subsidy is 35% in rural areas and 25% in urban areas.\n \nBeneficiary contribution \nis 10% (General C\nategory) and 5% (Special Category).\n \nThe maximum projects cost is Rs. 50 \nlakh in the manufacturing sector and Rs. 20 lakh in the service sector.\n \n \nPerformance of PMEGP scheme in Parbhani District during last three years is as \nfollows:\n \n \nYear\n \nProject\n \n(in numbe\nrs)\n \nMargin Money released\n \n(Rs. in lakh)\n \nEmployment\n \n(in numbers)\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n87\n \n119.95\n \n696\n2020\n-\n21\n \n41\n \n66.42\n \n328\n2021\n-\n22\n \n73\n \n88.18\n \n584\n \n(c)\n \n&\n \n(d)\n:\n \nThe findings of a study \nconducted by KVIC to assess the impact of COVID\n-\n19 \nPandemic on MSMEs, including units set up under PMEGP, is placed at \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \n \n(e):\n \nKVIC is not implementing any specific scheme of financial assistance to BPL \nartisans. However, BPL artisans who are not\n \nable to put in their own contribution, are \nprovided training and tools & equipment under various programmes of Gramodyog Vikas \nYojana, like Honey Mission, Kumbhar Sashaktikaran Programme, Pilot project on Agarbatti, \netc.\n \n \n(f):\n \nKVIC has taken many initiati\nves for promoting, developing and strengthening the \nrural traditional industries in the country and for employment generation.  Further, KVIC, \nthrough its training centres and through other promotional schemes conducts Skill \nDevelopment Programme (SDP) and\n \nEntrepreneurship Awareness Programmes (EAP) in the \ncountry for unemployed youth to generate self\n-\nemployment opportunities in traditional \nindustries. Details of KVIC’s initiatives which are provided in \nAnnexure\n-\nII\n.\n \n \n \n* * * * *\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI referred to in reply to part (c)&(d) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.732 \nfor answer on 21.07.2022\n \n \n \nStudy conducted by Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) to assess the impact \nof COVID\n-\n19 Pandemic on MSMEs including units set u\np under Prime Minister’s \nEmployment Generation Programme (PMEGP).  The findings of the study are as under:\n \n \ni.\n \n88% of the beneficiaries of PMEGP scheme reported that they were negatively \naffected due to Covid\n-\n19 while the remaining 12% stated that they wer\ne "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |733", "t": "NBCC's Green View Project", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 733 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nNBCC’s GREEN VIEW PROJECT \n \nNO.  733.  SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate : \n \n(a)  Whether the residents of National Buildings Construction Corporation \n(NBCC’s) green view project have been asked to vaca te the building \ndue to poor quality of construction and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  the details of action taken by the Government against NBCC and the \ncontractor; \n \n(c)  the reasons for the absence of any oversight o n NBCC’s Green View \nproject; \n \n(d) whether the final EoT (Extension of Time) certi ficate was granted to \nthe contractor by Management of NBCC which led to t he release of \npayment for shoddy work; and \n \n(e) If so, whether the Government proposes to take any action in this \nregard and if so, the details thereof ? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a): Yes Sir. The residents have been asked to vaca te the NBCC Green \nView project on safety consideration as advised by Indian Institute of \nTechnology, Delhi vide their report dated 06.10.2021.  \n \nSubsequently, NBCC appointed an expert committee co mprising of two \nscientists each from Central Building Research Inst itute (CBRI), Roorkee \nand Indian Institute of Technology (IIT), Roorkee. The decision of NBCC \n(India) Ltd. asking residents to vacate NBCC Green View project was re-\naffirmed by the Expert Committee. The report of the  Expert Committee \nalso concluded that “ the building structures in th eir current condition \nare unsafe for any habitation. Therefore, it is rec ommended that the \npresent structures, highly contaminated with chlori des, may be \ndismantled/ demolished.” \n…2/- \n \n\n-2- \n          \n(b): In view of the complaints received from variou s homebuyers etc., \nCentral Vigilance Commission (Chief Technical Exami ner) conducted a \ntechnical examination of the project and based on t heir findings, 03 \nboard level and 07 below board level executives of NBCC have been \nissued charge sheets.  \nNBCC has taken following actions against the contractors: \nI.   M/s Supreme Infrastructure India Limited (SIIL) an d M/s Ramacivil \nIndia Construction Pvt Ltd. (RICPL) have been place d on Holiday List \n(i.e.blacklisted from participating in future tenders). Consultant of the \nproject M/s Behal Joshi and Third Party Quality Age ncy (TPQA) M/s \nTUV SUD have also been placed on Holiday List. \n \nII.  Bank guarantees and dues amounting to Rs. 75.35 cro res of M/s \nRICPL have been en-cashed / withheld from all their  running and \ncompleted projects in NBCC. \n \nIII.   All the running projects of M/s RICPL with NBCC ha ve been \nterminated.  \n(c): NBCC has adequate quality control mechanism in place.  However, in \nthis case, poor quality work by the contractor &       non-observance of \nlaid down quality assurance protocols by the concer ned officials are the \nmain reasons for lapses in this case. \n(d):  Final Extension of Time (EoT) to both the con tractors i.e. M/s \nSupreme Infrastructure India Limited and M/s Ramaci vil India \nConstruction Pvt. Ltd. has been granted with levy o f maximum \n‘compensation for delay’ in terms of the contract. Final bills of both the \ncontractors have not been released.    \n(e):  Disciplinary action has been initiated agains t 04 (four) officials of \nNBCC (India) Ltd. for not levying ‘compensation for  delay’ in the case of \nM/s Supreme Infrastructure India Limited in terms o f the contract \nagreement at the time of the termination of their contract. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |734", "t": "Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN Yojana", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 734 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21 st July, 2022 \nPradhan Mantri JI-VAN Yojana  \n734. SHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY:  \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n                               [ [ \n(a) the details of the funds allocated and utilised under Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN \nYojana since its inception; \n(b) whether there has been an underutilisation of funds under this yojana and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(c) the details of projects supported through the scheme along with their current \nstatus including Andhra Pradesh; \n(d) whether the yojana  has made 2G Ethanol plants financially viable and \ntechnologically feasible and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the details of the steps taken by the Government to promote 2G Ethanol Plants? \nANSWER \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h  \n¼Jh jkes'oj rsyh½ \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & \nNATURAL GAS  \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (d):  In March, 2019, Government had notified the “Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN \n(Jaiv Indhan- Vatavaran Anukool fasal awashesh Nivaran) Yojana” for providing \nfinancial support to integrated bio-ethanol projects for setting up Second \nGeneration (2G) ethanol projects in the country using lignocellulosic biomass and \nother renewable feedstock. The total financial outlay for the scheme is Rs. 1969.50 \ncrore for the period 2018-19 to 2023-24.  \nUnder the PM JI-VAN Yojana, the maximum financial assistance of Rs.150 crore \nper project for commercial projects and Rs.15 crore per project for demonstration \nprojects has been prescribed for improving commercial viability as well as \npromoting R&D for development and adoption of technologies in the field of \nproduction of 2G ethanol. \n \n\n \nFinancial assistance of Rs. 150 crore each to the four commercial Second \nGeneration (2G) bio-ethanol projects at Bathinda in Punjab, Panipat in Haryana, \nBargarh in Odisha and Numaligarh in Assam and Rs. 15 crore to one \ndemonstration project at Panipat in Haryana has been approved under the Scheme.  \nThese commercial projects are in advanced stage of construction and are expected \nto be commissioned from 2022-23. So far, Rs. 151.50 crore has been released \nbased on the milestones achieved as per the scheme.   \nAt present, there is no project for 2G ethanol in the State of Andhra Pradesh under \nthis Scheme. \n(e): Apart from financial support through PM JI-VAN Yojana, other steps taken to \npromote 2G Ethanol Plants include imposition of additional excise duty on non-\nblended fuels; encouraging studies on various aspects including; identifying areas \nhaving the potential of surplus Biofuels feedstocks; policy interventions to \nmainstream biofuels; separate price for 2G ethanol, etc. \n  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |735", "t": "Namami Gange Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 735 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \nNAMAMI GANGE PROGRAMME \n \n735  DR. VISHNU PRASAD M.K. \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:- \n(a) the details of new sewage treatment plants cons tructed during the last three years under Namami Ga nge \nProgramme (NGP), State-wise; \n(b) whether it is a fact that most of the projects under NGP have not been completed within the stipul ated \ntime frame and the programme has not achieved its targets; \n(c) if so, the time by which the projects are likely to be completed; \n(d) the details of the action taken by the Government to solve the problem; and \n(e) the details of the outcome of NGP since its inc eption and the total expenditure incurred on said p rojects, \nState-wise? \n \nANSWER  \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n \n(a) Under Namami Gange Programme (NGP), during last thr ee years, a total of 51 new sewerage treatment \nplants having total capacity of 936.42 MLD have been completed as per below mentioned state-wise details:- \n \nS.No  State  No. of new STP \ncompleted  \nNew Treatment Capacity Created in \nMillion Litre Per Day (MLD)  \n1 Uttarakhand 27 135.39 \n2 Uttar Pradesh 8 186.01 \n3 Bihar 6 221 \n4 Jharkhand 3 15.50 \n5 West Bengal 5 58.8 \n6 Delhi 1 318 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 1 1.72 \n  Total 51 936.42 \n \n(b) & (c) Under Namami Gange Programme, 161 sewerage infrastructure projects have been taken up with a \nsanctioned cost of Rs.24581 crore for creation and rehabilitation of 5015 Million Litres per Day (MLD)  of \nSewage Treatment Plant (STP) capacity and laying of  5,134 km of sewerage network, out of which 92 \nprojects have been completed resulting in creation and rehabilitation of 1,643 MLD of STP capacity and  \nlaying of 4,156 km sewerage network. \n\n Some of the projects undertaken as part of the Nam ami Gange Program had faced delays due to a variety  of \nfactors. Salient reasons for delays in the project are delays in project award due to restructuring, l and issues, \ndelay in handing over land, delays in getting permi ssion from National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) \nand Railways, delays due to Covid-19 etc.  \n Cleaning of river is a continuous process and Gove rnment of India is supplementing the efforts of the  State \nGovernment in addressing the challenges of pollutio n in river Ganga and its tributaries by providing \nfinancial and technical assistance under Namami Gan ge Programme. The projects have now taken their pac e \nand efforts are now being made to complete the projects by their scheduled completion timeline. \n(d)  In order to expedite implementation of the sewerag e infrastructure projects by overcoming the \nbottlenecks and ensure timely completion of project s, the following measures are being taken by Nation al \nMission for Clean Ganga (NMCG): \n i. Regular interaction / discussions / site visits  and coordination meetings with state agencies by NMCG. \nii. Resolving delay in road/railway crossing permis sions by conducting high level inter-Ministerial meeting \nwith Railways and NHAI. \niii. At State level, State Ganga Committees headed by the Chief Secretaries have been constituted for \ncoordinating the Ganga rejuvenation projects betwee n various State agencies and for the first time eve r a \ndistrict level mechanism has been set up in the for m of District Ganga Committees (DGC) headed by the \nDistrict Magistrate to ensure speedy implementation  of interventions and projects and for carrying out  \nvarious activities pertaining to Ganga and its tributaries. \n(e)  Since inception of Namami Gange Programme, 374 pro jects have been taken up at an estimated cost of \nRs. 31,099 Cr. Out of these, 210 projects have been  completed thereby creating / rehabilitating 1,643 MLD \nof STP capacity, laying of 4,156 km sewerage networ k, development / rehabilitation of 183 ghats & kund s, \n46 crematoria, River Front Development at Patna, creation of 12,38,075 nos. of IHHLs. \nUnder the Namami Gange Programme, Central Pollution  Control Board (CPCB) is carrying out water \nquality assessment of the River Ganga at 97 locatio ns in 5 main stem States through the respective Sta te \nPollution Control Boards (SPCBs). As per CPCB data for 2021, the observed water quality of river Ganga  \nindicates that Dissolved Oxygen (DO), which is an i ndicator of river health, has been found to be with in \nacceptable limits of notified primary bathing water  quality criteria and is satisfactory to support th e \necosystem of the river for almost the entire stretc h of the river Ganga. None of the Ganga Stretches a re in \npriority category I to IV and only two stretches ar e in Category V with Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD)  \nranging between 3-6 mg/l as per CPCB categorization  of polluted stretch. As a result of multi sectoral  \ninterventions, as per comparison of median data of water quality parame"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |738", "t": "BH Series Registration of Vehicles", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 738 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2022 \n  \nBH SERIES REGISTRATION OF VEHICLES \n  \n738.  SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \nSHRI B.Y. RAGHAVENDRA: \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \nSHRI S. MUNISWAMY: \nSHRI SHA.BRA.DR.JAI SIDDESHWAR SHIVACHARYA MAHASWAMIJI: \nSHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \nSHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA:  \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the  objectives of introducing the Bharat (BH) series registration of \nvehicles in the country; \n(b) the name of States which have adopted it along with the reasons for \nother States which are opposing to adopt it; \n(c) the number of vehicles registered under the BH series in the States of \nKarnataka and Maharashtra; \n(d) the breakup of the BH series vehicles in these two States between \nCentral and State PSUs; and \n(e) whether the Government proposes to extend the BH series registration \nto employees of private organisations also and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has implemented Bharat (BH) \nSeries Registration vide GSR 594(E) dated 26.08.2021, with date of \nimplementation as 15.09.2021. It has been implemented with the objective \n\nof promoting ease of transfer of vehicles owne d by employees of Central \nGovernment/ State Government/ Public Sector Undertakings and Private \nSector Companies having offices in 4 or more   States. Station relocation \noccurs with both Government as well as private sector employees and such \nmovements create a sense of unease in the minds of such employees with \nregard to obtaining the NOC (No Objection Certificate) from the present \nState, assignment of new registration mark in the new State and \napplication for refund of taxes from the previous State   on transfer from \none State to another State. One registration mark has been made   valid \nthroughout the country. Owner of the vehicle registered under BH series is \nrequired to pay vehicle taxes  every two years or in multiples of it,   to the \nstate where it is located during that period, at 25% higher  than the uniform \nrate as notified. In order to facilitate seamless transfer of such vehicles, \nBH Series is helpful not only to citizens but also in reducing the burden of \nRTOs which deal with such vehicle transfers. \n  \n(b) Following States/UTs have started BH Series registration: \n \n1. Andaman & Nicobar Island \n2. Arunachal Pradesh        \n3. Assam                    \n4. Bihar                    \n5. Chandigarh               \n6. Delhi                    \n7. Goa                      \n8. Gujarat                  \n9. Himachal Pradesh         \n10. Jammu and Kashmir        \n11. Karnataka                \n12. Maharashtra              \n13. Manipur                  \n14. Meghalaya                \n15. Mizoram                  \n16. Nagaland                 \n17. Odisha                   \n18. Puducherry               \n19. Rajasthan                \n20. Sikkim                   \n21. Tripura                  \n22. UT of DNH and DD         \n23. Uttar Pradesh            \n\n24. West Bengal              \n  \nThe transport department of Kerala State Government has filed an affidavit \nin reply to the Writ Petition WP(C) No. 30887 of 2021 ( received as part of \nthe Writ Appeal No. 602 of 2022 filed as State of Kerala & others Vs \nMarysadaan Projects Pvt. Ltd. before the Honorable High Court of Kerala \nat Ernakulam and  Writ Appeal No. 604 of 2022 filed as Secretary \n(Transport) State of Kerala & others Vs Sathyendra Kumar Jha & Anr. \nbefore the Honorable High Court of Kerala at Ernakulam) stating that if the \nState implements BH Series registration, it would result in a financial loss \nto the State exchequer as the owner can pay one time tax of 15 years or of \n2 years at his discretion. However, a detailed response has been submitted \nby MoRTH, to be filed , mentioning that the owner of the motor vehicle shall \npay the motor vehicle tax for a period of two years, or in multiples of it, at \n25% higher rate.The same is being remitted to respective States/UTs \nonline. Hence , there is no financial los s to the State exchequer.   This \nenables the citizen to avoid the cumbersome process with regard to \nobtaining the NoC from the present State, assignment of new registration \nmark in the new State and application for refund of taxes from the previous \nState  on transfer from one State to another State. This facility is available \nto employees of Central Government, State Government, PSUs, Public \nSector Banks, Government autonomous bodies or organisations, and the \nprivate sector (who are employed in an organization with offices in four or \nmore different States/UTs). \n(c)  As on 15 th July, 2022, the number of vehicles registered under the BH \nseries in the States of Karnataka and Maharashtra are as follows: \nState No. of vehicles regis"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |739", "t": "Utilization of the Allocated Budget", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 739 \nFOR ANSWER ON 21.07.2022 \n \nUTILIZATION OF THE ALLOCATED BUDGET \n739. SHRI ABHISHEK BANERJEE: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the percentage of the allocated budget under the Ministry that was \nutilised in the last FY 2021-22, State and category-wise; and;  \n \n(b) the details of the percentage of the allocated budget utilised in the current FY 2022-\n23, State and category-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b):  Ministry of Minority Affairs does not allocate budget State-wise and \nCategory-wise. The utlisation percentage of the allocated budget under Ministry of \nMinority Affairs during FY 2021-22 was 99.51%.  \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |74", "t": "Recommendation for sanctioning AIIMS", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Expenditure \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 74 \n \nMONDAY, THE 18th July, 2022/Ashadha 27, 1944(Saka) \n \nRECOMMENDATION FOR SANCTIONING AIIMS \n \n74. ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has received any recommendation from the Ministry of Health \n& Welfare for sanctioning All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) for Kerala; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has considered the proposal for setting up of AIIMS in \nKerala; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the decision taken thereon? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a)-(b): Ministry of Finance had received proposal of the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare \nfor sanctioning an All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) for Kerala. \n \n(c)-(d): AIIMS are set up under the Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) \nimplemented by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare. Government has approved setting \nup of AIIMS across the country in a phased manner. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |740", "t": "Pollution in Bharathapuzha River", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 740 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \nPOLLUTION IN BHARATHAPUZHA RIVER \n 740 SHRI V.K.SREEKANDAN \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether it is a fact that the condition of river B harathapuzha in the State of Kerala is very \npathetic as it is highly polluted and needs restora tion and rejuvenation and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b)  whether it is a policy of the Government to set up  independent statutory authorities for the \nrejuvenation and restoration of large rivers in the country; \n(c)  if so, whether the Government proposes to set up a  Bharathapuzha Management Authority, \nsince it is an inter-state river;  \n(d)  whether the Government has received any representation in this regard; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government thereon? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n(a)  As per the last report published by CPCB in Septem ber 2018, 351 polluted stretches have \nbeen identified on 323 rivers based on monitoring r esults of 521 rivers in the country in terms of \nBio-chemical Oxygen Demand (BOD), an indicator of o rganic pollution. Based on BOD value, \nCPCB has categorized polluted stretches as Priority -I (>30 mg/l), Priority-II (20-30 mg/l), \nPriority-III (10-20 mg/l), Priority-IV (6-10 mg/l) and Priority-V (3-6 mg/l). As per this ibid \nreport, the stretch along Patambi town situated on Bharathapuzha River in Kerala has been \ncategorized as Priority-IV with a BOD value of 6.6 mg/l. \n\n(b) & (c)  For conservation and rejuvenation of rivers, this Ministry has been supplementing \nefforts of the States/Union Territories (UTs) by pr oviding financial and technical assistance for \nabatement of pollution in identified stretches of r ivers in the country through the Central Sector \nScheme of Namami Gange for Ganga and its tributarie s, and the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of \nNational River Conservation Plan (NRCP) for other r ivers. Sewerage infrastructure, setting up of \nsewage treatment plants (STPs), interception & dive rsion, river front development, low cost \nsanitation, afforestation, etc  in polluting towns/ cities along the rivers are important components \nof these programs.  \nAs per the information provided by Kerala Pollution  Control Board (KPCB), the State \nGovernment, in accordance with NGT O.A. No.673/2018 , the pollution loads were ascertained \nand it has been observed that pollution is mainly d ue to domestic effluents through storm water \ndrains. Action plan was prepared by various State G overnment departments for rejuvenating the \nRiver Bharathapuzha, the same was approved by CPCB and implemented. The action plan \ncomprises of activities such as establishment and modernization of municipal solid waste (MSW) \ntreatment plant, material collection facility, reso urce recovery facility, door to door collection \nand transportation of MSW, management of municipal plastics, hazardous, biomedical and \nelectronic waste, awareness programme for sections of public, monitoring of river quality by \nsampling, Green protocol implementation in all offi ces institution and public function are fast \nprogressing. Installing household and community lev el solid waste management units, primary \nsewage treatment plant and septage treatment plant,  installation of modern slaughter house and \nrendering plant, construction of sanitary plants in  public place are also progressing. The progress \non the implementation of action plan is being monit ored and as per the analysis reports, \nBharathapuzha is constantly achieving bathing quality standards (BOD<3 mg/l, FC<500 mg/l).  \n(d) & (e)  In February, 2021, a request for setting up of Bha rathapuzha Management Authority \nwas received from the Friends of Bharathapuzha (FoB ), a Society for Restoration of River Nila. \nThe same was referred for necessary action by this Ministry to Government of Kerala in March, \n2021.  In addition, a similar request was recently received from Shri V. K. Sreekandan, Hon’ble \nMember of Parliament, Kerala in July , 2022. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |741", "t": "Maintenance of Dilapidated NH", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 741 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2022 \n  \nMAINTENANCE OF DILAPIDATED NH \n  \n741.    SHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR: \n  \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n                                                 सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether th e Lucknow -Sitapur-Shahjahanpur-Bareilly-Rampur-\n Moradabad-Delhi National Highway (NH) is in a  very dilapidated \n condition and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the details of funds allocated for the maintenance and construction \n of the said NH during each of the last three years and the current \n year; \n(c) the utilization status of the said funds during the said period; and \n(d) the steps taken by the Government for the repair and maintenance of \n the said NH? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) The details of road condition and development work on  stretch from \nLucknow to Delhi are as under: \nName of Stretch Status \nLucknow-Sitapur Construction work has already been completed and is in \nOperation & Maintenance stage (O&M). It is in traffic -\nworthy condition. \nSitapur-Bareilly   Balance work of Sitapur-Bareilly section is in progress. \nBareilly-\nMoradabad \nThe stretch is in traffic worthy condition. \nMoradabad-Hapur The stretch is in traffic worthy condition. \nHapur-Delhi Construction work has already been completed and is in \nOperation & Maintenance stage (O&M). It is in traffic -\nworthy condition. \n\n  \n(b) to (d)     After completion of road works on stretches namely  Lucknow-\nSitapur, Bareilly -Moradabad, Moradabad -Hapur & Hapur-Delhi respective \nConcessionaires are maintaining theses stretches in traffic worthy \ncondition as per provisions contained in the Concession Agreements. \n  \n In respect of Bareilly-Sitapur section of NH-24, details of development \nworks sanctioned are as follows: \n  \nYear Name of Works sanctioned Amount (Rs. in crore) \n2019-20 Maintenance work of Bareilly Sitapur \nsection \n4.83 \nBalance road work of Bareilly Sitapur \nSection \n697.42 \n2020-21 Balance work of ROB at Km 290.445 in \nBareilly Sitapur Section \n41.38 \n  \nThe details of funds utilized for  Bareilly Sitapur Section of NH -24 during \neach of the last three years and current year is tabulated below: \n  \nYear Fund Utilised (Rs. in crore) \n2019-20 16.46 \n2020-21 154.09 \n2021-22 284.36 \n2022-23  (up to June 2022) 78.37 \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |742", "t": "Subsidy on LPG", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 742 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21 JULY, 2022 \nSubsidy on LPG \n742.   ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has any plan to resume direct subsidy on LPG and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) the details of the price of LPG revised during the last three years, date and amount-wise price \nhiked; and \n(c) the detail of the Government’s expenditure for LPG subsidy during the last five years and the \ncurrent year, year-wise? \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी   \n(\u0000ी  रामेश ्  वर तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a): The prices of petroleum products in the country are linked to the price of respective \nproducts in the international market. However, the Government continues to modulate the \neffective price to consumer for domestic LPG. The subsidy, as admissible, is credited to the \nbank account of eligible beneficiaries.  In addition, the Government has started a targeted \nsubsidy of Rs. 200 per 14.2 Kg cylinder for Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) \nbeneficiaries for upto 12 refills a year for year 2022-23. \n(b): The details of prices of domestic LPG at Delhi since April 2019 are given as Annexure.   \n(c): The details of subsidy on LPG given by Government during each of the last five years are \ngiven below: \n           (Rs. /Crores) \nParticulars 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22(P) \nDirect Benefit Transfer for LPG \n(DBTL) subsidy (A) 20905 31539 22726 3658 242 \nPradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana \n(PMUY) subsidy (B) 2559 5670 1446 76 - \nPradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan \nPackage (PMGKP) subsidy (C)    8162  \nTotal Subsidy on LPG             \n(A+B+C) \n23464 37209 24172 11896 242 \nSource: Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC) \nx-x-x-x-x \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 742 asked by \nAdv. Adoor Prakash to be answered on 21 July, 2022 regarding “Subsidy on LPG” \n \nDate \nDomestic LPG Rs./14.2 Kg \nCylinder at Delhi \n01 April 2019 706.50 \n01 May 2019 712.50 \n01 June 2019 737.50 \n01 July 2019 637.00 \n01 August 2019 574.50 \n01 September 2019 590.00 \n01 October 2019 605.00 \n01 November 2019 681.50 \n01 December 2019 695.00 \n01 January 2020 714.00 \n01 February 2020 714.00 \n12 February 2020 858.50 \n01 March 2020 805.50 \n01 April 2020 744.00 \n01 May 2020 581.50 \n01 June 2020 593.00 \n01 July 2020 594.00 \n02 December 2020 644.00 \n15 December 2020 694.00 \n04 February 2021 719.00 \n15 February 2021 769.00 \n25 February 2021 794.00 \n01 March 2021 819.00 \n01 April 2021 809.00 \n01 July 2021 834.50 \n17 August 2021 859.50 \n01 September 2021 884.50 \n06 October 2021 899.50 \n22 March 2022 949.50 \n07 May 2022 999.50  \n19 May 2022 1003.00  \n06 July 2022 1053.00  \nSource: Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC) \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |743", "t": "Re-development of District Centre", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 743 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nRE-DEVELOPMENT OF DISTRICT CENTRE \nNO. 743. SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that the Delhi Development Aut hority \n(DDA)/South Delhi Municipal Corporation (SDMC) have  \ntransferred an amount of Rs.180 crores approximatel y for re-\ndevelopment/revamp of Nehru Place district centre a nd also \nappointed any contractor/builder to carry out any r e-\ndevelopment/revamp work; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor  and the \nsteps taken by the Government in this regard; \n(c)  whether any such work of re-development/revamp is b eing \nmonitored by DDA, SDMC or by All Nehru Place Develo pment \nand Welfare Association (jointly or separately) and  if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(d)  whether DDA, Delhi Police, SDMC or All Nehru Place \nDevelopment and Welfare Association are conducting their \nmeetings on regular intervals to assess and monitor  the \nprogress/the status of re-development/revamp in the  Nehru \nPlace Market, New Delhi; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof along withthe corrective  measures \ntaken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI  KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a): Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has appr oved a proposal \nof Delhi Development Authority (DDA) for re-develop ment of District \nCentre, Nehru Place for Rs. 182.13 Cr out of which Rs. 130.23 Cr is \nfunded under Urban Development Fund (UDF) while bal ance Rs 51.90 \nCr is being borne by DDA. Three contractors have be en appointed by \nDDA for executing the work. \n...2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \n(b): The work is being undertaken for refurbishment  of Nehru \nPlace so as to redevelop it as a modern district ce ntre. The scope of \nwork involves – \n \nSl No Sector Component Cost  \n(in Cr) \nSectoral \nCost  \n(in Cr) \nUDF \n \nDDA \n1 Transportation 98.39 24.59 122.98 \n2 Sanitation 31.84 7.97 39.81 \n3 Commercial  13.82  13.82  \n4 \nSocial \nInfrastructure  5.52 5.52 \n5 Total 130.23 51.90 182.13 \n \nTransportation includes multi-level car parking, sk ywalk, surface \nparking, plaza, road and foot path etc. \n \nSanitation includes construction of new toilet bloc k by DDA in lieu of \nold and shifting of sewer lines by Delhi Jal Board. \n \nCommercial includes creating space for 40 hawkers, creating 52 \nkiosks and facade improvement of existing DDA and other buildings. \n \nSocial infrastructure covers amphitheatre, art and sculpture work and \nhorticulture. \n \n(c) to (e): The re-development work is being monito red regularly by \nDDA which is the implementing agency. The project i s being \nimplemented as per the Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) \namongst DDA, South Delhi Municipal Corporation (SDM C) and Market \nAssociation. Issues, if any, are resolved through mutual consultation. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |744", "t": "Capacity of Oceanic Energy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 744 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nCAPACITY OF OCEANIC ENERGY \n744. SHRI GYANESHWAR PATIL \nWill the Minister of New and Renewable Energy be pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of the estimated capacity of oceanic energy including tidal energy, wave energy, salinity \nand difference in temperature of the ocean in the country, State-wise; \n(b) the funds allocated for research and development on each of the oceanic energy including tidal \nenergy, wave energy, salinity and difference in temperature of the ocean during the last three years and \nthe current year; \n(c) whether the Government has launched any tidal energy scheme and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) whether the Government proposes to launch any pilot project in the field of oceanic energy in the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND  CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)  \n  \n(a) The Indian Institute of Technology, Chennai in association with Credit Rating Information Servic es \nof India Limited (CRISIL) had  conducted a study  on “Tidal & Waves Energy in India: Survey on the \nPotential & Proposition of a Roadmap” in December 2014. As per this report, the state -wise estimated \ntheoretical potentials of tidal energy and wave energy in the country are as follows: \n \nState Tidal \nEnergy \n(MW) \nWave \nEnergy \n(MW) \nAndhra Pradesh 100 6,900 \nGujarat 10,425 4,100 \nKarnataka 100 6,100 \nKerala 100 4,900 \nMaharashtra  200 8,100 \nOdisha 400 600 \nTamil Nadu 230 10,600 \nWest Bengal 900 -- \nTotal 12,455 41,300 \n \nThe study did not estimate the potentials of salinity and difference in temperature of the ocean in the \ncountry. \n \n(b) The funds allocated for research and development (R&D) in the area of new and Renewable Energy \ntechnologies during the last three years (2019 -20, 2020-21 & 2021 -22) is Rs. 91 crore and during the \ncurrent year (2022-23) is Rs. 35.00 crore. The R&D budget of the Ministry is a composite one without \nspecific allocations for different technologies. Since no viable R&D project was received, no R&D funds \nhave been spent on oceanic energy. \n \n(c)&(d) The Ministry has not launched any scheme on tidal energy. However, the Renewable Energy \nResearch and Technology Development Programme  of the Ministry, supports R&D projects for \ntechnology development and demonstration in various areas of new and renewable energy, including \noceanic energy projects in the country. R&D Division vide notification dated 26/05/2022 has issued “call \nfor proposals” for R&D projects including oceanic energy in the country.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |745", "t": "Cities under Smart Cities Mission", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 745 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nCITIES UNDER SMART CITIES MISSION \n \nNO. 745.    SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n  SHRI C.R. PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of cities identified in Gujarat und er Smart Cities Mission \n(SCM); \n \n(b)  the status of implementation/completion of var ious projects in the \nabove identified cities, city and project-wise duri ng the last three \nyears and the current year; \n \n(c)  the funds disbursed so far for smart cities in  the said State during the \nlast five years; \n \n(d)  whether any request has been received by the G overnment from the \nState Government of Gujarat for inclusion of more c ity corporations \nunder SCM; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details of the action taken with regard to the above request? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)   to (e) : Government of India launched the Smart Ci ties Mission \n(SCM) on 25 June 2015 for development of 100 cities  as Smart Cities. The \nselection of 100 Smart Cities has been completed th rough 4 rounds of \nselection from January 2016 to June 2018. In the St ate of Gujarat 6 cities \nnamely Ahmedabad, Dahod, Gandhinagar, Rajkot, Surat  & Vadodara have \nbeen selected for development as Smart Cities. The present status of \nphysical progress in Smart City projects and funds released by the Central \nGovernment to the Smart Cities in Gujarat are given in Annexure-I. \nNo proposal has been received from the State Govern ment of Gujarat for \ninclusion of more city corporations under SCM. \n*****  \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I in reply to parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabh a Unstarred Question No. 745 for answer on \n21.07.2022 regarding Cities under Smart Cities Miss ion asked by Shri Rajeshbhai Chudasama \nand Shri C.R. Patil, Hon’ble MPs. \n \nStatus of Smart Cities projects in Gujarat \n(₹ in crore) \nSmart City Tender Stage Work Order Stage Work Compl eted Total \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmount No. of \nProjects \nAmount No. of \nProjects \nAmount No. of \nProjects \nAmount \nAhmedabad 0 0.00 11 1,211.93 57 2,056.52 68 3,268.4 5 \nDahod 0 0.00 15 568.42 22 435.81 37 1,004.23 \nGandhinagar 1 0.00 13 869.77 11 237.09 25 1,106.86 \nRajkot 2 1,087.00 45 1,748.15 25 413.32 72 3,248.47  \nSurat 0 0.00 12 1,052.95 70 2,053.87 82 3,106.82 \nVadodara 2 80.00 8 580.73 41 1,100.63 51 1,761.36 \nTotal 5 1,167.00 104 6,031.95 226 6,297.24 335 13,4 96.19 \nSource: SCM Geospatial Management Information System (GMIS), as on 8 July 2022. \n \n \nStatus of funds released under SCM by Central Government for the Smart Cities in Gujarat & \nutilization thereof \n(₹ in crore) \nSmart City During \n2015-16 \nDuring \n2016-17 \nDuring \n2017-18 \nDuring \n2018-19 \nDuring \n2019-20 \nDuring \n2020-21 \nDuring \n2021-22 \nDuring \n2022-23 \nTotal \nCentral \nRelease \nUtilization \nof GOI \nFunds \nAhmedabad 2.00 194.00 0.00 98.00 0.00 49.00 49.00 2 4.50 416.50 392.00 \nDahod 2.00 0.00 17.00 167.00 10.00 0.00 98.00 0.00 294.00 294.00 \nGandhinagar 2.00 0.00 18.00 90.00 86.00 0.00 147.00  0.00 343.00 263.51 \nRajkot 2.00 0.00 19.00 175.00 0.00 49.00 49.00 0.00  294.00 291.64 \nSurat 2.00 194.00 0.00 98.00 204.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 498.00 490.00 \nVadodara 2.00 0.00 109.00 85.00 0.00 49.00 49.00 0. 00 294.00 293.59 \nTotal 12.00 388.00 163.00 713.00 300.00 147.00 392. 00 24.50 2,139.50 2,024.74 \nSource: SCM Geospatial Management Information System (GMIS), as on 8 July 2022. \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |746", "t": "Working of Biogas Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 746 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nWORKING OF BIOGAS PLANTS   \n+746. SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR  \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has set up any system to ensure that the installed biogas plants \nare working smoothly; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND  CHEMICALS \nAND FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n(a)&(b) The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE) had been supporting the installation \nof biogas plants under the New National Biogas and Organic Manure Programme (NNBOMP). \nThe scheme has been continued beyond 31.03.2021 only for meeting the committed liabil ities. \nUnder this scheme, MNRE has been  providing assistance for supervision of the installed biogas \nplants and trainings for biogas users, biogas masons & officials of the State Programme \nImplementing Agencies for smooth operation and maintenance of biogas plants.  For this, purpose, \nthe ministry has created eight Biogas Development Training Centres.        \n \nMNRE had instituted a Third Party Evaluation Study to assess the functionality of the biogas \nplants installed during the period 2017 -18 to 2019-20. The study conducted across 13 states of \nthe country reported that about 96 % of the small biogas plants were functional.   The State wise \nDetails of functionality /operational status of installed small biogas plants are given in    \nAnnexure I. \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I  \n \nANNEXURE-I, REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 746 FOR ANSWERING ON 21.07.2022 ON “WORKING OF BIOGAS \nPLANTS” \n \nThe State wise Functionality /operational Status of Biogas Plants \n \nSl \nNo. \nStates Total Not \nOperating \nOperating % of \nN.O. \nOperating \n(%) \n1 Assam 637 149 488 23% 77% \n2 Bihar 15 1 14 7% 93% \n3 Gujarat 201 8 193 4% 96% \n4 Jharkhand 20 4 16 20% 80% \n5 Karnataka 1377 30 1347 2% 98% \n6 Madhya \nPradesh \n893 - 893 0% 100% \n7 Maharashtra 2050 21 2029 1% 99% \n8 Meghalaya 24 1 23 4% 96% \n9 Mizoram 57 1 56 2% 98% \n10 Rajasthan 90 - 90 0% 100% \n11 Tamil Nadu 75 - 75 0% 100% \n12 Uttar Pradesh 72 - 72 0% 100% \n13 Uttrakhand 285 1 284 0.4% 99.6% \nGrand Total 5796 216 5580 4% 96% \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |747", "t": "Status of Energy Projects in Kerala", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 747 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nSTATUS OF ENERGY PROJECTS IN KERALA \n747. ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has the data on the funds sanctioned and disbursed to the State of Kerala \nduring the last three years and the current year for projects related to New and Renewable Energy \ngeneration; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has the details of the projects proposed, undergoing and completed in \nthe State of Kerala during the said period thereunder; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the details of the projects from the said list that are delayed or pending? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND  CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n \n(a) & (b) The details of funds released as Central Financial Assistance (CFA) by the Ministry for \nimplementation of various renewable energy schemes/programmes in the State of Kerala during the last \nthree years, i.e. from 2019-20 to 2022-23, are given below: \n                                                           (Rs. in Crores) \nYear Central Financial \nAssistance (CFA) \nReleased  \n2019-20 3.57 \n2020-21 12.37 \n2021-22 37.16 \n \nNo release has been made in the current year 2022-23 in the state of Kerala. \n \n(c) to (e) A total of 2735.27 MW renewable energy capacity (including large hydro) has been installed in \nthe State of Kerala as on 30-06-2022. This includes 1864.15 MW Large Hydro Power, 539.60 MW Solar \nPower, 266.52 MW Small Hydro Power, 62.50 MW Wind Power and 2.50 MW Bio-Power. \n \nFurther, a capacity of 450.13 MW is under various stages of implementation. This includes 312.63 MW \nSolar Power, 100 MW Large Hydro Power and 37.5 MW Small Hydro Power. \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |749", "t": "Targets under AMRUT", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 749 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 21, 2022 \n \nTARGETS UNDER AMRUT \nNO. 749. SHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA: \n  SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \n  SHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has tracked the progres s in achieving the \ntargets under Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urb an \nTransformation(AMRUT) in Andhra Pradesh and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b)  the details of the cities included under AMRUT  2.0 in the said State, \ndistrict-wise; and \n(c)  the details of funds allotted and assessment o f designated works in its \ncomparison? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a): U nder Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transf ormation \n(AMRUT), States/Union Territories (UTs) have been e mpowered to select, \nappraise, approve and implement projects as per the ir local requirements \nand within the overall framework of Mission guideli nes. The Ministry of \nHousing and Urban Affairs approves the State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs) \nsubmitted by the States/UTs and releases Central fu nds as per the Mission \nguidelines.  \n...2/- \n\n \n \n-2- \nThe progress of the Mission is regularly monitored in the Ministry \nthrough various mechanisms such as dedicated AMRUT portal on which \nprogress of works is updated by the States/UTs, vid eo conferences, \nwebinars, workshops, review visits etc. The progres s of States/UTs is also \nreviewed regularly by the Apex Committee of AMRUT. \nState of Andhra Pradesh has s o far provided 3.93 lakh water tap \nconnections and 3.85 lakh sewer connections under A MRUT & in \nconvergence with other schemes against the targeted  7.29 lakh water tap \nconnections and 1.31 lakh sewer connections. Furthe r, 187 Million Litres per \nDay (MLD) Sewage treatment capacity is under development through ongoing \nprojects of the Mission apart from construction of about 57 Kilometres of \nstorm water drainage to reduce urban flooding in AMRUT cities.  \n(b): The district-wise list of cities of the State of Andhra Pradesh included \nunder AMRUT 2.0 is placed at Annexure. \n(c):    For the State of Andhra Pradesh, SAAPs amou nting to \n\u00012,890.17 crore, \nincluding committed Central Assistance of \u00011,056.62 crore have been \napproved for the entire Mission period under AMRUT.  So far, \u0001866.39 crore \nhas been released to the State of Andhra Pradesh as  Central funds for \nimplementation of projects under the Mission. The State of Andhra Pradesh \nhas, however, taken up 226 projects amounting to \u00013,333.76 crore, which is \nin excess of the approved SAAPs. The entire additio nal cost of the projects \nwill be borne by the State. \n Of these 226 projects taken up under AMRUT by the State Government \nin the sectors of Water Supply, Sewerage & Septage Management, Storm \nWater Drainage and development of Green Spaces/Park s, works have been \ncompleted for 100 projects costing \n\u0001739.79 crore, while 126 projects costing \n\u00012,593.97 crore have been grounded and are at variou s stages of \nimplementation. Total works worth \u00012,239.36 crore have been physically \ncompleted so far. \n \nUnder AMRUT 2.0, \u00013,158 crore has been allotted to the State of \nAndhra Pradesh as committed Central Assistance for implementation of \nprojects. \n***** \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF UNSTAR RED QUESTION \nNO. 749 REGARDING “TARGETS UNDER AMRUT”, DUE FOR AN SWER IN THE \nLOK SABHA ON 21 JULY, 2022.  \nDistrict-wise list of cities of Andhra Pradesh covered under AMRUT 2.0 \nSl. No.  Name of District \nName of ULB/ City/ Town covered under  \nAMRUT 2.0 \n1 Anantapur Rayadurg \n2 Anantapur Guntakal \n3 Anantapur Tadipatri \n4 Anantapur Anantapur \n5 Anantapur Dharmavaram \n6 Anantapur Kadiri \n7 Anantapur Hindupur \n8 Anantapur Gooty \n9 Anantapur Kalyandurgam \n10 Anantapur Puttaparthi \n11 Anantapur Madakasira \n12 Anantapur Penukonda \n13 Chittoor Srikalahasti \n14 Chittoor Tirupati \n15 Chittoor Madanapalle \n16 Chittoor Nagari \n17 Chittoor Puttur \n18 Chittoor Punganur \n19 Chittoor Chittoor \n20 Chittoor Palamaneru \n21 Chittoor Kuppam \n22 Chittoor B Kothakota \n23 East Godavari Tuni \n24 East Godavari Peddapuram \n25 East Godavari Rajahmundry \n26 East Godavari Samalkot \n27 East Godavari Pithapuram \n28 East Godavari Kakinada \n29 East Godavari Mandapeta \n30 East Godavari Ramachandrapuram \n\n \n \nSl. No.  Name of District \nName of ULB/ City/ Town covered under  \nAMRUT 2.0 \n31 East Godavari Amalapuram \n32 East Godavari Yeleswaram \n33 East Godavari Gollaprolu \n34 East Godavari Mummidivaram \n35 Guntur Macherla \n36 Guntur Piduguralla \n37 Guntur Mangalagiri Tadepalli  \n38 Guntur Sattenapalli \n39 Guntur Vinukonda \n40 Guntur Narasaraopet \n41 Guntur Chilakaluripet \n42 Guntur Guntur \n43 Guntur Tenali \n44 Guntur Ponnur \n45 Guntur Bapatla \n46 Guntur Repalle \n47 Guntur Gurajala \n48 Guntur Dachepalli \n49 Guntur Tadepalli \n50 Kadapa Badvel \n51 Kad"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |75", "t": "Special Development Package", "head": "����������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������\n��\n�� �� ������ ������������������\n����������������������������������������������������\n��\n������