�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������� ���������� ������������ ������������������������ �������� ������������ ���������������������� �������� ������������������ ������ �������������� ������\n������������������������ ������������ �������������������� ������ �������������� ������ �������������������� ������������ �� ������������������ ������ ���������������������� ������ ��������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������ �������������������� ������ �������� ������������ ������������������ ������������������������ �������� ������������ �������������� ���������������� ��������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������� ������ �������������� ������������������ �������� ������������������ ���������������� ���������������� ������������������������ ���������������� ��������\n������������������������ ������ ������������ ������������������ �������������������� ������ �������� ������������ ������������ �������� �������������� ���������������� ������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |750", "t": "Unelectrified Villages", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.750 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nUNELECTRIFIED VILLAGES \n \n750.  SHRI ADHIKARI DEEPAK (DEV): \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether all villages in the country would be electrified by 2022; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the number  of villages that are still \nto be electrified; \n \n(c)  whether Baidaposi village in Mayurbhanj district of Odisha is still to be \nelectrified; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e)  the time by which it is likely to be electrified? \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e): As reported by the S tates, all the inhabited un -electrified census \nvillages across the country stand electrified as on 28 th April, 2018 under \nDeen Dayal  Upadhyaya Gram Jyoti Yojana . State Government of Odisha  has \nreported that Baidaposi  village in  Mayurbhanj district of Odish a stands \nelectrified. \n \n*************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |751", "t": "Production of Crude Oil and Petroleum", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 751 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2022 \n \nProduction of Crude Oil and Petroleum \n \n \n751. SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \n \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken the steps to increase production of crude oil and \npetroleum in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(c) whether the Government has taken the steps to reduce cess to encourage domestic crude \noil production; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n(\u0000ी रामे\u0000र तेली) \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b) Various steps/policy initiatives undertaken to increase the production of crude oil \nand petroleum inter-alia include \n \n1. Long term Policy initiatives: \n \ni. Policy for Relaxations, Extensions and Clarifications under Production Sharing \nContract (PSC) regime for early monetization of hydrocarbon discoveries, 2014.  \nii. Discovered Small Field Policy, 2015.  \niii. Hydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy, 2016.  \niv. Policy for Extension of Production Sharing Contracts, 2016 and 2017.  \nv. Policy for early monetization of Coal Bed Methane 2017   \nvi. Setting up of National Data Repository, 2017.  \nvii. Appraisal of Un-appraised areas in Sedimentary Basins under National Seismic \nProgramme, 2017   \nviii. Re-assessment of Hydrocarbon Resources 2017   \nix. Policy framework to streamline the working of Production Sharing Contracts in Pre- \nNELP and NELP Blocks, 2018.  \nx. Policy to Promote and Incentivize Enhanced Recovery Methods for Oil and Gas, \n2018.  \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \nxi. Policy framework for Exploration and exploitation of Coal Bed Methane (CBM) from \nareas under Coal Mining Lease allotted to Coal India Limited (CIL) and its \nsubsidiaries 2018.  \nxii. Policy Framework for Exploration and Exploitation of Unconventional Hydrocarbons \nunder Existing Production Sharing Contracts (PSCs) Coal Bed Methane (CBM) \nContracts and Nomination Fields, 2018.  \nxiii. Reforms in Hydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy for enhancing domestic \nexploration and production of oil and gas 2019.  \nxiv. Natural Gas Marketing Reforms 2020. \n \n2.  Short and medium term initiatives: \n  \ni. Early Monetization of Existing Discoveries.  \nii. Improving Recovery Factor through implementation of Improved Oil Recovery (IOR) \nand Enhanced Oil Recovery (EOR) techniques.  \niii. Revival of sick wells.  \niv. Infill drilling of wells.  \nv. Renewal of Facilities and other infrastructure.  \nvi. Monetization of small and marginal discoveries in onshore through service contract \nand outsourcing.  \nvii. Redevelopment of existing matured fields and development of new fields/marginal \nfields.    \nviii. Induction of suitable technologies on selective fields.   \nix. Implementation of Production Enhancement Contracts. \nx. Implementation of IOR/EOR techniques. \n \n(c) & (d) The cess on production of domestic crude oil is calibrated by Government from \ntime to time based on the need to encourage domestic production and overall economic/ fiscal \nsituation. \n \n******* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |752", "t": "Extraction of Methane Hydrate", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 752 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2022  \n \nExtraction of Methane Hydrate \n \n752.    SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken steps for commercial extraction of methane hydrate \ndeposits in Krishna-Godavari basin and Mahanadi- Andaman basin, which has potential to \nmake India energy independent;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?  \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी   \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर  तेल\u0000) \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): Gas hydrate exploration in India is taken up under National Gas Hydrate Program \n(NGHP). Till date, two expeditions have been undertaken under NGHP to explore the \npresence of gas hydrates. Under first programme, NGHP-01, in 2006, 39 holes were drilled \nat 21 sites in Krishna-Godavari (KG), Mahanadi, Andaman, and Kerala-Konkan Basin and \nunder second programme, NGHP-02, in 2015, 42 holes were drilled at 25 sites in KG and \nMahanadi Basin.  \n(c):  Does not arise in view of above. \n********* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |753", "t": "Aims of Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram", "head": "  \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 753 \nANSWERED ON: 21.07.2022 \n  \nAIMS OF PRADHAN MANTRI JAN VIKAS KARYAKRAM \n753.     SHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA:  \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \n            SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU:  \nSHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the aims and objectives of Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK); \n(b) the details of minority concentrated areas/blocks identified under PMJVK in the State of \nTelangana, district-wise;  \n(c) the details of projects, particularly women-centric, approved in the above identified \nblocks/areas; and \n(d) the details of physical targets set and achieved under PMJVK during the last three years and \nthe current year, year-wise? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) & (b) The Pradhan Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakram (PMJVK), a Centrally Sponsored Scheme \n(CSS), is being implemented with the objective to develop infrastructure projects in identified \nareas for socio economic development of said areas. The priority sectors under PMJVK are \nEducation, Health, Skill Development, Sports, Sanitation, Solar Energy, Women Centric \nprojects, etc. The scheme is being implemented in all Districts of the country, including all \nDistricts of Telangana, with effect from financial year 2022-23. \n \n(c)  The infrastructure projects, including women-centric projects approved under PMJVK in the \nState of Telangana w.e.f. 2013-14 is at Annexure-I. \n \n (d)  The proposals under PMJVK are sent by the States/UTs as per requirement of infrastructure \nin the identified areas. The projects approved under PMJVK, during the last three financial \nyears, i.e. w.e.f. 2019-20 to 2021-22, are at Annexure-II.  \n***** \n  \n\n Annexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (c) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question no. 753 for \nanswer on 21.07.2022 asked by Shri Venkatesh Netha Borlakunta, Dr. G. Ranjith Reddy, \nShrimati Kavitha Malothu and Shri Pasunoori Dayakar regarding “Aims of Pradhan \nMantri Jan Vikas Karyakram” .  \n  \nInfrastructure projects approved under PMJVK in the State of Telangana w.e.f. 2013-14 \n  \nName of the project No. of Units Total project cost (Rs. in lakhs) \nSchool Buildings 8 1974.45 \nResidential Schools  36 67400.00 \nResidential School for Girls 18 38000.00 \nGirls Hostels  8 1683.78 \nHostels 12 3963.78 \nAdditional Classrooms  133 679.45 \nToilets in Schools  5 25.00 \nSmart Classrooms/ \nComputers/Teaching Aids \n367 133.58 \nHealth Sub-Centres 9 202.32 \nIndustrial Training Institutes 2 783.12 \nAnganwadi Centres 50 225.00 \nCommon Service Centres  11 1540.00 \n  \n  \n   \n \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (d) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question no. 753 for \nanswer on 21.07.2022 asked by Shri Venkatesh Netha Borlakunta, Dr. G. Ranjith Reddy, \nShrimati Kavitha Malothu and Shri Pasunoori Dayakar regarding “Aims of Pradhan \nMantri Jan Vikas Karyakram” . \n  \nInfrastructure projects approved under PMJVK during the last three financial years. \n  \n (Rs. in lakhs) \nName of the project 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nNo. of \nUnits \n Total cost No. of \nUnits \n Total \ncost \nNo. of \nUnits \n Total cost \nSchool Buildings 195 26370.65 31 4796.20 7 2451.74\nResidential Schools  64161395.72 12 33631.82 3 15696.00\nHostels 129 38625.32 39 10493.97 12 7490.92\nColleges 11 21186.51 11 11018.57 3 35446\nAdditional Classrooms in \nSchools \n554 7008.49 759 8439.08 2 1020.30\nComputer \nLab/Laboratories/Libraries in \nSchools/ Auditorium/ Hall in \nSchools/Playground in Schools \n16 568.85 9 197.24 38 1977.118\nSmart Classrooms/Teaching \nAids in Schools \n3485 13384.06 548916854.79 3 8.91\nDrinking Water and Toilets in \nSchools \n395 1841.6 31996670.7241305 3262.5\nHealth Projects 27740370.245 12850790.89 315 112242.82\nIndustrial Training Institutes \n(ITIs), Polytechnics and \nAdditonal infrastructure in \nITIs/Polytechnics \n24 23242.62 3 2720.9 11 7355.81\nSkill Centres/ Hunar Hubs 18 3734.34 1 735.91 17 2963.86\nWorking Women Hostels 15 9450.69 1 100.00 4 3531.45\nAnganwadi Centres 94 1515.17 58 435.00 171 533.40\nCommon Service Centres/ \nSadbhav Mandaps/ Community \nHalls \n136 19100.44 29 13001.93 13 1814.90\nSports Facilities 9 15835.98 65 18566.75 16 15257.62\nMiscellaneous projects 215 27108.55 6 570.12 68 11232.7\n  \n  \n   \n  \n  \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |754", "t": "Road Projects under Char Dham Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 754 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY 2022 \n  \n  \nROAD PROJECTS UNDER CHAR DHAM PROGRAMME \n  \n754. MS. MIMI CHAKRABORTY: \n  \n           Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the present status of construction of all weather road under Char \nDham Programme; \n(b) whether the project is divided into many segments and if so, th e \ndetails thereof along with the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether it is a fact that bypassing of Environmental Impact \nAssessment by segmentation of road projects destroys the ecology \nof the region; and \n(d) if so, the reaction of the Government in this regard? \n  \nANSWER  \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) and (b) Improvement of 5 National Highways (NHs) connecting \nChardham viz. Yamunotri , Gangotri, Kedarnath and Badrinath \nincluding Tanakpur to Pithoragarh section of Kailas - Mansarovar \nyatra is being undertaken for development of about 825 km of roads \nlength under 53 packages. Each package is an independent project \nhaving distinct starting and ending points which are important places \non the Highway. Out of 825 km, road length of about 578 km has been \ncompleted. \n  \n(c) and (d) Does not arise. \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |755", "t": "Institute of Driving Training and Research", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.755 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2022 \nINSTITUTE OF DRIVING TRAINING AND RESEARCH \n \n755.  SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SONI: \nSHRI ARUN SAO: \nSHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to establish Institute of Driving \nTraining and Research (IDTR) in  various parts of the country \nincluding Chhattisgarh; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the locations  where IDTR is \npresently functional along with the locations where such institutes \nare proposed to be set up, State-wise; \n(c)  the criteria set for opening new IDTR in the State  of Chhattisgarh \nincluding Bilaspur, Mungeli, Gaurela,  Durg, Bemetara, Raipur and \nBaloda Bazar along with the  details of necessary infrastructural \nfacilities for the purpose; \n(d)  whether the Government has identified the locations where IDTR is \nto be set up on Public Private Partnership (PPP) mode and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the steps taken by the Government in \nthis regard; and \n(e)  whether the Union Government is providing financial assistance for \nthe purpose and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a)  Yes Sir.  A scheme to set up Institute of Driving Training and \nResearch (IDTR) in States/UTs has been implemented by Ministry of Road \nTransport and Highways (MoRTH). The population criteria for sanction of \nIDTR under the scheme is one IDTR per 5 Crore Population during the \n15th Financial Commission Period. \n  \n\n(b)     As on date, MoRTH has sanctioned 31 IDTRs (including erstwhile \nDTI), throughout the country. State -wise breakup is given in the \nAnnexure–A. As per the population criteria (one IDTR per 5 Crore \nPopulation), States namely Bihar, Gujarat, Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu, Uttar \nPradesh, and West Bengal are eligible for sanction of new IDTR(s) during \nthe 15th Financial Commission period. The proposals under the scheme \nare initiated by the State Government. \n  \n(c)  One IDTR at Naya Raipur in Chhattisgarh has already been \nsanctioned under the scheme. As per scheme provisions, the State does \nnot qualify for another IDTR.   The infrastructure created under the \nScheme includes 10-15 acres land, buildings covering classrooms, office \nand staff rooms, laboratory, workshops, library and Hostels, vehicles, \nequipment, driver training simulators, driving range etc. \n  \n(d)    The proposa l under the scheme is initiated by the State \nGovernment. Private Partner under the scheme is decided by State \nGovernment as per their requirement. \n  \n(e)  As per Scheme Guidelines, MoRTH provides one time assistance of \n100% of the capital investment subject to maximum of ₹ 17.25 Crore per \nIDTR as per following break up: \n  \n• Civil Construction:                                  ₹ 14 Crore \n• Office and Workshop equipment:           ₹ 0.75 Crore \n• Vehicle and Simulator:                             ₹ 2.50 Crore \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure – A \nStatement referred to in reply to Part (b) of Lok Saba Unstarred Question \nNo. 755 to be answered on 21.07.2022 asked by  Shri Sunil Kumar Soni \nand Ors. regarding Institute of Driving Training and Research. \n  \nS. No State Location \n1 Andhra Pradesh Vijayawada \n2 Andhra Pradesh Dasri \n3 Andhra Pradesh Dhone, Kurnool \n4 Assam Guwahati \n5 Bihar Aurangabad \n6 Chhattisgarh Naya Raipur \n7 Delhi Sarai Kale Khan \n8 Haryana Bhiwani \n9 Haryana Rohtak \n10 Himachal Pradesh Mandi \n11 Himachal Pradesh Sarkaghat \n12 Jammu & Kashmir Jammu \n13 Jharkhand Jamshedpur \n14 Karnatka Bellary \n15 Kerala Eddappa \n16 Madhya Pradesh Chhindawara \n17 Madhya Pradesh Indore \n18 Maharashtra Pune \n19 Maharashtra Lature \n20 Maharashtra Nagpur \n21 Manipur Imphal \n22 Nagaland Dimapur \n23 Odisha Jeypore \n24 Rajasthan Rajasmand \n25 Sikkim Pakyong \n26 Telangana Karimnagar \n27 Tripura Agartala \n28 Uttar Pradesh Kanpur \n29 Uttar Pradesh Rai Baraily \n30 Uttarakhand Dehradun \n31 West Bengal Kolkata \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |756", "t": "National Highways in Odisha", "head": "  \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO. 756 \nANSWERED ON 21st JULY, 2022  \n  \nNATIONAL HIGHWAYS IN ODISHA \n  \n756. MS CHANDRANI MURMU: \n  \n       Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the length of National Highways (NHs) taken up for construction in \nthe State of Odisha out of the 25,000 km as proposed in the Union \nBudget;  \n  \n(b) the length of new NHs that would pass through the rural areas of the \nState particularly Keonjhar district; and  \n  \n(c) the time frame set for completion of these new NHs?  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n  \n(Shri NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) Construction/ Development of 2168 km length of National Highways \n(NHs) is in progress in the State of Odisha and 256.24 km length of NHs \nhave been sanctioned for construction/development during 2021 -22. \nAlso, construction/development of 897 km of NHs have been proposed \nfor sanction during 2022-23 in the State of Odisha. \n\n(b) Most of the NHs in the State pass through rural areas. Presently, NH-\n20, NH-49, NH-220, NH-520 and NH -720 having total length of 381 km \npass through the district of Keonjhar. \n(c) Development and upgradation of National Highways is a continuous \nprocess. The status of development of NHs passing through the district \nof Keonjhar is given below: \nNH No. Status \nNH-20 JH/OD Border to Rimuli - Strengthening work in progress.  \nRimuli – Panikoili section – 4-lane completed in  142km \nNH-220 2-laning with paved shoulder work completed except Patna \nbypass and Karanjia bypass. Bridge over river Simuli and \nBaitarani on Patna bypass have been sanctioned by Ministry \nand is under bidding stage. Widening to 2 -lane & \nstrengthening of Patna bypass and Karanjia bypass have \nbeen included in Annual Plan 2022-23 of Odisha. \nNH-520 4-lane completed in 43 km \nNH-49 4-lane completed in 93 km \nNH-720 Presently 77 km is of 2-lane standard, DPR is in progress for \n4-laning \n  \n**** \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|9|UNSTARRED |757", "t": "Biomass Energy System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 757 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nBIOMASS ENERGY SYSTEM \n+757. SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has prepared any guidelines for the development of biomass energy \nsystem in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the quantum of financial assistance extended to every State including Maharashtra for the said purpose \nduring the last three years and the current year?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND  CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n(a)&(b) The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy had been supporting biomass energy projects \nup to 31.03.2021 through the following schemes: \n \nI. Scheme to Support Promotion of Biomass Based Cogeneration in Sugar Mills \nand Other industries in the Country. \nII. New National Biogas and Organic Manure Programme (NNBOMP); \nIII. Biogas based Power Generation (Off-Grid) and Thermal Energy Applications \nProgramme (BPGTP); and \nIV. Programme on Energy from Urban, Industrial, Agricultural Wastes/ Residues \nand Municipal Solid Waste. \n \nThe Central Financial Assistance provided by the Ministry for the major components of the \nabove bioenergy schemes, was as following: \n \nI. Rs. 25 Lakhs/ Megawatt for Bagasse Cogeneration Projects on surplus exportable capacity \nand Rs. 50 Lakhs/ Megawatt for Non-Bagasse Cogeneration Projects on installed capacity; \nII. Rs. 7500/- to Rs. 35,000/- per plant based on size of the plant in cubic meter;  \nIII. Rs. 25,000/- to Rs. 40,000/- per kilowatt for power generation and Rs. 12,500 /- to Rs. \n20,000/- per kilowatt equivalent for thermal applications;  \nIV. a)  Rs 1.0 crore per 12000 m3 per day for biogas generation and upto Rs 3 Cr/Megawatt for \npower generation based on biogas; and  \n            b) Rs 4.0 Crore per 4800 Kg/day of Bio CNG (Maximum Rs 10Cr/project). \n                 \nThe above-mentioned schemes which were valid till 31.03.2021, were initially continued for meeting \nonly the committed liabilities till 2025 -26. Now the Expen diture Finance Committee has \nrecommended to implement the Umbrella National Bioenergy Program for new sanctions also. The \nUmbrella National Bioenergy Program provides support to biomass energy projects through above \nschemes except bagasse based cogeneratio n projects  and municipal solid waste based power \ngeneration projects. \n \n(c) The details of quantum of financial assistance extended to every State including Maharashtra for the said \npurpose during the last three years and the current year are given in Annexure. \n***** \n\n \n \nAnnexure \nDetails of Financial Assistance provided to States (including Maharashtra) during the \nlast three years and the current year to Biomass energy Projects (except Municipal \nSolid Waste to Power projects):  \nS No State/UT Financial Assistance (Rs. in Crores) \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n2022-23 \n(upto \n30.6.2022) \nTotal \n1 Andhra Pradesh 3.74 5.50 52.32 0.42 61.98 \n2 Assam 0.00 2.90 0.32 0.00 3.22 \n3 Chhattisgarh 2.49 0.00 1.98 0.00 4.47 \n4 Gujarat 2.40 6.45 15.11 0.00 23.97 \n5 Haryana  0.00 0.06 6.00 0.00 6.06 \n6 Karnataka  3.67 6.64 3.96 0.00 14.27 \n7 Kerala  1.46 0.83 0.77 0.00 3.06 \n8 Madhya Pradesh 4.14 4.81 2.91 0.00 11.